      const char *const Page5Pieces[] =
      {
         "What the backup carries, and what it does not\n=============================================\n\nThe built-in backup writes one 7-Zip archive per run: an XML index, and \xe2""\x80""\x94"" when\n**Back up messages** is on \xe2""\x80""\x94"" the whole message store beside it. A restore is\ndestructive and has no transaction: it deletes every domain, every account and\nevery public folder before it puts the archive's back. So the only question that\nmatters about an archive is what is *in* it, because everything an account has\nthat is not in it is deleted by the restore and not replaced.\n\nThis document answers that question, item by item, and is checked rather than\nremembered: `build/check-account-stores.py` reads the `CREATE TABLE` scripts\nagainst the server's own declaration and fails the build when a table that holds\none account's data is in neither list. It runs on every pull request.\n\nBefore 15 September 2026 seven of the tables below were in neither, and\nrestoring an archive destroyed them. If you are running 6.3.3 or earlier, read\nthe last section.\n\nMigrations and PST import are on the same page of the Control Panel, Backup,\nrestore & migration, as its Migrations card: how many connections the running\nmigrations may hold to one source host, and the folder Outlook data files are\ncopied into to be imported. Bringing mail in from another server or a PST file\nis not a backup or a restore, and has a document of its own,\n[Migration.md](Migration.md): the jobs in\n[Five hundred mailboxes: migration jobs](Migration.md#five-hundred-mailboxes-migration-jobs),\nand the import in [Outlook data files (PST)](Migration.md#outlook-data-files-pst).\n\n\nIn the archive\n--------------\n\n### The server\n\n| What | Notes |\n|---|---|\n| Domains, and their aliases, distribution lists and signatures | With each domain its allow and block list, its spam traps, its quarantine digest switch and hour, and its own values of the settings with levels (`hm_settingoverrides`, scope 1; see [SettingLevels.md](SettingLevels.md)) |\n| Accounts | Address, password hash, forwarding, vacation, signature, quota, admin level, TOTP secret, anti-spam thresholds. The TOTP secret is carried opened and sealed again under the restoring server's stored-secrets key, so that a restore onto a server with another key, or onto new hardware, keeps every second factor; one that did not open where the backup was taken is carried as its envelope, about a hundred characters, which fits an older server's column too ([SecretsAtRest.md](SecretsAtRest.md)) |\n| A domain's S/MIME authority | With its domain: each root's certificate, its private key **as the envelope it is stored in** (none for a retired root whose certificates have all expired), its serial counter, and every certificate it issued, carried by the address of the account it was issued to. The envelope is the platform's secret store's, never the legacy scheme's: on Windows it opens on the machine that protected it, to any process there; on Linux wherever the stored-secret key file goes. An archive restored where it does not open brings back an authority that must be rotated there before it signs; one restored where it does is a working authority, so guard the archive as you guard the server. See [SmimeAuthority.md](SmimeAuthority.md) |\n| Aliases and distribution lists, with their members | |\n| Routes, IP ranges, TCP/IP ports, SSL certificate *records*, incoming relays, groups | Only with **Back up settings** on |\n| IP warm-up schedules | Only with **Back up settings** on: the address, the start as a date, the plan and the switch. A restore changes a schedule the server already has for the address to the archive's, keeping its counts; removes one the archive does not name; and adds the rest with none. See [IpWarmup.md](IpWarmup.md) |\n| Anti-spam and anti-virus lists | DNS black lists, SURBL servers, white list, blocked senders, the server's allow and block list, blocked attachments, greylisting white list |\n| Public folders, and the permissions on them | |\n| The server settin",
         "gs that were the `[Settings]` section of `hMailServer.ini` | The rows of `hm_inisettings`, which is the settings store since schema 6042; the section in the file is the server's copy of them, and is not what the archive reads. Carried as name and value with **Back up settings** on, so a row this build does not know still comes back. A secret among them - `SRSSecret`, `OAuth2HmacSecret` and the rest - is carried opened and sealed again on the way in ([SecretsAtRest.md](SecretsAtRest.md)). **Not `PasswordPepper`** - see *Not in the archive* |\n| Custom administrator roles | The rows of `hm_roles` that are not built in, with their permissions and descriptions, whenever **Back up domains** or **Back up settings** is on. Restored first, before any domain is removed, so that the assignments the accounts carry find their role on a server that has never seen it. A role the restoring server already has is kept as it is there, never overwritten with the archive's, and the difference is named in the backup log. See [Roles.md](Roles.md) |\n\n### Everything an account has\n\n| What | Table | Notes |\n|---|---|---|\n| Folders and messages | `hm_imapfolders`, `hm_messages` | Only with **Back up messages** on. A message keeps its UID, its flags **and its keywords** \xe2""\x80""\x94"" the webmail's labels, the second and third stars, follow-up flags, mute and pin |\n| The parts of messages held once | The files under `<data directory>\\Blobs` | With the messages, because **Back up messages** copies the whole data directory. `hm_storeblobs`, which counts the names that need each part, is not carried: a restore rebuilds it from the stored files when it ends, and a mailbox restore brings in the parts its messages need that the server lacks. See [MessageStore.md](MessageStore.md) |\n| Rules | `hm_rules`, and its criteria and actions | |\n| Fetch accounts, and the UIDs already downloaded | `hm_fetchaccounts`, `hm_fetchaccounts_uids` | |\n| Application passwords | `hm_apppasswords` | The hashes, so a restore does not silently revoke every client the account holder set up |\n| The address book | `hm_contacts` | Including the vCard a CardDAV client stored, under the resource name it chose |\n| The webmail's remembered choices | `hm_accountprefs` | Theme, density, reading pane, notifications, language, labels and their colours, saved searches, swipe actions \xe2""\x80""\x94"" the server attaches no meaning to a key and carries each verbatim |\n| Scheduled sends and snoozes | `hm_scheduled` | See *References*, below |\n| Files sent as expiring links | `hm_files` | The record: name, owner, size, expiry, download count and the password hash. The bytes are not messages: they are files under `<data directory>\\Files\\<token>`, and an archive holds them only because **Back up messages** copies the data directory. An archive taken without it, or with `BackupMessagesDBOnly`, has the records and not the files; restored onto a data directory that does not hold them, each link answers *No such file* |\n| The mailbox key of an account encrypted at rest | `hm_accountkeys` | Its public half, its private half wrapped by the account's password and sealed to the domain's recovery key, and the copy under this machine's key while it waits for a first sign-in - which on Windows opens only on the machine that wrote it, and on Linux wherever the data directory's key file goes (the archive does not carry that file). Every encrypted message the archive brings back is encrypted to this key and opens with nothing else. The domain's recovery key (`hm_domainrecovery`) travels with the domain, with the key a replacement that was cut short keeps beside it. A mailbox restore encrypts a message archived before the switch-on to the key on the way in. See [EncryptionAtRest.md](EncryptionAtRest.md) |\n| S/MIME certificates and keys | `hm_smimekeys` | Including the wrapped private key. This server cannot open it, so if a restore lost it nothing would fail \xe2""\x80""\x94"" the account would simply never read its own encrypted mail again |\n| The password re",
         "use history | `hm_passwordhistory` | So a restore does not let every account go back to a password it was made to change |\n| Who has written to the account before | `hm_knownsenders` | The memory the first-contact note is decided against, as of the backup. Without it a restore would announce every correspondent of every account as a first contact again \xe2""\x80""\x94"" see [DomainTransforms.md](DomainTransforms.md) |\n| Calendars and their events and tasks | `hm_calendars`, `hm_calendarobjects` | Tombstones included, so a CalDAV client's next sync is answered correctly rather than being told nothing was ever deleted |\n| Safe senders and blocked senders | `hm_senderlist`, the account's own entries | With their reasons and expiries. The table also holds each domain's list and the server's, which travel with the domain and with the settings; see [SenderListsAndDigests.md](SenderListsAndDigests.md) |\n| Its own values of the settings with levels | `hm_settingoverrides`, the account's own rows | Written back under the restored account's id through the same checks a write makes, less the limit's ceiling, which caps the value when it is read; a row this build refuses is left out and named as `HM7312`. See [SettingLevels.md](SettingLevels.md) |\n| Whether the quarantine digest was stopped | `hm_digeststate` | A person who asked for the digest to stop is still opted out after a restore. The digest's position is carried too, and an entry held after the last digest went is still new if a restore onto a fresh database starts the quarantine's numbering again |\n| The administrator role it was given, and the domains it reaches | `hm_roleassignments` | The role by its name, found again on the restoring server. Put back first thing after the account's row, before its folders, and the account's admin level - which the archive also carries, and which alone would give back authority the role had narrowed away - is withheld until the assignment is written and given back only behind it, so a restore that stops part-way leaves no account wider than its role. An assignment naming a role that neither the archive nor that server has is dropped, the level stays withheld, and the backup log and the error log (`HM6129`) name the account and the missing role |\n\nThe per-account stores are carried as rows under their account, in the archive's\nown XML. A value is written as an attribute where every character of it is safe\nin one, and as base64 of its UTF-8 under the same name with `.base64` appended\nwhere it is not \xe2""\x80""\x94"" so most of the index stays legible, and a vCard, an iCalendar\nobject or a PEM block cannot make the archive unreadable.\n\n\nNot in the archive, deliberately\n--------------------------------\n\n| What | Why, and what to do |\n|---|---|\n| `[Database]` and `[Directories]` in `hMailServer.ini` | Per-machine. They are how this installation finds its database and its data folder, and they would be wrong on the machine you restore onto. Keep a copy of the file |\n| The administrator password and its second-factor secret | In `hMailServer.ini`, for the same reason: they are what lets you in when there is no database |\n| The stored-secrets key (`[Security] StoredSecretsKey`), and the database's record of it | The key the second factors and the `[Settings]` secrets are sealed under in the database. The archive does not need it - it carries those secrets opened - and a restore seals them under the restoring server's own key; the database's record of which key it is (`StoredSecretsKeyId`, and the last export's) is not carried either, since it names this installation's key. **A database-level backup does need it** - a dump, a SQL Server backup, a copy of `hMailServer.sdf`: restored without the key it brings back every second factor and `[Settings]` secret unopenable, and on Windows a copy of `hMailServer.ini` is no copy of the key. Export it as soon as it is made - `hMailServer.exe --export-stored-secrets-key FILE`, elevated, or `sudo hmailserver --export-stored-secrets-key FILE` - ",
         "and keep the file with the database backups, stored apart from them as the pepper is; the setup checklist asks until it has been. Lost with no copy anywhere, `--forget-sealed-secrets KEYID` clears what it sealed ([SecretsAtRest.md](SecretsAtRest.md)) |\n| `PasswordPepper` | A `[Settings]` key the settings store never holds and the archive never carries; the live update's trust, below, is kept the same way. Its whole purpose is to be somewhere the password hashes are not, and the archive holds the hashes: every Argon2id and scrypt hash was made with it, and without it each verifies false. It lives in `hMailServer.ini` only - the server takes it back out of the database if an earlier start put it there, and says so in the application log. **A restore onto new hardware needs the same pepper in that machine's `hMailServer.ini` before an account whose password is an Argon2id or scrypt hash can sign in**; PBKDF2, SHA-256 and MD5 hashes, and the administrator password, do not use it. Keep a copy of the value with the backups, apart from the archive |\n| TLS certificate and DKIM key **files** | The database stores the *paths*, not the files. A restore onto a machine where those paths hold nothing gives you a server that starts, serves no TLS and signs nothing. Keep them inside the data directory (where the message-store backup carries them) or copy them yourself |\n| Mail still in the delivery queue, and its recipients | A queued message is in flight rather than in a mailbox. Let the queue drain before a migration |\n| Deleted items kept for recovery, and what a legal hold kept | The rows of `hm_recoverable` are not carried, and **Back up messages** leaves `<data directory>\\Recoverable` out of its copy of the data directory; a restore of the data directory leaves that folder where it is, so the rows the server has and the files they name stay together. See [RecoverDeleted.md](RecoverDeleted.md) |\n| eDiscovery cases, searches, holds and exports | The four `hm_discovery*` tables are not carried, and **Back up messages** leaves `<data directory>\\Discovery` - each search's results and each export's zip - out of its copy; a restore of the data directory leaves it where it is. A server rebuilt from an archive has no case and no hold, so place the holds again before its mail can be deleted. A restore with **Back up domains** deletes every domain first, so it is refused, before anything is changed, while an account or a domain is under a legal hold. See [Discovery.md](Discovery.md) |\n| Messages that expire, and password-protected messages | The rows of `hm_confidential` and `hm_protectedmessages` are not carried, and **Back up messages** leaves `<data directory>\\Confidential` - each kept copy of a message that expires, whole and in the clear - and `<data directory>\\Protected` - the ciphertext behind each password-protected message's link - out of its copy of the data directory; a restore of the data directory leaves both folders where they are. A copy made to be deleted at a time is not carried into an archive that outlives it. Archives taken by 6.3.4 builds before this change **do** hold those folders: a message that has expired or been withdrawn since is still in them, so apply the retention you would apply to the mail itself, or delete those archives. A restore from one of them leaves the folders out as it leaves them out of a backup. See [ConfidentialMessages.md](ConfidentialMessages.md) and [OpenPGP.md](OpenPGP.md) |\n| The live update's folder, `<data directory>\\Updates` | **Back up messages** leaves it out of its copy of the data directory from 6.3.6, and a restore leaves this machine's own where it is: the installer or package waiting to be applied and its Sigstore bundle, the rollback image, the helper's copy, its logs and request, the single-use apply token and the outcome of the last apply. None of it is mail and none of it is wanted after a restore, here or on another machine: an installer is downloaded and verified again, a token is g",
         "ood for one apply within the hour on the machine that issued it, and the outcome file is read at start-up, so a restored copy would have a server report an apply it never made. Up to 6.3.5 every archive carried it, so the backup taken before an unattended apply held the package that apply was about to install - 15 MB beside 353 bytes of mail in the first Linux live update. An older archive's copy is not put back. See [UpdateFeed.md](UpdateFeed.md) |\n| The live update's trust: `UpdateSigningIdentity`, `UpdateSigningIssuer`, `UpdateSourceRepository`, `UpdateTrustRootsFile` and `UpdateLogPublicKeyFile` | From 6.3.6 read from `hMailServer.ini` alone, never from the database, so that the process an update is checked for cannot choose what it is checked against. The archive does not carry them, and a restore does not put back the copy an archive from 6.3.4 or 6.3.5 carries - the application log names it. A site that sets them copies them to a new machine with the file. See [UpdateFeed.md](UpdateFeed.md), \"Where the trust is read from\" |\n| The mail of a domain named as one of those five folders | A domain's mail is kept in the directory named after it, so a domain named `recoverable`, `discovery`, `confidential`, `protected` or `updates` has its mail in the folder the backup leaves out, and a restore does not put it back. Such names are refused for a new domain since 6.3.4, and one that already has one is reported in the error log (HM7597) and on the first-hour checklist: rename it before relying on the backup. See [SetupChecklist.md](SetupChecklist.md) |\n| A managed mailing list's archive | The rows of `hm_listarchive` are not carried: they are what the list sent, not its configuration. The archived copies themselves are files under `<data directory>\\Lists\\<list id>\\`, and an archive taken with **Back up messages** holds them because that copies the data directory - a members-only list's messages included, as plain text. Restored, the files are back on disk under the list's old id with no row naming them: no page shows them and nothing removes them, so delete that folder by hand. The list's settings and members come back, and its archive starts again from its next posting. See [MailingLists.md](MailingLists.md) |\n| The full-text index | Derived from the messages, and rebuilt by the indexer. Carrying it would roughly double the archive to save work a background task does anyway |\n| Per-message and per-mailbox annotations (RFC 5257, RFC 5464) | Keyed on ids a restore reassigns. Recorded as a gap in `Roadmap2.md` rather than silently dropped |\n| The expunge log a QRESYNC client is answered from | Restoring it would tell a client that messages the restore has just put back are gone |\n| The sandbox's bearer token (`SandboxToken`) | A credential for a service outside this server that no interface reads back; an archive written in the clear would be the one place it could be read. A restore leaves the token the server holds as it is; on a new server, enter it again. See [Sandbox.md](Sandbox.md) |\n| What is held for a sandbox's verdict, and the remembered verdicts | `hm_sandboxholds` and `hm_sandboxverdicts`: a hold is a message in flight, like the delivery queue's, and a verdict expires within `SandboxCacheHours`; the cache fills again from the sandbox |\n| What the last reputation check found (`hm_reputationchecks`) | An operational record about this machine's own addresses, rewritten by the next check, which runs within a day of the restore. What it checks - the three `ReputationWatch` settings - is in `hm_settings`, which the archive does carry. |\n| Quarantine, message trace, the send API's record of sends (`hm_apisends`), metric history, the archive index | Server-wide operational records rather than an account's data. Back the database up if you need them. The send record is keyed on queue ids, which a restore does not keep, so a restored server's API keys list no sends ([SendApi.md](SendApi.md)) |\n| The journal of mailbox moves (`hm_m",
         "ailboxmoves`) | A record of what this installation did to its own store, not part of a mailbox. A restore is not a moment in a move: the account comes back at the address the archive holds, so a carried row would describe a move this store has no trace of. The audit trail is the record a person reads - see [MailboxMove.md](MailboxMove.md) |\n| The IP warm-up's daily counts (`hm_warmupcounts`) | Traffic: how many recipients each warming address sent to each provider on each day. The schedules themselves are carried; a schedule restored onto another server, or moved with the database to another backend, starts its day at zero there, so its first day there may send up to that day's ceiling again. See [IpWarmup.md](IpWarmup.md) |\n| The links in quarantine digests already sent (`hm_digesttokens`) | Each is good once and expires within `QuarantineDigestLinkHours`, and names a quarantine entry the archive does not hold. The next digest issues new ones |\n\n\nA destination inside the data directory\n---------------------------------------\n\n**Back up messages** copies the data directory into a staging folder and puts the\ncopy in the archive, and a restore copies the archive's store back and removes\nwhat the archive does not hold (*How a restore replaces the data directory*,\nbelow). From 6.3.6 both leave three directories alone wherever they are inside\nthe data directory, at any depth:\n\n- **the backup destination**, with the archives in it;\n- **the temp directory** (`TempFolder`), where an encrypted backup stages its\n  copy and every restore, and every backup's verified restore, extract the\n  store - the Linux packages' `hMailServer.ini` puts it at\n  `/var/lib/hmailserver/temp`, inside the data directory;\n- **the run's own staging folder**, and for a restore the folder of the archive\n  it is restoring from and the store extracted from it.\n\nA directory is recognised by what it is - its identity on the file system, the\ndevice and inode on Linux, the volume, the file system's 128-bit file identifier\nand the creation time on Windows - not by how its path is written: a destination\nwritten in another case, with other separators, through a symbolic link, a\njunction, a `subst` drive, the administrative share or with a short name is the\nsame directory, and `/var/lib/hmailserver2` is never taken for\n`/var/lib/hmailserver`. A directory the file system gives no identifier of its\nown - an identifier of 0 or all ones, as a network server without file\nidentifiers answers - is not taken for any of them, so a destination on such a\nshare inside the data directory fails as it did up to 6.3.5 rather than a\nmailbox being taken for it and left out. The backup\nlog names each one a copy left out (\"Left out of the copy of the data directory\n...\") and each one a restore left in place (\"Left in place by the restore of\nthe data directory ...\"); a directory on the way down to one of them has what\nthe archive does not hold removed around it, and is never removed itself. An\narchive in the data directory's own root - a destination that is the data\ndirectory itself - is\nneither copied into the staging folder nor deleted by a restore. A copy of one\nof them that an earlier run left in the staging folder - the nested folders a\nfailed backup of 6.3.5 or earlier left behind - is removed rather than archived,\nand a restore does not write an older archive's copy of one back into it. The\nfolder of the archive being restored is the exception to that last: it is left\nin place, with the archive and whatever is beside it, and what the archive holds\nfor that folder is copied into it, since it may be a mailbox's folder that\nsomebody put the archive in.\n\nSo a destination inside the data directory works, and it is accepted as it\nalways was. What it means is that **the backup lives on the disk it backs up**:\na disk that fails, fills or is lost takes the mail and every archive of it\ntogether, and the archives use the space the mail needs. On Linux it is the easy\nchoice - `/var/lib/hmailserve",
         "r` and `/var/log/hmailserver` are the only places\nthe packaged unit lets the service write without a drop-in - and the better one\nis still a directory elsewhere, added to the unit ([PackagesDeb.md](PackagesDeb.md),\n\"The live update\"), or a copy of the archives taken off the machine.\n\nUp to 6.3.5 such a destination failed every backup: the copy walked into its own\nstaging folder, copying the copy into itself until the path was too long\n(`HM5014`, \"Could not backup data directory\"), and left the nested folders\nbehind. With `UpdateBackupBeforeApply=1`, the default, that also refused every\nunattended update - which is how the first Linux live update found it, on 29\nSeptember 2026. Reading the code for the fix found two more of the same kind on\nthe Linux packages, whose temp directory is inside the data directory; neither\nwas reproduced, and 6.3.6 removes both: an encrypted backup with messages staged\nin the temp directory and copied itself into itself the same way, every time;\nand a restore of messages, emptying the data directory, deleted the temp\ndirectory and with it the store it had extracted there a moment before, then\nfailed with the data directory already emptied and the domains already removed -\nthe archive itself untouched.\n\n\nHow a restore replaces the data directory\n----------------------------------------\n\nA restore with **Restore messages** makes the data directory what the archive's\nmessage store holds. Since 6.3.6 it does that in three steps, and the first is\nthe only one that can fail:\n\n1. **The archive's store is copied in, over what is there.** A file the data\n   directory already holds byte for byte is left as it is. One that differs is\n   renamed beside itself (`<name>.hmailserver-previous-<8 hex>`) and the archive's\n   copied in its place. One that cannot be renamed - the account the service runs\n   as may not delete it - is left as it is and named. **This installation's own\n   self-signed certificate and key** (`Certificates\\self-signed-key.pem` and\n   `self-signed-certificate.pem`, which a new installation makes for itself -\n   [SecureDefaults.md](SecureDefaults.md)) are left as they are whenever the\n   archive's key is not this one's, byte for byte: the archive's pair is another\n   installation's, a key and a certificate are kept or replaced together so that\n   the listeners using them still have a pair, and under\n   `NT SERVICE\\hMailServer` the key is one the service could not replace\n   anyway. The archive's pair is restored where this\n   installation has none, and its certificate where the key is the same. No file\n   at the root of the archive's store is copied: a backup leaves the root's files\n   out - they are the delivery queue's, which a restore does not put back - so\n   one there is in no archive the server made, and could only take the place of\n   one of the server's own.\n2. **A copy that fails is undone**: what it wrote is removed - with the part of\n   a file a copy stopped by a full disk left - what it renamed is put back, the\n   folders it made are removed. The data directory is then what it was before\n   the restore's copy, and the restore stops with `HM5014`. Until the copy is\n   complete the data directory holds the archive's files beside the ones they\n   replace, so it needs room for both - less the domains' folders, which a\n   restore of domains deletes before the copy - and a disk that fills is a copy\n   that fails, undone. The staged copy in the temp folder is removed with it -\n   the archive holds the messages - unless something could not be put back;\n   then the message names what, and the staged copy is kept, as it always was\n   after a copy that failed.\n3. **Only once the copy is complete** is what the archive does not hold removed:\n   every file below the folders the restore replaces that the archive's store has\n   no file at, and every folder left empty that it has no folder at; then what was\n   renamed in step 1. At the data directory's root only the delivery queue's\n   message file",
         "s (`*.eml`, and what a writer of the store left of one when it\n   stopped part-way) are removed - never the server's own files there, which\n   no archive carries: on Linux `.hmailserver-secret-key`, the key every `LINUX1:`\n   secret on the machine is sealed under ([SecretsAtRest.md](SecretsAtRest.md)),\n   the container's `.hmailserver-environment`, the sending limits' state. A file\n   that cannot be removed is left and named.\n\nWhat is left alone is what was left alone before: the folders a backup does not\ncarry (*What the archive does not carry*, above), and wherever they are inside the\ndata directory the destination, the temp folder, the folder of the archive being\nrestored and the store extracted from it.\n\n**What the backup log says.** \"The data directory now holds the backup's message\nstore: N file(s) copied from it, ...\"; a line for files gone from the store\nextracted in the temp folder before they could be copied - something removed\nthem meanwhile, an anti-virus scanner most likely; they are not restored, and a\nfile of the server's of that name is left in place; a line for this installation's\nown pair when it was kept, with where the archive's is; and \"Left as it is in the\ndata directory: ...\" for each file the restore could not replace or remove, with\nthe reason. Those last are also one line in the error log, **HM5705**, which says\nthe restore is otherwise complete. To have the archive's copy of such a file,\nstop the service, copy it from the archive as an administrator, and start the\nservice.\n\n**Up to 6.3.5 the restore emptied the data directory first**, and two things went\nwrong with that order:\n\n* **On Windows, every full restore with messages failed on an installation made\n  new with 6.3.4 or 6.3.5**, whose service runs as `NT SERVICE\\hMailServer` - the\n  account a new installation's service has run as since 6.3.4\n  ([ServiceAccount.md](ServiceAccount.md)) - and which made its own self-signed key.\n  That key, which `--apply-new-install-defaults` writes, gives the service's account\n  Read and nothing more, and its Modify on the data folder does not include deleting a\n  child of a folder. So the emptying stopped at `Data\\Certificates` (`HM5700`,\n  \"Could not delete the directory ... Access is denied\"), the copy then failed on\n  the archive's copy of the same key after 30 s of retries (`HM5014`, \"...\n  self-signed-key.pem could not be copied: Access is denied\") before it reached\n  the domains, and the restore ended with **no domain on the server and the\n  backup's messages only in `Temp\\{GUID}\\DataBackup`**. A restore of one mailbox\n  was not affected. A key or certificate file an administrator *moved* into the\n  data folder - a moved file keeps the permissions it had where it was - did the same\n  on any installation whose service's account those permissions did not let delete\n  it.\n* **On Linux, from 6.3.0, every full restore with messages deleted\n  `.hmailserver-secret-key`** with the other files at the data directory's root.\n  Nothing in an archive puts it back, so no value sealed under it that the archive\n  does not carry opened again - the `LINUX1:` lines of `hMailServer.ini`, and from\n  6.3.4 `[Security] StoredSecretsKey` among them, so that from the restore on no\n  account's second factor and no `[Settings]` secret opened (`HM7495` at the\n  reinitialise and at each start).\n\n**If a restore failed that way.** The archive is as it was. Upgrade to 6.3.6 or a\nlater release and restore again from the same archive: the restore puts back the\ndomains, the accounts and the messages, and leaves this installation's key where\nit is. The staged copy the failed restore left in the temp folder\n(`Temp\\{GUID}\\DataBackup`, named in its `HM5014` line) holds the same messages as\nthe archive; delete it once the restore has completed. On 6.3.4 or 6.3.5, without\nupgrading: give the service the rights to remove the key first - Delete, and Write\nattributes and Write extended attributes, which the removal asks for as well\n(Delete alone sti",
         "ll fails with `HM5700`) -\n`icacls \"<data folder>\\Certificates\\self-signed-key.pem\" /grant \"NT SERVICE\\hMailServer:(D,WA,WEA)\"` -\nand restore again. That restore puts the archive's copy of the key in its place\nwith the data folder's permissions, which up to 6.3.5 let every user of the\ncomputer read it, so once it has completed make the key private again; the\nservice keeps Read:\n`icacls \"<data folder>\\Certificates\\self-signed-key.pem\" /inheritance:r /grant:r *S-1-5-18:(F) *S-1-5-32-544:(F) \"NT SERVICE\\hMailServer:(R)\"`.\nUpgrading is simpler. On Linux, put the key file back from a copy of the\ndata directory if there is one; otherwise import the stored-secrets key from its\nexport - remove the `[Security] StoredSecretsKey` line, run\n`sudo hmailserver --import-stored-secrets-key FILE` and restart - or, with no\nexport, `--forget-sealed-secrets KEYID` as [SecretsAtRest.md](SecretsAtRest.md)\nsays, and enter again the other `LINUX1:` values `hMailServer.ini` held.\n\n\nReferences: how a scheduled send survives a restore\n---------------------------------------------------\n\nA restore inserts every row afresh and the database hands out new identities, so\nnothing in the archive can be an id. That is what makes the archive portable\nbetween database backends at all \xe2""\x80""\x94"" see\n[MigratingDatabaseBackend.md](MigratingDatabaseBackend.md).\n\nOne row type names another: a scheduled send names the draft to send and the\nfolder to return a snoozed message to. Writing those numbers into the archive\nwould, after a restore, point a scheduled send at whatever message happened to be\ngiven that id \xe2""\x80""\x94"" so they are carried as what survives a restore instead: the\nfolder's path, segment by segment, and the message's UID. On the way back in the\npath and the UID are resolved against the folders and messages this restore has\njust put back.\n\nA reference that cannot be resolved \xe2""\x80""\x94"" the draft was deleted, or the archive was\nrestored without its messages \xe2""\x80""\x94"" drops that one row, counts it, and says so in the\nbackup log by account. Nothing else in the account is affected. Restoring a row\nthat points at nothing is the one outcome worse than losing it.\n\n\nWhat the verification proves\n----------------------------\n\nEvery run reads its own archive back before it is called a backup, and before\nretention is allowed to consider deleting an older one:\n\n1. **The index opens**, through the same code a restore uses to open one, and\n   describes what this run put in it.\n2. **The per-account rows are all there.** What the run built is counted against\n   what the archive reads back, per table. A difference means the archive does not\n   contain what was backed up, so it is discarded and the backup fails.\n3. **The message store extracts** to exactly the files and bytes that were staged\n   for compression. Fewer of either is a truncated archive, whatever 7-Zip's exit\n   code said.\n4. **Every message row has a file.** This one is *reported*, never failed: the\n   rows were read before the copy started and mail flow does not stop for a\n   backup, so a message deleted in between is normal. A large number means the\n   backup was taken during a bulk deletion and is worth taking again.\n\nStep 3 costs disk: for the length of the check the message store exists twice, in\nthe temp directory. A temp volume that cannot hold it skips the step with an\nexplanation rather than failing a good backup. `BackupVerifyRestore=0` turns\nsteps 3 and 4 off and says so in the log.\n\n\nAn archive that is encrypted\n----------------------------\n\nOff until a passphrase is set, and then every archive is encrypted as it is\nwritten: **7-Zip AES-256, with the archive's headers encrypted too** (`-mhe=on`),\nso that an archive without its passphrase cannot even be *listed*. Whoever finds\nthe file learns neither the mailboxes in it nor that it is an hMailServer backup.\n\nSet it in the Control Panel (**Backup, restore & migration \xe2""\x86""\x92"" Encryption**), in the Control\nDeck's Backup view, over REST (`encryption_passphrase` in\n`PUT /api/v1/settings/backup`",
         "), or with `hmctl backup passphrase set`. Clearing\nit \xe2""\x80""\x94"" an empty passphrase \xe2""\x80""\x94"" goes back to unencrypted archives.\n\n### What the passphrase is, and where it is not\n\n| | |\n|---|---|\n| Kept | In the database, as the `backupencryptionpassphrase` row of `hm_settings`, protected by the machine's secret store: a DPAPI envelope on Windows while `ProtectStoredSecretsWithDPAPI` is 1, the key file on Linux \xe2""\x80""\x94"" the way the route and relay passwords are kept |\n| Never | On a command line (see below), in a log, in a COM or REST read, or **in the archive itself** |\n| Written to the archive | Nothing. The settings section the archive carries skips this one setting, as the stored `[Settings]` skip `PasswordPepper`. A key kept inside the lock it opens is not a key \xe2""\x80""\x94"" and it also means a restore never overwrites the passphrase this server is using with one out of an archive |\n| Read back | Never. There is no getter over COM and none over REST. What can be asked is whether one is **set**: `Settings.Backup.EncryptionPassphraseSet`, `encryption_passphrase_set` |\n| If it is lost | **Everything in every archive written with it is lost.** Not recoverable by this server, by its author, or by anybody. Keep it somewhere other than beside the backups \xe2""\x80""\x94"" a restore asks for it, and the day it is needed is the day the server it was stored on is gone |\n\nA passphrase is 12 to 256 **printable ASCII** characters and may not begin or end\nwith a space. The ASCII rule is not fussiness: the passphrase reaches the\narchiver through its standard input and is decoded by whatever code page or\nlocale that archiver runs under, which is not the same on the Windows machine\nthat wrote the archive and the Linux machine somebody restores it on. Within\nASCII there is no such difference, so the archive opens with the same passphrase\nin any 7-Zip anywhere.\n\n### Not on the command line\n\nEverything after a program's name is in the process list, which every user of the\nmachine can read. So the passphrase is never an argument: 7-Zip is given `-p`\nwith **no value**, which is its \"ask for the password\", and the server writes the\npassphrase into the process's standard input before the process starts\n(`ProcessLauncher::SetStandardInput`, `Compression::LaunchCommand_`). The same\nchange also means an archive that asks for a passphrase nobody gave now reads the\nend of its input and fails, where before it would have waited for ever at a\nconsole prompt nobody could see.\n\nThe archiver's own **output** is left where it was: on Linux it is the server's,\nso 7-Zip's account of a failure \xe2""\x80""\x94"" an unsupported switch, a full disk, a refused\npath \xe2""\x80""\x94"" still reaches the journal, which on that platform is the only explanation\nanybody gets beyond an exit code; on Windows it goes to the null device, because\na service has no console for a child to write to either way.\n\n`hmctl` holds the same line: `hmctl backup passphrase set` and\n`hmctl backup restore` take `--passphrase-file FILE` (or `-` for standard input)\nor `--ask`, and never a passphrase as an argument.\n\n### The restore\n\nEvery way in asks for it, and every one of them refuses **before anything on the\nserver is touched** \xe2""\x80""\x94"" the archive is opened, its version checked and what was\nasked for checked against what the archive holds, and only then is the\ndestructive part begun:\n\n| Where | How |\n|---|---|\n| Control Panel | The **Restore** card's passphrase box |\n| COM | `BackupManager.LoadEncryptedBackup(file, passphrase)`, beside the old `LoadBackup(file)` |\n| REST | `POST /api/v1/backup/restore` with `file`, `passphrase` and the three parts \xe2""\x80""\x94"" **the only restore a Linux server has**, since COM is Windows-only |\n| Control Deck | The Backup view's Restore card |\n| hmctl | `hmctl backup restore FILE --domains --messages --passphrase-file FILE` |\n\nThe two failures are told apart, because the answers are different: *this archive\nis encrypted and you gave no passphrase* (find the passphrase) and *it could not\nbe opened with the passphrase given: either it is not this archive'",
         "s passphrase,\nor the archive is damaged* (find another archive). Which one it is comes from the\narchive's own header \xe2""\x80""\x94"" `Compression::GetArchiveEncryption` reads the 7z signature\nheader and the coder ids of the block it points at \xe2""\x80""\x94"" so it costs nothing and\nneeds no passphrase. `Utilities.BackupArchiveIsEncrypted` answers the same\nquestion over COM.\n\nThe question that header answers is narrow, and worth stating exactly: **whether\nthe archive's headers are encrypted**, which is how this server writes every\nencrypted archive (`-mhe=on`) and is what hides the file names. 7-Zip can also\nencrypt an archive's *contents* while leaving its names readable. This server\nnever writes one, but an archive made elsewhere may be one, and it fails where a\ntruncated archive fails rather than where an encrypted one does \xe2""\x80""\x94"" so when an\narchive with readable names will not open and no passphrase was given, the\nrefusal names both possibilities instead of calling it corrupt. The header is\ndeliberately not searched for encryption of that kind: the AES method id sits\namong the coders of an unencrypted header too, and hunting for those four bytes\nin a header whose file names are arbitrary text would eventually call a good\narchive encrypted and send somebody looking for a passphrase that never existed.\n\n**An archive taken without a passphrase still restores without one**, and offering\none for it is not an error either: whoever restores does not have to know which\nkind of archive they are holding.\n\n**A restore is recorded** \xe2""\x80""\x94"" the request, not what it replaces. One audit row is\nwritten the moment one is asked for, by whoever asked, over COM or over REST:\nobject type `backup`, the archive's path, action `restore started`, and which\nparts are being put back and whether the archive is an encrypted one. What the\nrestore then does is not recorded, because it runs on the maintenance work queue\nwhere no actor exists to attribute it to; `docs/AlertsAndAuditTrail.md` says\nwhich half is which and why.\n\nOne difference between the ways in, and it is about threads rather than archives.\n`POST /api/v1/backup/restore` does its checking on one of four HTTP worker\nthreads, so the archiver run that reads the index is bounded at **two minutes**;\npast that the route answers **504**, starts nothing and says that the archive was\nnot judged. Without the bound, an archive whose path leads to a drive that has\nstopped answering would hold that thread for as long as the drive stayed that\nway, and four such requests are the whole API. Nothing else has the bound or\nneeds it \xe2""\x80""\x94"" the Control Panel over COM and the backup's own verification each run\non a thread whose only job is that one backup \xe2""\x80""\x94"" so an archive too slow for the\nroute still restores from the Control Panel, or after being copied to a local\ndisk.\n\n### What it changes about a backup run\n\n* The verification extracts the archive with the passphrase, so what is verified\n  is that the archive opens **with the passphrase this server holds** \xe2""\x80""\x94"" not with\n  one it used a moment ago.\n* After the archive is written it is read back and its header checked: an archive\n  that was meant to be encrypted and is not is discarded, not reported as a\n  successful backup.\n* A stored passphrase this machine cannot open \xe2""\x80""\x94"" a database carried from another\n  installation, a key file that has gone \xe2""\x80""\x94"" **fails the backup**. An unencrypted\n  copy of every mailbox is never written in place of an encrypted one.\n* **Compression must be on** when messages are backed up. Without it the message\n  files are copied into a `DataBackup` folder *beside* the archive, where nothing\n  encrypts them, so that combination is refused before anything is written.\n* **The staging moves off the destination.** Both halves of a backup are written\n  to a file before they are archived \xe2""\x80""\x94"" the index (`hMailServerBackup.xml`, which\n  carries every account and the server's stored secrets) and a whole copy of the\n  message files in a `DataBackup` folder, because 7-Zip is handed a directory.\n  An ",
         "unencrypted backup stages both in the destination; an encrypted one stages\n  them in a directory of its own under `TempDirectory` and removes it on every\n  way out, including the failures. Otherwise a plaintext copy of every mailbox\n  would sit in the destination \xe2""\x80""\x94"" the share, the cloud mount whose sync client\n  uploads a file as it appears \xe2""\x80""\x94"" for as long as the compression took, which is\n  the exposure the encryption exists to prevent. The archive's contents are\n  unchanged by this: 7-Zip names members from the last component of the path it\n  is given, so they are `DataBackup\\\xe2""\x80""\xa6""` either way and a restore cannot tell.\n* **So an encrypted backup needs room in the temp directory**, as much as the\n  message store plus a little. It is checked before the copy starts and the run\n  is refused with both numbers if it is not there. Point `TempDirectory` at a\n  disk with room, or take the backup unencrypted deliberately. (The verified\n  restore already needed the same room, and skipped itself without it; this\n  refuses, because staging in the destination instead would be writing the mail\n  where it was not to be written.)\n* Not every archiver can encrypt: `7zr`, the reduced p7zip build, cannot, and\n  fails the run. Install 7-Zip (`7zz`) or p7zip-full. The failure says so.\n* Changing the passphrase does not touch the archives already written. Each\n  archive needs the passphrase it was written with, which is a reason to write\n  the passphrase and its date down together.\n\nOne thing encryption does **not** hide: the archive's own file name\n(`HMBackup <date> <time>.7z`), its size, and the fact that a backup ran. That is\nwhat a folder listing shows, and no archive format hides it.\n\nAnd one place the passphrase still exists in the clear, said plainly because\n\"never in any read\" is otherwise easy to over-read: **the server's own memory\nwhile it is using it**, and so a process dump taken at that moment. The copies\nare overwritten as soon as they are finished with \xe2""\x80""\x94"" the bytes handed to 7-Zip go\nthe moment they are in the pipe, and the passphrase a backup or a restore was\ngiven goes with the object that held it \xe2""\x80""\x94"" but the settings store keeps the\nvalue for the life of the process, as it does the smart host's password, and a\nminidump written while a backup is running can contain it. Whoever can take a\ndump of a service running as `LocalSystem` can read that server's secrets by\neasier routes as well; this is a statement of what the feature does not claim,\nnot a way in.\n\nAnd one thing it protects that is easy to miss: the archive carries the server's\nown stored secrets in the clear \xe2""\x80""\x94"" the smart host's password and the `[Settings]`\nsecrets in its settings section, every account's second factor with the account \xe2""\x80""\x94""\nbecause a restore has to be able to put them back, and on another machine too. An\nunencrypted archive hands those to whoever can read the destination folder.\n\n\nWhat a restore will not extract\n-------------------------------\n\nA restore extracts whatever archive it is pointed at, as the account the service\nruns as: the service runs 7-Zip itself, under its own account. On Windows that is\n`NT SERVICE\\hMailServer`, the service's virtual account, for an installation made\nfrom 6.3.4 on, and `LocalSystem` where setup was told to use it, where an upgrade\nkept it, or where the move to the virtual account could not be made\n([ServiceAccount.md](ServiceAccount.md)); on Linux it is the packages'\n`hmailserver` user. Under the virtual account it reads only an archive the\naccount may read. So since 25 September 2026 every way a restore opens\nan archive \xe2""\x80""\x94"" loading it, a whole restore, the check of the archive a backup has\njust written, and a mailbox or a message restored from one \xe2""\x80""\x94"" first lists its\nentries (`7za l -slt`, which reads only the archive's headers, with the\npassphrase when the headers are encrypted) and refuses the whole archive,\nbefore anything in it is extracted, when an entry:\n\n- has an absolute path: a leading `\\` or `/`, a drive (`C:`), a UNC path;\n- ",
         "has a `..` segment, or a segment of nothing but dots and spaces that Windows\n  would read as one;\n- is a link with somewhere to point: a tar's symbolic or hard link, or an entry\n  whose attributes mark it a symbolic link, a junction or another reparse point\n  and which carries the link's target as its data. A directory that 7-Zip 19.00\n  archived by following a junction keeps the mark with no data, and is not\n  refused.\n\nA listing that an archive has written into \xe2""\x80""\x94"" a name or a comment printed with\nits newlines, as p7zip 16.02 prints them \xe2""\x80""\x94"" is refused as well, and the check is\nmade again just before each later extraction from the archive, since each\nopens it again by its path.\n\nThe server never writes such an entry into a backup, so an archive that holds\none was made or changed somewhere else. The refusal names the entry and goes to\nthe backup log like any other, and it is also reported as an error, **HM7540**,\nbecause unlike an archive that cannot be read it is not an ordinary mistake.\nNothing is extracted and nothing on the server is changed.\n\nThis is defence in depth. The 7-Zip the Windows installer ships moved from 19.00\nto **26.03** on the same day \xe2""\x80""\x94"" 19.00 predated the fixes for CVE-2023-31102 and\nCVE-2025-55188 \xe2""\x80""\x94"" and 26.03 itself drops a `..` and a drive from what it\nextracts and checks where a link points. The check does not depend on that, nor\non which 7-Zip or p7zip a Linux server has.\n\n\nRestoring one mailbox, or one message\n-------------------------------------\n\nThe whole restore above replaces the server. Since 22 September 2026 an archive\nin the backup destination can also give back **one account's mailbox** \xe2""\x80""\x94"" every\nfolder and message it held when the backup was taken \xe2""\x80""\x94"" or **one message** of\nit, and touch nothing else: not the account's settings, rules, contacts or\ncalendars (those are the account, and the whole restore is what brings them\nback), and not any other account. It is the same operation in every interface,\n`MailboxRestore`, and it runs the way the whole restore runs: checked on the\ncaller's thread and refused with the reason before anything is done, then\nqueued on the maintenance work queue under the backup manager's one run slot,\nso that a mailbox restore, a whole restore and a backup never overlap, and\nreported to the backup log.\n\n| Where | How |\n|---|---|\n| REST | `GET /api/v1/backup/archives` (the archives), `GET /api/v1/backup/{archive}/mailboxes/{address}` (the account as the archive holds it), `POST .../restore` with `mode`, or `message` and `folder` (never both), and `GET /api/v1/backup/restore-mailbox/{job}` |\n| Control Deck | The Backup view's **Restore a mailbox** and **Restore one message** cards |\n| COM | `BackupManager.RestoreMailbox(file, address, mode, passphrase)`, `RestoreMessage(file, address, id, folder, passphrase)`, `DescribeMailbox(file, address, passphrase)` and `MailboxRestoreStatus(job)` |\n| Control Panel | The Backup page's **Restore a mailbox** and **Restore one message** cards |\n| hmctl | `hmctl backup archives`, `hmctl backup mailbox ARCHIVE ADDRESS`, `hmctl backup restore-mailbox ARCHIVE ADDRESS --merge\\|--replace [--message ID [--folder F]]`, `hmctl backup restore-status JOB` |\n\n**Which archives.** Only one inside the backup destination, and over REST,\nthe Deck and `hmctl` only by the **name** the listing answers\n(`HMBackup YYYY-MM-DD HHMMSS.7z`) \xe2""\x80""\x94"" a path cannot be given there at all, since\nthe route's path segment is not decoded for separators, and one that is tried\nanswers 404 as a name the destination lacks. Over COM the archive may also be\nnamed by its path inside the destination; a path anywhere else is refused\nthere with the reason, whatever it points at: the whole restore takes any path\nfrom an administrator who has decided to replace the server, but this is\nreached from a mailbox picker, and a picker must not be a way to read files\nthe server was never told about. The archive must hold domains and messages\n(`BackupDomains` and `BackupMessages` on when it was taken),",
         " its version is\nchecked as the whole restore checks it, and an encrypted one needs its\npassphrase \xe2""\x80""\x94"" in the request body, or in the `X-hMailServer-Backup-Passphrase`\nheader on the listing, which has no body. One archive at a time: there is no\npicker across several archives for the version of a mailbox at a chosen moment;\nthe listing says when each archive was taken, and that is the choice.\n\n**What the index may say.** The one thing read out of an archive that chooses a\npath is a row's file name, and the index is a file in a directory more hands\nwrite to than the server has administrators. So a file name is taken only in\nthe shape the executer writes \xe2""\x80""\x94"" `{` + 36 hexadecimal characters and hyphens +\n`}.eml`, no separator, no colon, no leading dot \xe2""\x80""\x94"" and a row with any other\nname is never listed, never staged and never written under the account's\ndirectory: it is counted (`unrestorable` in the listing, `failed` in a whole\nmailbox's outcome) and named in the backup log, the first ten.\n\n**Merge and replace.** *Merge* creates each archived folder that is missing and\nadds each archived message whose file is not in the live mailbox; nothing there\nis removed. *Replace* empties every live folder of the account first and then\nfills as merge would, so that the mailbox is the archive's \xe2""\x80""\x94"" mail that arrived\nsince the backup is gone. The folders themselves stay, with their subscriptions\nand rights; what replace removes is kept **only for `RecoverDeletedDays`, which\nis off by default** (0 keeps nothing), and both confirm sentences say so.\nReplace is refused up front, with 400, when the archive holds no messages for\nthe account \xe2""\x80""\x94"" it would only empty the mailbox \xe2""\x80""\x94"" and a replace whose fill could\nnot put every archived message back ends `failed`, with the counts in `error`\nand the same report a failed backup makes, because the mailbox then holds less\nthan either the archive or the live mailbox did. A single message is merge of\none row, into the folder it was in or a named one, which is created if it is\nmissing; `mode` given together with `message` is refused with 400, since a mode\ndoes not apply to one message.\n\n**The identity rule.** The executer names every message file once, with a GUID,\nand the same name in the live mailbox \xe2""\x80""\x94"" in whatever folder \xe2""\x80""\x94"" is the same\nmessage. That is what \"not in the live mailbox\" means: a message the person has\nmoved since the backup is not brought back to its old folder, a message they\ndeleted is, and merging the same archive twice adds nothing the second time. A\nrestored message comes back with its flags, keywords and dates as the archive\nholds them, a fresh UID in its folder (the archive's UIDs are not reused), its\nsize charged to the account as a delivery's is, and a session with the folder\nselected is told the way delivery tells it. The full-text index picks the new\nrows up on its own pass, nudged by the restore so that a search finds them in\nseconds.\n\n**What is refused, and what is counted.** The account must exist on this server\n(404 if not \xe2""\x80""\x94"" a mailbox is restored into the account it belongs to, and a\ndeleted account is the whole restore's job or an administrator's to make first;\nrestoring into a different account is not offered), the archive must hold it\n(404), the message named must be in it (404), and a second restore while a\nbackup or restore runs is 409 \xe2""\x80""\x94"" the job the request registered is then marked\n`failed` with that reason, so that no status ever reads `queued` for something\nthat will not run. A message whose file the archive does not hold \xe2""\x80""\x94"" deleted\nfrom the store while the backup ran \xe2""\x80""\x94"" is counted as *failed* and named in the\nbackup log (the first ten), and a merge goes on: one missing file is not a\nreason to leave the other thousand out. The outcome \xe2""\x80""\x94"" `folders`, `added`,\n`skipped` (the same file name was still there) and `failed` \xe2""\x80""\x94"" is read from the\njob's status route, from `MailboxRestoreStatus` over COM, and from the backup\nlog; the last fifty jobs are kept for the life of the process. A job that\ncannot ",
         "complete at all, and a replace with anything in `failed`, fails the way\na whole restore fails: a `BACKUP ERROR` line, a critical error, the\nbackup-failed alert and the `OnBackupFailed` event.\n\n**Who.** The roles that may restore the server may restore a mailbox from one\nof its archives (`manage_server`); reading an account's messages out of an\narchive \xe2""\x80""\x94"" senders, dates and subjects \xe2""\x80""\x94"" is reading the mailbox as it was\n(`view_mailbox`), which an auditor may do and a help desk may not. All four\nroutes are server-wide and refused to a credential confined to named domains.\nThe request is recorded on the audit trail as the whole restore's is \xe2""\x80""\x94"" object\ntype `backup`, the archive's path, action `mailbox restore started`, and a\nsentence naming the account and the mode \xe2""\x80""\x94"" by whoever asked, over COM or REST;\nwhat the restore then does runs with no actor on its thread and is not.\n\n**What the listing costs.** The archive index carries a message's file name,\nsender, date, size and flags, and not its subject. The listing reads subjects\nfrom the message files, staging them out of the archive to do it \xe2""\x80""\x94"" the listed\nmessages' files, named to the archiver one by one, and never the account's\ndirectory or the store \xe2""\x80""\x94"" for a listing of at most 500 messages; a larger\nmailbox is listed without subjects, the answer says so, and `?folder=` narrows\nboth the listing and what is staged to one folder. A restore stages the same\nway: the files of the messages it will put back, and nothing beside them. On\nthe REST route the archiver runs under the same two-minute bound as the whole\nrestore's check.\n\n**Not in this.** An archive taken with `BackupMessagesDBOnly` holds rows and no\nfiles, and a mailbox cannot be restored from rows alone; the job says so and\nfails. A public folder is not an account's mailbox and is not restored here.\nNothing here recreates an account, a domain, or an account's settings, rules,\ncontacts or calendars: that is the whole restore.\n\nVersions\n--------\n\nAn archive from an **older** hMailServer restores. What that version did not\nwrite is simply absent, and the restore does not complain about it: an archive\nfrom before this change carries no per-account stores and no keywords, and\nrestores exactly as it always did.\n\nAn archive from a **newer** hMailServer is refused, with a sentence naming both\nversions and telling you to upgrade first. There is deliberately no override: a\nnewer archive can carry properties this build has no column for, and those would\nbe dropped in silence while the restore reported success.\n\nAn archive that names a table this build does not have \xe2""\x80""\x94"" which can only be a\nhand-edited one, or one from a build whose version string cannot be parsed \xe2""\x80""\x94"" has\nthose rows counted and named in the backup log, and the rest of the restore goes\nahead. Refusing a whole restore over rows nothing here could use would cost you\nyour mail to protect something you cannot have.\n\n\nAdding a per-account table\n--------------------------\n\nFor anyone working on the server. Everything that holds one account's data is\ndeclared in\n`hmailserver/source/Server/Common/Application/AccountStores.cpp`, in one of two\narrays:\n\n* `stores_` and `columns_` \xe2""\x80""\x94"" the table, and every one of its columns with the\n  role it plays. The backup reads it through that declaration and the restore\n  writes it back through the same one.\n* `elsewhere_` \xe2""\x80""\x94"" the table, and the reason it is not carried there: a business\n  object already writes it, or it is derived, or it is in flight.\n\n`build/check-account-stores.py` fails the build when a table that reaches\n`hm_accounts` through `ON DELETE CASCADE`, or that has an account-id column, is\nin neither \xe2""\x80""\x94"" and when a column of a declared store is missing, when the three\n`CREATE TABLE` scripts disagree about one, or when a store has no test in\n`hmailserver/test/RegressionTests/Infrastructure/BackupAccountStores.cs`.\n\nThe test shape is the same for every store: make the data through the surface\nthat really writes it, take a backup, delete the whole domai",
         "n the way a disaster\nwould, restore, and find the data exactly as it was.\n\n\nIf you are running 6.3.3 or earlier\n-----------------------------------\n\nThe seven per-account tables above \xe2""\x80""\x94"" the address book, the webmail's settings,\nscheduled sends, file links, S/MIME keys, calendars and the password history \xe2""\x80""\x94""\nand a message's keywords were in no archive those releases wrote, and a restore\ndeletes them. Until you are on a release that carries them:\n\n1. **Back up the database as well**, with your database server's own tool:\n   `pg_dump`, `mysqldump`, SQL Server's backup, or a copy of `hMailServer.sdf`\n   with the service stopped - and, from 6103 on, keep the exported stored-secrets\n   key beside those backups, or the second factors and `[Settings]` secrets they\n   hold do not open where they are restored ([SecretsAtRest.md](SecretsAtRest.md)).\n2. **Restore from that** when what you are recovering is a whole server, and use\n   the built-in restore for what it was good at: moving domains, accounts and the\n   message store between installations.\n3. If you restore from an archive those releases wrote, expect users to find\n   their address book, labels and webmail settings gone, and tell them before\n   they find out themselves. An archive written by an older release does not gain\n   what it never held by being restored onto a newer server.\n",
         nullptr
      };

