      const char *const Page8Pieces[] =
      {
         "# The calendar and the tasks\n\nWhat the webmail's Calendar and Tasks pages do, what the account's calendars are, how they are shared, and how an invitation travels by mail. The server half - CalDAV, the iCalendar module, recurrence - is in the Roadmap2 rows for them; this is the page and the routes it uses.\n\n## Calendars\n\nAn account has one calendar until it makes more: `Calendar`, made on first use, at `/dav/calendars/<address>/calendar/`. The Calendars box beside the grid adds a second one with a name and a colour (`POST /api/v1/me/calendars`), renames or recolours it (`PUT /api/v1/me/calendars/{id}`), and deletes it with its events (`DELETE`); the default cannot be deleted. Over CalDAV the same calendar is `/dav/calendars/<address>/<name>/`, with `displayname` and Apple's `calendar-color` on it, so a phone shows the same colour; `MKCALENDAR` there makes one the page then lists, and `PROPPATCH` sets the name and colour. Since 17 September 2026 (schema 6064) a calendar has a colour and can be shared.\n\n**Sharing.** The owner names another account of this server and whether it may write (`PUT /api/v1/me/calendars/{id}/shares/{address}` with `write`). The other account sees the calendar in its own Calendars box, named by its owner, and in its CalDAV home as `shared-{id}/` with `owner` pointing at the sharer's principal and a `current-user-privilege-set` of read, or read and write. A read-only share takes no PUT, no event from the page and no PROPPATCH; the sharer takes the share back, or the other account leaves it (`DELETE /api/v1/me/calendars/{id}` on a calendar that is not its own). When an account is deleted, the shares granted to it go; its own calendars and their shares go with it through the foreign keys. `GET /api/v1/me/calendars/{id}` is one calendar with its shares, each `{\"address\", \"write\", \"delegate\"}` or, since 22 September 2026, `{\"group\", \"members\", \"write\", \"delegate\"}`.\n\n**Shared with a group.** Since 22 September 2026 (schema 6086) the name in `PUT /api/v1/me/calendars/{id}/shares/{name}` may be one of the server's groups (`hm_groups`, the groups the Control Panel and `/api/v1/groups` keep) instead of an account's address; a name that is both is the account. Every member of the group then sees the calendar exactly as a named sharee does - the Calendars box, `shared-{id}/` in the CalDAV home, `owner`, the privileges the share grants - and the membership is read at the moment the member asks, so somebody added to the group later sees it and somebody removed no longer does. The share is the group's, not any member's: a member cannot leave it (403 on the page's leave and on a CalDAV DELETE of `shared-{id}/`; they leave the group, or the owner takes the share back by the group's name). An account reached by a group share and by a share of its own has the widest rights of the two; leaving a calendar reached both by name and through a group gives the own share back and answers 403 with `{\"left\": false, \"group\": \"<name>\"}` - over CalDAV, 403 with the same sentence - because the calendar stays. An owner who is a member of a group their own calendar is shared with sees it once, as their own. Shared with a group as a delegate (below), every member of the group may answer the owner's invitations, and each answer carries that member's `SENT-BY`. Anybody who may share a calendar learns from the PUT's answer that a group exists and how many members it has; the DELETE by name answers one 404 whether the name is a group or not, so the group list cannot be read from it. There is no foreign key from a share row to its group, because a share with an account carries group id 0, which is not a group: the group's own removal deletes its share rows, and the owner's listing drops a row whose group is gone as it meets it. In the page's Calendars box a share with a group is listed by the group's name with how many members it has.\n\n**A delegate.** A share may also be a delegation (`delegate: true` on the PUT, which implies `write`): the s",
         "haree may answer the owner's invitations on the owner's behalf, as an assistant does in Exchange. For that to be possible the owner's invitations have to be in the calendar, so since 22 September 2026 a `METHOD:REQUEST` delivered to an account is filed into the account's default calendar as it arrived - the account's `PARTSTAT` what the organiser wrote, `NEEDS-ACTION` as a rule, so the owner's page shows it as not answered and the delegate's shared view shows it too - when, and only when, it names the account as an `ATTENDEE` (by its address, an alias domain's spelling of it, or an account alias that points at it) and does not name it as the organiser; a message flagged as spam files nothing at all, whatever it carries, and a `REQUEST` that names somebody else - a forwarded invitation, one to a list the account is in, or calendar spam - is not the account's to keep. A copy already in the calendar under the same UID is replaced when the invitation is the same or a later `SEQUENCE` and never when it is older, and never when the stored copy's `ORGANIZER` is not the invitation's: a UID is mailed to every guest, so anybody who saw an invitation could otherwise rewrite the event under it. For the same reason a delivered invitation replaces a stored copy only when the server verified that its organiser sent it (**Who may change a stored event**, below); a new one is filed from anybody. At the same `SEQUENCE` the answer the account already gave is carried into the replacement, since a resend (an organiser that never received the reply, or an attendee-list or description change, for which RFC 5546 does not step the sequence) is not a new question; a higher `SEQUENCE` is, and arrives as it arrived. An account that has never opened a calendar (no calendar row yet) gets nothing filed, since a delivery is not a use. Answering from the message afterwards updates that one event rather than adding a second: the message route and the delivery filer are one path (`Imip::KeepInvitation`). The answer itself is `POST /api/v1/me/calendar/events/{id}/reply` with `partstat` `ACCEPTED`, `TENTATIVE` or `DECLINED` and an optional `comment`, allowed to the calendar's owner and to a delegate - not to a plain writer or a reader (403) - and only for an event that names the owner among its `ATTENDEE`s and somebody else as `ORGANIZER` (400 otherwise: an event a phone copied in, or a forwarded invitation, has no organiser expecting the owner's reply, and the page offers no buttons for it). It sets the OWNER's `PARTSTAT` on the event and mails the organiser the `METHOD:REPLY` the message route sends, with the `ATTENDEE` being the owner's address; when a delegate answered, that `ATTENDEE` carries `SENT-BY=\"mailto:<delegate>\"` (RFC 5545 section 3.2.18), kept in the stored object so the owner's page and every syncing client show who answered, and the mail is `From:` the owner with `Sender:` the delegate, sent from the delegate's own address, which is what a mail client shows as \"on behalf of\". The delegate's own calendar is not touched; the owner answering in person clears the mark. On the page, an invitation opened in a calendar shared as a delegate shows Accept, Maybe and Decline \"on behalf of\" the owner; the owner's own editor shows the same three buttons for an invitation in their calendar, and the answer beside each guest says who gave it.\n\n## Free-busy\n\nSince 22 September 2026 an account of the domain can see when a colleague is busy, and nothing else about them. `GET /api/v1/me/freebusy?address=<account>&from=<when>&to=<when>` answers, for an active account of the caller's own domain (an alias domain's spelling resolved), the busy periods of that account's own calendars - not the ones shared with it - over a window of at most 62 days (400 beyond), the bounds written as `YYYY-MM-DD`, `YYYY-MM-DDTHH:MM[:SS]` with `Z` or an offset, or the iCalendar `YYYYMMDDTHHMMSSZ`. Every event instance is expanded through the same bounded recurrence module the calendar-quer",
         "y uses; an event that is `TRANSP:TRANSPARENT` or `STATUS:CANCELLED`, or that the account declined, is left out; a period is `tentative` when the account's `PARTSTAT` on the event is `TENTATIVE` or `NEEDS-ACTION`, else `busy`. The answer is `{\"address\", \"from\", \"to\", \"busy\": [{\"start\", \"end\", \"kind\"}]}`, merged and sorted, with no title, location or description. Another hosted domain's account, an address nobody has, an inactive account, and an account whose `freebusy` preference is `nobody` are one and the same 404 - the same body, not the same time: the preference is one indexed select and a shown account's periods an expansion, so a caller measuring closely could tell the three apart, and what the route promises is the answer, not the timing - so the route enumerates nothing. The preference is the account's own, in Settings (\"Who may see when you are busy\": people in your domain, or nobody), kept in the preferences store like the rest of the page's choices.\n\nOver CalDAV the same computation answers the `free-busy-query` REPORT (RFC 4791 section 7.10) on a calendar collection the caller may read - its own, or one shared with it - as one bare `VFREEBUSY` - `UID`, `DTSTAMP`, `DTSTART`, `DTEND` and `FREEBUSY` lines of `FBTYPE=BUSY` and `BUSY-TENTATIVE`, no `ORGANIZER` and no `ATTENDEE`, which section 7.10 leaves out and which belong to RFC 6638's scheduling object - over the `time-range` asked for, which needs both bounds and spans at most 62 days; the collection's `supported-report-set` names it. This is not RFC 6638 scheduling: there is no `schedule-outbox`, no `schedule-inbox`, and no free-busy of a calendar on another server.\n\nIn the page's event editor, once guests are named and a start is set, an availability strip shows the event's day: one row per person - the organiser first - with the busy periods the route answers drawn as blocks, tentative ones lighter, and the event's own span outlined; the strip is redrawn when the time or the day changes, from what was already answered when the day is the same. A guest whose address is not of this server's domain is marked \"not on this server\"; one the route will not answer for - not an account of the domain, or having chosen nobody - is marked \"not shown\".\n\n## Events\n\n`GET /api/v1/me/calendar/events?start=&end=` answers the window occurrence by occurrence, across every calendar the account can see: each recurring event expanded by the server's recurrence module with its overrides and exclusions applied, each occurrence carrying its `recurrence_id`, the wall clock in the event's own zone (`local_start`, `local_end`), the calendar's colour, the caller's answer when they are an attendee (`my_partstat`), the reminder (`alarm_minutes`) and the rule (`rrule`). The page keeps its own zone - the one chosen in Settings, else the browser's - and says so under the calendars; it converts every instant into it for the month grid of six weeks, the week and day views of twenty-four hourly slots, and the agenda of the next month.\n\nCreating an event (`POST /api/v1/me/calendar/events`) takes the title, `start` and `end` as wall-clock times in `tz` (an IANA zone the server knows), `all_day`, the location and notes, `rrule` (daily, weekly on chosen days, monthly, yearly, with an interval and an end by count or by date), `alarm_minutes`, and the guests. The server writes `DTSTART;TZID=<zone>` so a weekly 09:00 stays 09:00 across a clock change, a `VALARM` for the reminder, and an `ATTENDEE` line per guest with `ORGANIZER` set to the account. Changing one (`PUT .../{id}`) takes the same fields, each optional; with `instance` set to an occurrence's `recurrence_id` the change is an override of that occurrence alone, which is what dragging one meeting to another day does, and what the editor's \"only this occurrence\" does; without it the series changes. `DELETE .../{id}?instance=` excludes one occurrence (an `EXDATE` on the master, its override dropped); without it the event goes. Drag a chip in the month",
         " view to another day and it keeps its time; drag it onto an hour in the week or day view and it takes that hour; drag the bottom edge of a block to change the end in quarter hours.\n\n**Reminders.** A `VALARM` before the start is what CalDAV clients ring for. While the page is open it looks a day and a half ahead every ten minutes and, a minute at a time, shows a toast when an alarm falls due - and a browser notification when the reader has allowed them in Settings - once per occurrence, remembered in the browser.\n\n## Tasks\n\nThe Tasks page lists every `VTODO` of every calendar the account can see, soonest due first (`GET /api/v1/me/tasks`): title, notes, due day or time, priority, the list it is in, and whether it is done. Adding one (`POST`) takes the title, a due day and the list; ticking it off (`PUT` with `completed: true`) writes `STATUS:COMPLETED`, `COMPLETED` and `PERCENT-COMPLETE:100` as a client does, and unticking clears them. What Apple Reminders or Tasks.org put in over CalDAV is in the list, and what the page adds is on the phone. A second calendar is a task list of its own.\n\n## Invitations by mail (iMIP)\n\nAn event with guests, saved with \"Send invitations by e-mail\", mails each guest a message with the event as a `text/calendar` part with `method=REQUEST` beside its plain text (RFC 6047) - what Outlook, Microsoft 365, Gmail and Apple Mail turn into an invitation card of their own. Changing the event and saving it again mails an update with the sequence stepped; deleting it mails `METHOD:CANCEL`.\n\nA message that carries a `text/calendar` part, or an `.ics` attachment, is marked `invitation: true` in its JSON, and the page asks `GET /api/v1/me/messages/{id}/invitation` for what it says: the method, the event's title, when and where, the organiser, the guests with their answers, whether the event is already in one of the reader's calendars, and whether that copy holds what the message says (`calendar_agrees`, below). The card above the message text offers Accept, Maybe and Decline, and \"Propose a new time\". An answer (`POST .../invitation/reply` with `action`) keeps the event in the reader's default calendar with their `PARTSTAT` set - a newer invitation replacing the stored copy, an older one not undoing a newer - and mails the organiser a `METHOD:REPLY` with that one attendee's answer. A proposal mails `METHOD:COUNTER` with the proposed time. A cancellation is read the same way, and \"Remove from calendar\" takes the event out - or, for a cancellation of occurrences, those occurrences alone, the rest of the series kept (below).\n\n**Filed on delivery.** When a `REPLY` is delivered to an account that organises the event it answers, the attendee's `PARTSTAT` is set in the stored event, so the organiser's calendar - the page, and every phone syncing it - shows who accepted without anyone opening the mail; a `CANCEL` delivered to a guest marks the stored copy cancelled. A `CANCEL` of occurrences of a recurring event - every event in it carries a `RECURRENCE-ID` (RFC 5546 section 3.2.5) - takes those occurrences out of the guest's copy and leaves the rest of the series as it was, as a guest's own \"remove this occurrence\" does: each one's override is dropped and an `EXDATE` written on the series, in the form of its `DTSTART` (a day for an all-day series, floating for a floating one, else UTC, which is the same instant in the series' own zone); one named with `RANGE=THISANDFUTURE` ends the series there, its later overrides and `RDATE`s dropped and its `RRULE` given an `UNTIL` the moment before it in place of any `COUNT` (an `RRULE` that ended before it already is left alone). When the occurrences named are everything the copy holds - a guest invited to one occurrence, or a cancellation from the first occurrence on - the copy is marked cancelled, as a `CANCEL` of the whole event marks it. A `RECURRENCE-ID` is matched by the instant it names, read in the `CANCEL`'s own zones. Until 6.3.6 a `CANCEL` of one occurrence marked the g",
         "uest's whole series cancelled, and the card's remove took the whole series out. A room or a piece of equipment takes occurrences out of a booking as it did ([RoomsAndEquipment.md](RoomsAndEquipment.md)). The look costs a message without a calendar part a read of its header. A filing that stops at an unexpected error leaves the message in the mailbox as it was delivered and, since 6.3.6, says so in the error log (`HM7555`, once an hour at most).\n\n**Who may change a stored event.** Since 6.3.6 what a delivered message says changes an event already in a calendar only when the server verified who sent the message - from what it recorded when the message arrived, and never from what the calendar part says, since a UID is mailed to every guest and a message says whatever its writer put in it:\n\n* a `CANCEL` only from the organiser the stored event names;\n* an updated `REQUEST` replaces the stored copy only from that organiser (a new invitation is filed from anybody, as it arrived and not answered, as the message itself is delivered). A copy filed from an invitation whose organiser the server did not verify carries the server's mark, `X-HMAILSERVER-IMIP-UNVERIFIED:TRUE` on its `VEVENT` or `VTODO`, and does not hold off the organiser's own invitation by the `SEQUENCE` its writer gave it: the organiser's, verified, replaces it whatever the two sequences, so that a copy nobody verified never keeps the organiser's own invitation, or its updates, out of the calendar. An answer from the message's card marks the copy it writes when the calendar had none, or had a marked one, or the message is a later `SEQUENCE` than the copy, since the server did not verify who sent that version; whatever a message carries of the mark is taken out first. A copy that names another organiser than the invitation is never replaced on delivery - not even by an invitation the server verified, since a sender verified as the organiser it names is not the owner of the UID: anybody who saw an invitation can write its UID into one of their own - but since 6.3.6 the reader's own answer from a message's card replaces such a copy when it is one the server marked as filed unverified, and writes the answered event marked in its place: nobody verified the organiser the marked copy names, and the reader chose this invitation over it. So a copy planted under a UID before the organiser's own invitation came, naming somebody else, keeps the organiser's invitation out of the calendar only until the reader answers it, where until 6.3.6 the answer was refused (422). A copy the server did not mark - the organiser's own, verified, or one the reader or a phone put in - still refuses an answer under its UID that names another organiser, and a cancellation never replaces a copy;\n* a `REPLY` sets only the `PARTSTAT` of an attendee the event already lists who sent it, or whose delegate did - a sharee of one of that attendee's calendars with `delegate: true`, named by `SENT-BY`, which is kept beside the answer. An answer from another address of the same account as the one the event lists - the invitation went to an alias, and the answer came from the account's own address, as the webmail's does - is set on the address the event lists, when the sender may send as that address. A `REPLY` adds nobody to the event, and nobody to an occurrence the organiser left them out of - with one exception: the event lists a distribution list of this server's, and the answer is from one of its members, who sent it themselves or through their delegate, by the list's own record of its members (a confirmed member, or an account its membership query answers - the address as it is spelt, or the account's own address; not through a list within the list, nor a member row that is an alias). The member is then added beside the list, with `MEMBER` naming it (RFC 5545 section 3.2.11), on the components that list it: the organiser invited them through it. A list on another server cannot be asked who is in it, and its ",
         "members' answers are not filed.\n\nThe sender is verified as a room verifies an organiser ([RoomsAndEquipment.md](RoomsAndEquipment.md), \"Who is asking\"): an address of this server's own domains when a signed-in session - SMTP `AUTH`, the webmail's own sends, a room or a piece of equipment answering by itself - submitted the message as that account or as an address the account may send as (the send-as rules authenticated SMTP obeys, [SendAs.md](SendAs.md)); an address from outside when the message passed DMARC for exactly that address's domain and its `From:` is that address - one `From:` field naming one mailbox, written as one: a bare address, or a display name and one `<address>` that ends the field, with no second `<address>`, no text after the `>`, no address in a comment and none in the display name but the address itself (as Outlook writes a name it does not know). A field that another mail program could read as another address names nobody, since what DMARC vouches for is the address the sender's own mail system read in it. SPF on its own vouches for nobody's `From:`. With DMARC off nothing from outside is verified, and neither is mail that nobody signed in to send from an address range spam protection skips, a message an application sent through the send API - even with a key that may send as the address, which records the account for the domain rules and is still no session of the account's ([SendApi.md](SendApi.md)) - or mail fetched from an external account: none of it changes a stored event. Anything else is delivered as an ordinary message: it is in the mailbox, its card offers what it always did, and the application log says that the stored event was left as it was. The card says the calendar changed only when it did: `GET /api/v1/me/messages/{id}/invitation` answers `calendar_agrees`, true when the stored event holds what the message says - a `CANCEL`'s event marked cancelled, or for a `CANCEL` of occurrences none of them held any more (or all the copy held, marked cancelled), the card then saying the event is not in the calendar while it holds none of them, a `REPLY`'s answer recorded, a `REQUEST` at its version, or a later one that is not marked as filed unverified - and when it does not, the card says that the calendar still has the event as it was, and that the server applies such a message only from a sender it verified, beside the buttons it always had.\n\n**A room or a piece of equipment** - an account whose kind says so - does not keep an invitation as not answered: it answers by itself just after the delivery, by its booking policy, accepting or declining with an iMIP `REPLY` from its own address, and leaves what it does not answer to its delegates, who answer for it from its calendar as a delegate answers for a person. It believes an organiser only when the server recorded that the organiser sent the message - a signed-in session of theirs, or for one from outside a DMARC pass - and files, answers or cancels nothing for anybody else. See [RoomsAndEquipment.md](RoomsAndEquipment.md).\n\n## What is tested\n\n`API/RestApiCalendar.cs` on both benches: calendars made, coloured, renamed, shared read-only and read-write with a second account that sees them over REST and in its CalDAV home with the privileges the share grants, `MKCALENDAR` and `PROPPATCH` from the phone's side, the default calendar kept and another deleted; a weekly 09:00 in Europe/London with a reminder expanded across the October clock change, one occurrence moved alone and another removed alone, the series renamed and deleted, with what CalDAV serves carrying the TZID, the rule, the override and the exclusion; tasks added, ordered, ticked off and read back over CalDAV, a phone's VTODO listed; an invitation mailed to a guest, read on their page, accepted into their calendar with a REPLY mailed and filed into the organiser's event on delivery, a counter-proposal reaching the organiser, and a cancellation mailed, filed and removed; a `CANCEL` in the ",
         "organiser's name from nobody signed in and from a co-guest signed in, and an updated `REQUEST` in the organiser's name from nobody signed in that would move the event and cancel it, leaving the guest's copy as it was, a `REPLY` in the guest's name from nobody signed in and a co-guest's signed-in `REPLY` answering for the guest - plainly, and as a delegate it is not - and for an address the event does not list changing only the co-guest's own answer, an account the event does not list, signed in, not added by answering for itself, and the guest's own answers, from the message's card and from a mail program signed in, and the organiser's signed-in `CANCEL` filed; the cards of the messages not applied answering `calendar_agrees: false` and of those applied `true`; a `CANCEL` of one occurrence of a weekly series from the organiser signed in taking that occurrence out of the guest's copy and leaving the others, not cancelled, its card saying the calendar holds it and the event no longer in it, one in the organiser's name from nobody signed in changing nothing and its card's remove taking out that occurrence alone, a `CANCEL` from an occurrence on (`RANGE=THISANDFUTURE`) ending the series there, and a `CANCEL` of the whole event still marking what is left cancelled; a copy planted under a UID by nobody verified, naming another organiser, left in place by the organiser's own invitation on delivery and replaced by the guest's Accept on that invitation's card, another planted `REQUEST` then changing nothing, while a phone's copy with no organiser still refuses an Accept under its UID (422); an invitation to an alias of the guest's answered from the card, set on the alias the organiser's event lists with nobody added, while a co-guest's answer in the guest's own address changes nothing; a `CANCEL` in the organiser's name sent through the send API with a key that may send as any address of the domain leaving the guest's copy as it was, and the organiser's own filed; and from outside, with the suite's DNS zone making DMARC pass for a partner's domain, a `CANCEL` and a `REPLY` in a partner's name from another sender at that domain, and a `REPLY` from a domain that publishes no DMARC policy, changing nothing, and the partner's own filed; an answer from a member of a distribution list the organiser invited filed beside the list, while a co-guest's answer in the member's name and an account outside the list answering for itself add nobody; and a `REQUEST` in the organiser's name from nobody signed in, at `SEQUENCE` 40, filed before the organiser invites the guest and then replaced by the organiser's own at `SEQUENCE` 0, signed in, with another such `REQUEST` at `SEQUENCE` 60 changing nothing after it; and messages from outside shaped as Outlook and Gmail write them, from a domain DMARC passes for - an organiser's invitation, update and cancellation, a guest's answer, and an answer an assistant sends for a guest with `SENT-BY` - applied as before. Its raw SMTP sessions use the suite's own port, so the Linux suite (`LinuxRegressionTests`) runs these too. `API/RestApiCalendarSharing.cs` (ports 9818 and 9819): the free-busy of a colleague with a busy hour, a tentative half hour and a recurring meeting, a transparent, a cancelled and a declined event left out and a calendar shared with the colleague not counted, no title in the answer, the window's three forms and its 62-day ceiling, another hosted domain's account, an unknown address, an inactive account and an account that chose nobody one 404 and the choice put back answering again, an alias domain's spelling resolved, a weekly 09:00 in Europe/London busy at 08:00Z before the October clock change and 09:00Z after, and a daily rule filling 62 days with 62 periods; the CalDAV `free-busy-query` on an own and a shared calendar answering the route's periods as a bare `VFREEBUSY`, the owner's other calendar absent from the shared one's report, refused without a time-range and past 62 days, and not fo",
         "und on a calendar not shared; a calendar shared with a group seen by a member over REST and CalDAV and not by a non-member, gone when the member leaves the group and back when they rejoin, refused on CalDAV PUT, DELETE and sync-collection once out, the owner in the group seeing their own calendar once, the widest rights of an own share and two groups, leaving while a group still reaches the calendar answered `left: false` with the group's name over REST and 403 over CalDAV and leaving for real once no group does, a name that is both an account and a group being the account, the share taken back by the group's name and one 404 for a group not shared and a name that is no group, a group shared as a delegate answered for the owner by a member with that member's SENT-BY, and the group deleted taking its share; a REQUEST filed on delivery as NEEDS-ACTION into the guest's default calendar and updated, not doubled, by the answer from the message, with nothing filed for an account that has no calendar, nothing filed from a message flagged as spam or from a REQUEST that names somebody else while one naming an alias of the account is filed, a forged REQUEST under the organiser's UID with a foreign ORGANIZER leaving the organiser's event and the guest's copy as they were, and one in the organiser's own name from nobody signed in leaving the guest's copy too, while the real organiser's signed-in resend at a higher SEQUENCE updates the guest's copy, and a resend at the same SEQUENCE keeping the answer given; and the delegate's reply, allowed to the delegate and the owner, 403 to a plain writer and a reader, 400 for an event the owner is not a guest of with nothing sent, the owner's PARTSTAT set with SENT-BY the delegate, the calendar's sync token and the object's ETag stepped, the organiser's REPLY carrying SENT-BY on the ATTENDEE and Sender: the delegate beside From: the owner and filed into the organiser's event with that SENT-BY, the delegate's own calendar untouched. `API/RoomsAndEquipment.cs` files a room's own answer into the organiser's event, and does not take an invitation sent through the send API in an organiser's name as the organiser's. The server's own tests (`ImipTester`, run by `Infrastructure.MainOperations.TestInternals` on Windows and by `imip-tests` on Linux) hold the reading of a message's `From:` - one field, one mailbox, read alike by the address parser and the DMARC check, and written as one mailbox - and the DMARC token, which vouches for exactly its domain, the rule for a `REPLY` and a `CANCEL` given who sent them (`Imip::ApplyToStored`: an alias the event lists, an occurrence a guest was left out of, a member of a list the event lists, a `CANCEL` of occurrences taken out of the series and nothing more - one, two, one an override had moved, one and every one after it, one written in a zone, of a series of days, of a floating series and from an `RDATE` on, one the series does not have and one after its last changing nothing, one that is all a guest holds cancelling it, and 1,500 occurrences of a daily series from 1900 named at once taken out in the time of a few expansions of it, not two for each, and three named 56 years apart each taken out, 17 of a series with 58,000 `EXDATE`s taken out in the time of a few of its expansions, each of which costs what one of the series without them and a reading of its `EXDATE`s does, the first occurrence of a series whose next two are out already taken out alone - on delivery and by the card's remove - and a cancellation from an occurrence on, past 70 out already, ending the series before it, and cancellations as Exchange, Google and Apple write them: of one occurrence across the October clock change, its `RECURRENCE-ID` in the `CANCEL`'s own zone (a quoted Windows zone name from Exchange), of one day of an all-day series, and Exchange's of the whole series cancelling what is left - and `Imip::TakeOutOccurrences`, what the card's remove takes out), the rule for an invitation by ",
         "who stands behind it (`Imip::KeepRule`, and the mark `Imip::FiledUnverified` reads), and where a message came from (`Imip::OriginOf`, which takes no message sent through the send API for an account's session), to the cases that must not change or vouch for anything. The page's harness (`build/portal-script-test.js`) walks the month, week, day and agenda views, the editor with a rule, a reminder and guests, the availability strip drawn for the guests named with a colleague's blocks and the event's span and a guest elsewhere marked, an occurrence edited alone, a chip dragged to another day, a block resized in the week view, a calendar added and shared with a group's share listed, an invitation in a calendar shared as a delegate answered on the owner's behalf, the tasks page, the invitation card answered and countered, and the card saying the calendar holds a cancellation, an answer or an update only when the server says it does.\n",
         nullptr
      };

