      const char *const Page20Pieces[] =
      {
         "# Control Panel design system\n\nThe desktop Control Panel (`hmailserver/source/Tools/ControlPanel`, WPF on .NET 10 with WPF-UI 4.3) is being redesigned to read as one administration console - calm, dense enough for an administrator, the same on every page, and right in the light theme, the dark theme and High Contrast. This document is the contract the page waves build against: it names every token and every component, with its exact name and its public surface, and it says what the shell does around a page. It was written on 15 September 2026 with the first wave, which delivered the frame and migrated two pages - the sign-in page and the dashboard - as the pattern, and the other three waves followed it the same day: every page, both long settings views and all eighteen dialogs are on it now; a test (`ControlPanel.Tests/Services/DesignTokensTests.cs`) holds this document to the tokens file and the components, so a token or a component that is not here does not build.\n\nThree files carry the design. `Services/DesignTokens.cs` holds the numbers and the colours as plain C#, with no WPF, so the tests can hold them; `Views/Scaffold/Tokens.xaml` declares the same values as resources for XAML; `Views/Scaffold/Scaffold.xaml` holds the components' templates, as implicit styles keyed by type, and merges the tokens. `App.xaml` merges `Scaffold.xaml` after WPF-UI's own dictionaries. `Services/ThemeTokens.cs` turns the colours into brushes on every theme change, and `Services/Typography.cs` still holds the type ramp the sizes below repeat. The three status colours themselves stay in `Services/StatusPalette.cs`, where `ColourVisionTests` holds every pair apart for colour-blind eyes.\n\n## The frame\n\n`MainWindow.xaml` is a WPF-UI `FluentWindow` with the Mica backdrop, in four rows: the title bar, the command bar, the sidebar beside the content and the help pane, and the pending bar.\n\nThe **command bar** (`command-bar`) is `AppCommandBarHeight` tall and holds, left to right: the sidebar toggle (`sidebar-toggle`, its tool tip and accessible name saying what a click will do), the brand, the search (`nav-search` - a button drawn as a field, because what it opens is the Ctrl+K palette and that is where the typing happens; the shortcut still works), the server status pill (`server-status`, a `StatusPill` fed by `ServerSession.LinkStateChanged` and by sign-in: *Not connected* while the sign-in page shows, *Connected*, *Reconnecting\xe2""\x80""\xa6""* while the session heals, *Connection lost* when it cannot, and *Paused*, a Warning, while the link is up and the server reports its engine stopped; its tool tip and accessible name are \"Connected to host as user\", and while the link heals or after it went the host and what happens next - \"Reconnecting to host - what is on screen may be out of date\", \"Connection to host lost - the Control Panel tries again on your next action; nothing can be saved until it is back\"), the theme toggle (`theme-toggle`) and the administrator's menu (`admin-menu`, showing the account's name once signed in and holding who is signed in where, the server version, the language, the theme and the About page, then *Connect to another server\xe2""\x80""\xa6""* and *Sign out*, and first of all *Sign in again* while the link is lost). Each of the last three asks about a page's unsaved changes as every way off a page does, then ends the session - the server's object released, the password forgotten (`ServerSession.SignOut`), every page built for it let go - and shows the sign-in page on the server and the account that were signed in, the keyboard in the password, or in the server's box to type another; a refused second factor goes the same way (`MainWindow.ShowSignIn_`). Nothing that was in the sidebar's footer is lost: the connection badge became the pill, the language and theme buttons moved into the bar. **Before sign-in** the sidebar - the tree or the rail, its footer - the sidebar toggle and the search are not shown at all, and the content area runs to the window's ",
         "left edge with a square corner: they cannot be used until somebody has signed in, and drawn as they are when they work they offered sixty pages that did nothing when clicked (the owner's choice of 9 October 2026, to hide them rather than dim them). Each follows whether it can be used - `NavTree.IsEnabled` and `SearchButton.IsEnabled`, which the window clears before sign-in and after a refused second factor and sets on sign-in - so there is one switch and no second state to keep in step; the help button stays, dimmed, and the theme toggle and the administrator's menu work as before.\n\nThe **sidebar** is the `TreeView` built from `Services/NavigationMap.cs`, in the order a new administrator needs it (since 27 September 2026): *Welcome*, *Dashboard*, then *Set up this server* - the *Setup checklist*, *Setting profiles* and *DNS records* - then *Accounts & domains*, *Mail flow & delivery*, *Connections & protocols*, *Certificates & encryption*, *Spam filtering*, *Virus scanning*, *Sign-in & access*, *Monitoring & troubleshooting*, *Compliance*, *Maintenance* and *About*. Every page automation id (`nav-<key>`) is what it was; one page title changed (*External setup* is the *Setup checklist*), and two group headings were renamed in plain words - *TLS & certificates* is *Certificates & encryption*, and *Access & abuse protection* is *Sign-in & access*; each old name is an alias the palette finds, and a group's `navgroup-<slug>` follows its heading. *REST API keys* is under *Sign-in & access*, after *Administrative access* (since 10 October 2026, review finding F152): a key is a credential, and *Monitoring & troubleshooting* asks whether mail is flowing. The audit trail, journaling and eDiscovery are *Compliance*, after it, and the three virus pages *Virus scanning*, after *Spam filtering*, which holds spam alone (review findings F152 and F153, the owner's decision of 12 October 2026), each group its rail glyph - the gavel and the bug; at 760 by 520 the rail's last glyphs are below the fold, which is accepted, and the old heading *Spam & virus filtering* is an alias of both groups. Inside *Spam filtering* the six allow and block lists are under the sub-heading *Lists* (review finding F269): a sub-heading is a run of pages that share a `Section` in the map (`NavigationMap.SectionBefore` decides where one is drawn, on the pages as the credential sees them), drawn as a caption at the pages' text column that neither the pointer nor the keyboard lands on and a level-3 heading to a screen reader, in the tree and in the rail's flyout alike; it is not a level of the tree, so the breadcrumb and the palette do not know of it. A page row ellipsizes its title when the pane is narrow, as a group row does, and the whole title is in its tool tip. Rows are `AppNavRowHeight` tall with the control radius, the hover fill and the brand pill on the selected row, the secondary text at rest and the focus border round the row the keyboard is on, at every level: the tree's row style is each group's item style as well (`MainWindow.BuildNavItem`), since a `TreeView` gives its `ItemContainerStyle` to its own items alone, and the pages inside a group, which are their own containers, took WPF-UI's row until 9 October 2026 - the Windows accent's bar outside a shorter highlight, primary text at rest, no keyboard border. Pages are indented under their group's label rather than under its glyph, so the two text columns line up. Below `DesignTokens.Shell.CollapseSidebarBelow` (1000 pixels of window width) the sidebar collapses to the **rail** (`nav-rail`): one glyph per top-level group (`navrail-<slug>`), the group of the page on screen marked. Above that width a click on a glyph expands the sidebar on that group; below it the tree cannot be shown - a maximised window at 150 % scaling is 853 pixels - so the click opens the group's **flyout** instead (`MainWindow.NavFlyout.cs`): a popup beside the glyph (`nav-flyout`) listing the group's pages as rows the credential may o",
         "pen (`navflyout-<key>`), the page on screen marked, the arrow keys moving through them, Enter opening the page the keyboard is on and a click the one under the pointer, Escape or a click outside closing it, and the keyboard going back to what opened it - the glyph, or the breadcrumb's group button as the breadcrumb was rebuilt for the page chosen. The breadcrumb's group button opens the same flyout under itself while the rail shows, and reveals the group in the tree otherwise. A click never leaves the rail marking a group that does not hold the page on screen. The toggle collapses and expands the sidebar by choice above the threshold, and the choice is kept in the registry beside the window bounds (`SidebarCollapsed` under `HKCU\\Software\\hMailServer\\ControlPanel`). The footer under the tree carries the server's version and hides with the rail.\n\nThe **content area** keeps the breadcrumb bar (`breadcrumb-bar`, the trail and the *Related:* signposts) above the page. At the start of the bar, **Back** and **Forward** (`history-back`, `history-forward`, `MainWindow.History.cs`) open the page opened before and the one Back left, each naming its page in its tool tip and its accessible name, and neither pressable when there is nothing that way: the pages since sign-in, fifty at most (`Services/NavigationHistory`), each kept with its scroll offset and the tab its first tab strip was on, so that Back returns to the place as well as to the page. A step goes through the unsaved-changes question as every way to a page does, and Stay leaves the page and the history as they were. The **Related links** (`MainWindow.Related.cs`) are as many of the page's list as fit beside the trail, in the order the navigation map lists them and again as the window is resized, with the rest in a *More (n)* drop-down - *Related pages (n)* alone where not even the first fits - and only the pages the session's role may open (`NavigationMap.RelatedOf`); a jump to a page of the map that the role's tree leaves out says so in a toast, \"Your role cannot open Rules.\", where it did nothing. A page owns its own `ScrollViewer` and pads itself with `AppPagePadding`; the host adds nothing. The window remembers its size, position and maximised state as before.\n\nThe **shell's geometry** (T3.3 work item 7). The tree is a share of the window's width, 0.32 beside the page's 1 - 310 pixels at 1280, between `SidebarMinWidth` and `SidebarMaxWidth` - set in pixels as the window changes (`Services/ShellGeometry`, `MainWindow.Geometry.cs`), so that the **help pane** (`help-pane`, `Views/HelpPane.cs`), the third column that the command bar's help button and F1 open and its splitter sizes, takes its width from the page alone: as a star column beside the page the tree was squeezed by the pane to its 200-pixel floor at 1280 and cut three group names, and at the opening size \"Web services & autoconfiguration\" was cut in English and seven page labels in German, of which \"\xc3""\x9c""bersicht der Transportverschl\xc3""\xbc""sselung\" and \"Servergrenzen und Experteneinstellungen\" still end in an ellipsis at 310 pixels, whole in their tool tips (review findings F021, F022). Where the tree and the pane would leave the page less than 512 pixels (`ShellGeometry.PageMinWidthBesideHelp`) the sidebar is the rail while the pane is open - a glyph opening its group's flyout, as below the collapse threshold - and the tree again once the pane closes; neither is recorded as the administrator's choice, and the sidebar toggle still expands the tree beside the pane until the pane closes. Every top-level row has a glyph - Welcome `Home24`, the Dashboard `DataUsage24` and About `Info24` beside the groups' - so the rail draws no letters and the three labels start where the groups' do, and Sign-in & access is `PersonLock24`, no longer a second shield beside the spam group's (F023); `ShellGeometryTests` holds the glyphs distinct and no two neighbours of one family. The breadcrumb is one line: its trail is a `BreadcrumbTrailPanel` (`Vie",
         "ws/Scaffold`), each part as wide as it is where the line holds them all; where it does not, the page's name has the priority - the groups' segments and the search's marker are cut first, to 48 pixels, then the marker is left out, then the page's name is cut, to 96, then the groups to their ellipsis at 24, and the page's name last (`ShellGeometry.BreadcrumbWidths`) - each ending in an ellipsis and whole in its tool tip, and the Related links keep `DesignTokens.Space.Md`, 12 pixels, from it, where a `WrapPanel` put \"Certificates (ACME)\" on a second line in the narrowest window and \"Transport security\" ran into \"Related:\", and a `DockPanel` that gave the page's name what the group left gave it nothing beside the help pane at 760 (F047).\n\nThe **keys** the window answers on every page are one table, `Services/KeyboardShortcuts` (`MainWindow.Keys.cs` is the window's half): Ctrl+K opens the palette; F1 opens and closes the help pane, and Escape with the keyboard in the pane closes it and puts the keyboard on the Help button; F5 presses the page's *Refresh* - a list page's verb - or its *Reload* - a settings page's, which asks before it throws edits away; Ctrl+S presses the page's *Save changes*; Ctrl+F puts the keyboard in the page's search box, the one nearest the keyboard where a page has two (the domains page), and opens the palette on a page with none; and F6 and Shift+F6 move round the parts of the window that are on screen - the command bar, the navigation, the page, the help pane and the foot of the window, the pending bar and the paused server's bar - landing on the search, the sidebar's row for the page on screen (the rail's glyph while the rail shows), the control of the page that last had the keyboard, and the first control of the others. Alt+Left and Alt+Right are Back and Forward, as the keyboard's own Back and Forward keys and the mouse's two side buttons are; Alt with a letter stays the access keys'. While a tour runs, F6 reaches its card from anywhere but the card, as before. `Views/PageCommands` is what F5 and Ctrl+S reach: the page's own button, by its caption (*Refresh* and *Reload*, one fixed access key each, and *Save changes*) or, for a button whose caption says more, by `PageCommands.Command` (the scripts page's *Reload from disk* and *Save & reload*), pressed through its automation peer, so that a key does exactly what a click does and a button that cannot be pressed now cannot be pressed by its key either. The palette's *Keyboard shortcuts* row lists them all, from the same table, in the reader's language (Strg+F in German). `KeyboardShortcutsTests` holds the table, the order F6 moves in, the wiring, and every re-read caption to *Refresh* or *Reload*, the second only on a page that can hold an unsaved change.\n\nThe **help pane** (`help-pane`, `Views/HelpPane.cs` and `HelpPane.List.cs`) is the column beside the content that the command bar's help button (`help-toggle`) and F1 open and close, sized by the splitter at its left edge between 260 and 760 pixels and remembered with whether it is open (`HelpPaneOpen` and `HelpPaneWidth` beside the window bounds). A pane, not a window or a dialog: the page stays usable while the text is open. It shows the document the page's entry in `Services/HelpMap.cs` names, scrolled to the entry's heading, and follows the page and its tab as they change; a page no document is about shows its purpose, a sentence saying that no guide covers it, and the list of every document. Its head holds *Back* (`help-back`, named by where it goes), there while a link in the pane - in a document, in the list or *All documentation* - has shown something else, then the title and *Close* (`help-close`); a page's help starts that history again, and Back pressed from the keyboard with nowhere left to go back to hands the keyboard to the search. Under the head the search (`help-search`) finds in the open document, Enter the next match, or, while the list shows, says *Search all documentation* and narro",
         "ws the list to the documents whose title or text holds the words - two characters or more - the line under it (`help-list-result`, a polite live region) saying how many do or that none does, Enter opening the first. The list is grouped as the sidebar is - under each group the documents its pages and their tabs open - then *Reference*, every other document an administrator reads, and *Building and packaging*, the project's own - its pipelines, its build and test environments and each of its packages, the snap, the Nix flake and Alpine's among them - each heading's documents by title (`Services/HelpDocumentList.cs`). The foot holds *Open on the wiki*, while a document the project's wiki has a page made from shows and a wiki address is set - it opens the address with that page's name added, the project wiki's own name for it, whatever the address (`Services/HelpWikiPages.g.cs`, from `build/help-wiki-pages.txt`) - and *All documentation*.\n\nThe **pending bar** (`pending-settings-bar`) is the window's last row: the saved settings the server is not running on yet ([SettingsApply.md](SettingsApply.md)) - each key marked reinitialise or restart that was saved since what applies it last happened - with *Apply now*, which reinitialises for the ones a reinitialise takes, and *Restart the service\xe2""\x80""\xa6""* for the ones only a restart takes, offered while the server is the one on this machine. The notice's tool tip names the settings; the bar is collapsed while nothing waits, and neither button carries an access key, as the bar is on screen with every page.\n\nA page that edits says whether it holds **unsaved changes** - `IPageLifecycle.HasUnsavedChanges`, with `SaveChanges()` to save them as its own Save does - because the window keeps a page once it is built and the page reads the server again as it is next opened. The window asks before anything replaces such a page: the sidebar's selection, which every link, the palette and a tour go through, closing the window and a change of language. *Save* goes on only when everything was saved, *Discard* goes on, and *Stay* - Escape's and Enter's answer - keeps the page and puts the sidebar back on it. A page's own Reload asks *Discard your unsaved changes and read the settings again?* before it reads the server. `Services/UnsavedChanges.cs` holds the decision and `EditorBaseline`, the record of what a hand-built page's editors held when they were last read or saved; `UnsavedChangesTests` holds both and the wiring, including that every page with a *Save changes* button says whether it holds an unsaved change.\n\nA **paused server** is said under every page (`MainWindow.ServerPaused.cs`, review finding F019): Server status's *Pause the mail server* stops the server's engine - no connection is accepted and nothing is delivered - while the Windows service, and so the link, keeps running, and the pill went on saying *Connected* in green. While the server reports Stopped, a bar at the foot of the window under the pending bar (`server-paused-bar`) says \"The mail server is paused - nothing is accepted or delivered.\" with *Resume the mail server*, Server status's own caption for the same act, which carries no Alt key here, as the pending bar's buttons do not, and the pill says *Paused* at the Warning level with \"Connected to host as user - the mail server is paused\" as its tool tip and name; Server status's State says *Paused* as well, where it said *Stopped* under the pill. The window asks the server's state as each page is entered and on the Dashboard's two-second refresh, and Server status hands it the state it reads; a state that cannot be read shows no bar. Live logs' button that freezes the view is *Pause view*, with a glyph of its own (`PauseCircle24`), where the two pages offered the same *Pause* with the same glyph for a server stopped and a list frozen.\n\n## Tokens\n\nThe numbers first. Every margin, padding and gap in a page is one of the six steps; a layout that needs a seventh has gone wrong, and `build/chec",
         "k-spacing.py` fails a margin or a padding written off the scale in the Control Panel's XAML or C# (review finding F295, which counted 189 of 682 off it, and the page's and the card's own paddings on a seventh step, 20): an optical nudge that has to stay - the few pixels that set a mark beside a line of text, a pill's inner padding - is in its baseline, `build/spacing-baseline.txt`, which may only shrink (`--write` lowers it, and a new entry is added by hand, in the change that needs it, where a reviewer sees it; `--rebaseline`, which adds entries as well as removing them, is for the one who folds branches written before the check, who reads the baseline's diff). XAML takes the scale through the named margins and paddings below, a `Margin` and a `Padding` being Thicknesses; the six steps themselves are C#, `DesignTokens.Space.Xs` \xe2""\x80""\xa6"" `Xxl`, `DesignTokens.Radius.Control` / `Card` / `Pill`, `DesignTokens.Shell.CommandBarHeight` / `RailWidth` / `NavRowHeight` / `CollapseSidebarBelow` / `SidebarMinWidth` / `SidebarMaxWidth`, and `DesignTokens.Dialog.MinWidth` / `NarrowWidth` / `DefaultWidth` / `WideWidth` / `WidestWidth` / `ManyTabsWidth` / `MaxWidth` / `MaxHeightFraction` (360, 420, 520, 640, 760, 800, 900, 0.9). A dialog names one of the four widths - a prompt of one field is narrow, a message box and a form of a few fields the default, one with a table or a wide row wide, one whose body is a grid of columns or a message's source the widest - where each named a number of its own (400, 460, 480, 560, 600, 620, 680); the domain and account dialogs, of seventeen and sixteen tabs, take `ManyTabsWidth`, and the tabbed dialogs of a few tabs, the IP range and route dialogs, start from `WideWidth` and widen to their tab strips where the headers need it, as they do in German (`DialogFields.WidthForTabs`). No dialog names a number, in its frame's call or in a constant that the call is given (`SpacingScaleTests`).\n\n| Token | Value | What it is for |\n|---|---|---|\n| `DesignTokens.Space.Xs` | 4 | The gap between a mark and its word, a label and its editor. |\n| `DesignTokens.Space.Sm` | 8 | The gap between buttons in a row, between a pill and a title. |\n| `DesignTokens.Space.Md` | 12 | The gap between cards in a row, under a toolbar, between the rows of a form, under a card's description and over its footer's buttons. |\n| `DesignTokens.Space.Lg` | 16 | Under a page header, a card's inset above and below. |\n| `DesignTokens.Space.Xl` | 24 | The page's padding, a card's inset at the sides, between the sections of a settings page, a dialog's inset. |\n| `DesignTokens.Space.Xxl` | 32 | The inset of the sign-in card and of an empty or loading state. |\n| `AppPagePadding` | 24 | The padding of a page's `ScrollViewer`. Every page uses it; a page with a padding of its own is a page that will look wrong beside the others. |\n| `AppCardPadding` | 24,16 | A card's inset - the `Card` component's default and the old `Card` Border style's. The page's padding and the card's are the Control Deck's (`--s5` round the content, `--s4 --s5` in a card), where they were 24,20,24,24 and 20,16. |\n| `AppListCardPadding` | 8 | The compact inset of a card that holds a list or a grid, whose rows carry their own ten pixels (`DesignTokens.ListCardPadding`); it was 4, 6, 8, 10 or 12, page by page. |\n| `AppDialogPadding` | 24 | A dialog's inset round its heading, body and footer (`DialogFrame`), where it was 24,20,24,20. |\n| `AppHeaderGap` | 0,0,0,16 | The margin under a `PageHeader`. |\n| `AppCardGap` | 0,0,0,12 | The margin under a card, a toolbar or a notice that another follows. |\n| `AppFieldGap` | 0,0,0,12 | The margin under a `FieldRow`. |\n| `AppSectionGap` | 0,0,0,24 | The margin under a `SettingsSection`. |\n| `AppControlCornerRadius` | 4 | Controls, navigation rows, notices. |\n| `AppCardCornerRadius` | 8 | Cards and dialogs. |\n| `AppPillCornerRadius` | 4 | The status pill's box: the control radius, so it reads as a filled box and not a capsule. |\n| `AppCommandBarHeight` | 48",
         " | The command bar. |\n| `AppFormWidth` | 760 | The widest a form is laid out, whatever the window does: a caption and its editor at opposite ends of a maximised window are unreadable. `DesignTokens.FormWidth`. A page's column is this, for a form or a page of text, or `DesignTokens.OverviewWidth`, 1000, for an overview of tiles and tables - the spam, virus and transport encryption overviews, DNS records, External setup and Welcome - and names it, where the columns were 680, 760, 860, 880, 980 and 1000 pixels, page by page (review finding F295; `SpacingScaleTests`). |\n| `AppSubtitleWidth` | 840 | The widest a page's subtitle runs on its line under the title: some 120 characters of body text, two lines for the two sentences a subtitle holds. `DesignTokens.SubtitleWidth`. |\n| `AppCheckBoxIndent` | 28,0,0,0 | Under a check box's caption: the box, 20, and the gap after it, 8 (`DesignTokens.CheckBoxCaptionInset`). An option that belongs to the box above it is indented by it, and the lines under a box - its hint, Applies line and message in a `FieldRow`, a `DialogFields.Note` - start there. |\n| `AppRailWidth` | 48 | The sidebar collapsed to icons. |\n| `AppNavRowHeight` | 34 | One navigation row, and the minimum height of a grid row. |\n| `AppIconSizeSm` | 16 | A glyph beside text or on a small button: the breadcrumb's Back and Forward, the search field's, a row's 24-pixel button's (`DesignTokens.IconSize`; icon sizes ran from 12 to 40 with no token, review finding F296). |\n| `AppIconSizeMd` | 20 | A toolbar's or a page header's glyph button. |\n| `AppIconSizeLg` | 24 | The rail's group glyph. |\n| `AppIconSizeXl` | 40 | An empty state's glyph. |\n\n## Type\n\nThe ramp is Windows 11's: Caption 12, Body 14 (Body Strong is 14 SemiBold), Subtitle 20, Title 28. The sizes are resources and the roles are keyed `TextBlock` styles. They are keyed on purpose and must stay so: an implicit TextBlock style reaches into every control template and overrides the on-accent button text, which is how the forum's one look complaint arose (the note in `App.xaml` tells the story). The default text colour comes from property inheritance - the window and `FluentDialogWindow` set their Foreground to the theme's primary text brush - so a bare TextBlock needs no style to be readable.\n\n| Token | Value | What it is for |\n|---|---|---|\n| `AppFontSizeCaption` | 12 | Captions, hints, field labels, the breadcrumb, the pill. |\n| `AppFontSizeBody` | 14 | Body text, controls, navigation rows, card titles. |\n| `AppFontSizeSubtitle` | 20 | A dialog's heading, the sign-in card's heading. |\n| `AppFontSizeTitle` | 28 | The page title. |\n| `AppFontSizeMono` | 13 | Data in the monospaced face (`Typography.MonoFontFamily`, Cascadia Mono then Consolas; `Typography.Mono` in code): a cipher list or a list of key exchange groups, an LDAP filter, search base or DN template, a log line, a script, a token or a key, a DNS record, a path - in a box or a block of its own, the help pane's code blocks among them; the one-time code boxes, 28 for six digits typed off a phone, are the one exception. Data inside a sentence is a run of its own in that face at the sentence's size (`Services/DataText`: a colon-separated list breaks only after a colon, its hyphens the ASCII one held to the next letter by a word joiner, since Consolas, the face where Cascadia Mono is missing, has no non-breaking hyphen, a long path is cut in its middle with the whole of it in the tool tip). A sentence that is mostly data - the stalled-mail guide's \"{0} with a large {1}\", SpamTestSpamAssassin and Time: - keeps its words in the sentence's face, its data passed as the placeholders and set as runs (`DataTextBlock.FillPlaceholders`), so that no word around the data reads as data. The live log was 12, the diagnostics 12, the help pane's code blocks 12, and the event script, the TOTP setup key, the generated password and the quarantine's headers 14 (review findings F135, F304); `MonospacedDataTests` reads every site of the face ",
         "in the sources. |\n| `AppFontSizeGrid` | 13 | A grid cell's text. |\n| `AppLineHeightLog` | 17 | The height of a line of Live logs, whose text is `AppFontSizeMono`'s 13. |\n| `TextTitle` | 28 SemiBold, primary, trimmed with an ellipsis | The page title; the `PageHeader` draws it. |\n| `TextSubtitle` | 20 SemiBold, primary, wraps | A dialog's or a card-page's heading. |\n| `TextBodyStrong` | 14 SemiBold, primary, wraps | Card titles, section headings, the summary line of a notice card. |\n| `TextBody` | 14, primary, wraps | Body text. |\n| `TextSecondary` | 14, secondary, wraps | The page subtitle, an empty state's sentence. |\n| `TextCaption` | 12, secondary, wraps | Hints, descriptions, field labels, notes. |\n| `TextCaptionTertiary` | 12, tertiary, wraps | The quietest text: a keyboard hint, a footer line. |\n\nText follows the Windows Text size setting (Settings > Accessibility > Text size, the registry's `TextScaleFactor`). `TextScale.Install` reads it when the Control Panel starts, before the first window, and sets every font-size resource at it - the tokens above and WPF-UI's own, such as `ControlContentThemeFontSize` - and the line heights of text set in lines (`AppLineHeight*`), and `Typography`'s sizes for text set in code are at it too; an icon's size is not text and keeps its own. So text takes its size from a token by `DynamicResource` (in code, from `Typography`), never as a number of its own; a column of words is `Auto` or a share of the width over a floor - floors measured from its words by `GridStyles`, or a list of fields' named widths, which `TextScaleFactor.Scaled` raises with the text - never a fixed width that larger text cuts short, and only a column of numbers keeps its pixels; grid floors measured by `GridStyles` are measured in the scaled font; and a dialog widens with its text. When Windows says the setting changed, the resources follow at once and a toast says that pages already open keep the rest of their text until a restart. `TextScaleTests` holds it.\n\nTwo rules the shared templates in `App.xaml` keep, because each was broken once and reached every page. The tab strip weights and sizes its headers alone: the selected tab's SemiBold and the strip's 14 px are set on the header's presenter in the `TabItem` template (`TextElement.FontWeight`, `TextElement.FontSize`), never on the `TabItem`, since both inherit and a tab's page is the `TabItem`'s logical child - a setter on the item made every caption, hint and editor value on the open tab as heavy as its card titles. And a check box or radio button whose `Content` is a string shows it in an `AccessText` that wraps (an implicit data template for strings in the two implicit styles), so a long caption folds under itself instead of being cut mid-word at the edge of its card or dialog, keeps its Alt key, and is still the box's accessible name; a box whose `Content` is an element of its own is presented as before. Both implicit styles are based on WPF-UI's under its own key (`DefaultCheckBoxStyle`, `DefaultRadioButtonStyle`), never on `{StaticResource {x:Type CheckBox}}`: inside the dictionary that defines an implicit style, a StaticResource for the type key finds the entry being defined and yields null, and a style based on nothing gives every box the classic theme's control. The box stands on the text column, where its card's or dialog's captions, boxes and hints start - WPF-UI's padding put it eleven pixels right of them (review finding F294) - and its caption starts `DesignTokens.CheckBoxCaptionInset` (28 pixels) further in, as a radio button's does; the lines under a box start under its caption, which `FieldRow` does for its hint, Applies line and message when its editor is a check box or a radio button, and `DialogFields.Note` for a note laid under one, and an option that belongs to the box above it is indented by `AppCheckBoxIndent`. A caption that needs more than a line or two is better short, with the consequence in a note under it that is also the box's help ",
         "text (`AutomationProperties.SetHelpText`), so a screen reader reading the box still hears it. `SharedTemplatesTests` holds the templates and `ConsequenceNoteTests` the help text.\n\nText is set ragged right and unhyphenated everywhere, never justified, and two of WPF's defaults justify it. A `FlowDocument` justifies unless it is told otherwise, so every document the help pane shows starts from `MarkdownFlow.NewDocument` (review finding F026). WPF-UI's styles stretch a text box's content, which WPF's text box reads as justified text, so the implicit `TextBox` styles in `App.xaml` set a box that wraps - a signature, an auto-reply, a rule's reply, a DKIM record - to `HorizontalContentAlignment` Left, where each of its wrapped lines was spread to the box's width; a one-line box keeps WPF-UI's. `HelpDocumentTypographyTests` and `RaggedRightTextBoxTests` hold the two.\n\n## Colour\n\nFive status colours and a neutral, as brushes that `ThemeTokens.Refresh` recomputes on every theme change and republishes on the application's own dictionary, which shadows the dark values `Tokens.xaml` declares. Reference them with `DynamicResource`, never `StaticResource`, and in code with `SetResourceReference` by key (`StatusSemantics.For(level).BrushKey` gives the key for a level) rather than by holding a brush - a resolved brush keeps the colour of the theme it was resolved under. The light values each clear 4.5:1 on white; the dark values clear it on #1B1B1B. Under High Contrast every colour comes from the system palette and none is invented: brand and information are the highlight, the three status colours are the window text - their shape and word tell them apart, which is the rule below - neutral is the disabled-text grey, a card is the window colour with a window-text outline, and the notice tint goes to zero.\n\n| Token | Light | Dark | High Contrast | What it is for |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| `AppBrandBrush` | #2F6FE0 | #4C8DFF | Highlight | The selection pill, the selected tab's underline, the title bar's glyph, the accent that is not a status - and WPF-UI's accent: `ThemeTokens` gives it to WPF-UI after every theme change (`DesignTokens.Accent`), and republishes the brushes WPF-UI draws in its accent (`DesignTokens.AccentBrushes`), which its theme dictionary made with the Windows accent and keep it, so that the primary button, a ticked check box, a radio button, a switch, the progress ring and a text selection are this blue whatever accent Windows has (the owner's choice of 9 October 2026, the brand over the Windows accent; review finding F045), the primary button's fill a shade darker under the pointer and pressed in the light theme so that its white words keep 4.5:1. Under High Contrast the accent is the user's. The logo's `BrandGradient` is the one other blue. |\n| `AppSuccessBrush` | #1A7F37 | #3FB950 | WindowText | Good: connected, done, passed. Square. |\n| `AppWarningBrush` | #9A6700 | #D29922 | WindowText | Warning: a backlog, a certificate near expiry, a reconnect in progress. Triangle. |\n| `AppDangerBrush` | #CF222E | #F85149 | WindowText | Critical: a failure, a lost link, a validation message. Cross. |\n| `AppInfoBrush` | #6639BA | #A371F7 | Highlight | Information: a fact worth a notice. Diamond. Held at least \xce""\x94""E 8 from the three above under protanopia and deuteranopia. |\n| `AppNeutralBrush` | #57606A | #9DA7B0 | GrayText | A status that says nothing: not connected, a count of zero. |\n| `AppCardBackgroundBrush` | #B3FFFFFF | #0FFFFFFF | Window | The card surface. Translucent, so it sits on Mica and on the page alike. |\n| `AppCardBorderBrush` | #0F000000 | #14FFFFFF | WindowText | The card's outline. |\n| `AppCardHoverBrush` | #80F9F9F9 | #15FFFFFF | Window | A card that can be pointed at - a tile, a rail item - under the pointer. |\n| `AppDividerBrush` | #0F000000 | #14FFFFFF | WindowText | The hairline between a card's content and its footer, under the command bar, around the breadcrumb. |\n| `AppNoticeTintOpacity` | 0.12 | 0.12 | 0 | The ",
         "opacity of the status colour behind an `InlineNotice`. |\n| `AppFocusBrush` | #7542E5 | #B89CFF | Highlight | Where the keyboard is: the ring round a text field that has it, and the navigation's border round the row it is on. 3:1 or more beside every surface and beside its glow. |\n| `AppFocusGlowBrush` | #407542E5 | #47B89CFF | none | The glow outside that ring, at its strongest; it fades over six pixels. |\n| `AppSelectionFillBrush` | #1F2F6FE0 | #1F4C8DFF | none | Behind the selected row of every grid, list box and list view: the brand at the notice tint's opacity, so near the card's own that the row's text stays what it was. Beside it the brand pill at the row's left edge. |\n| `AppSelectionOutlineBrush` | none | none | Highlight | The one-pixel outline round a selected row under High Contrast, where the fill is nothing and the pill is the highlight too. |\n| `AppSuccessFillBrush` | #CCF0D6 | #1C4128 | Window | The fill of a Good `StatusPill` - \"Connected\", \"Done\". |\n| `AppSuccessFillTextBrush` | #116329 | #56D364 | WindowText | The word and mark on that fill, and a Good notice's word and mark. |\n| `AppInfoFillBrush` | #EBE0FF | #3A2A63 | Window | The fill of an Information pill - \"Next step\". |\n| `AppInfoFillTextBrush` | #5A32A3 | #C8A2FF | Highlight | The word and mark on it, and an Information notice's. |\n| `AppWarningFillBrush` | #FBE7AE | #45350F | Window | The fill of a Warning pill - \"Action needed\", \"Reconnecting\xe2""\x80""\xa6""\". |\n| `AppWarningFillTextBrush` | #7D4E00 | #E3B341 | WindowText | The word and mark on it, and a Warning notice's. |\n| `AppDangerFillBrush` | #FFDAD6 | #522024 | Window | The fill of a Critical pill - \"Connection lost\". |\n| `AppDangerFillTextBrush` | #A40E26 | #FF9492 | WindowText | The word and mark on it, a Critical notice's, and a destructive button's words (`DangerTextButton`). |\n| `AppNeutralFillBrush` | #DFE4E9 | #3A4048 | Window | The fill of a Normal pill - \"Not connected\", a count. |\n| `AppNeutralFillTextBrush` | #424A53 | #D1D9E0 | GrayText | The word and mark on it. |\n| `AppStatusFillBorderThickness` | 0 | 0 | 1 | The pill's outline: none where the fill is the box, one pixel in the text colour under High Contrast, whose fill is the window colour. |\n| `AppDangerSolidBrush` | #CF222E | #F85149 | WindowText | The fill of a filled destructive button - every `Appearance=\"Danger\"`: a confirmation's destructive choice, *Start restore*. App.xaml's implicit button style gives it, in place of WPF-UI's own #F44336. |\n| `AppDangerSolidHoverBrush` | #B91C28 | #FA6B64 | WindowText | That fill under the pointer. |\n| `AppDangerSolidPressedBrush` | #A40E26 | #E5534B | WindowText | That fill pressed. |\n| `AppOnDangerSolidBrush` | #FFFFFF | #1B1B1B | Window | The words on it, 4.5:1 or more on all three fills (5.4:1 at rest in the light theme, 5.1:1 in the dark); WPF-UI's left the theme's primary text there, white in the dark theme, 3.7:1. |\n| `AppLinkBrush` | #0B5CB5 | #6CB6FF | HotTrack | Every link. A `Hyperlink` - the About page's, the help pane's list of documents, a link in a help document - takes it through the implicit `Hyperlink` style in `App.xaml`, underlined; WPF-UI's link button (`HyperlinkButton`, a link with no underline and the theme's subtle fill under the pointer, which no page draws since the sign-in page's *Set up two-factor authentication\xe2""\x80""\xa6""* went in October 2026 - both factors are set up on Administrative access, review finding F241) takes it through `HyperlinkButtonForeground`, which `ThemeTokens` republishes on the application's own dictionary in place of the theme's shade of the Windows accent, and takes away under High Contrast, where the theme's own stands. 4.5:1 or more on the page, a card, a dialog and a hovered or selected row; WPF's own was the system's #0066CC, 2.5:1 on the dark card. |\n| `AppLinkHoverBrush` | #084A94 | #9CCFFF | HotTrack | A link under the pointer, a shade further from the surface and a `Hyperlink` still underlined, where WPF's own turned red, the danger colour; the link",
         " button takes it pressed as well (`HyperlinkButtonForegroundPointerOver` and `HyperlinkButtonForegroundPressed`). |\n\nThe fill-text shades are not the status colours: those are measured against the page and lose contrast on a tint - the light green on the old grey capsule measured 4.2:1, and on a notice's tint the light warning word measured 4.0:1 and the dark information word on a card 3.6:1. Every fill-text shade clears 5.5:1 on its fill, on a notice's tint, on the page and on a card, and `DesignTokensTests` holds each pair to 4.5:1. `DesignTokens.StatusFill(level, theme, system)` is the table. A status word or sentence anywhere on a surface takes its level's fill-text shade as well (`StatusSemantics.For(level).FillTextBrushKey`) - a certificate's verdict and its mark, a scanner's problem, a field's error and its mark, an action's result line, the never-ban list's status, the password strength line, the dashboard's *Backlog* badge - and the status colour itself (`BrushKey`) is a mark's colour, for shapes, glyphs, a chart's series and a notice's tint and outline, whose floor is 3:1 - Normal's being the neutral grey, `AppNeutralBrush`, where it was the primary text, so that a Normal mark was a black dot in the light theme and a white one in the dark (review finding F326), and a mark drawn beside its own word on a surface, a notice's or a certificate finding's, takes `MarkBrushKey`, the word's fill-text shade at every level with a colour and the grey at Normal: the status colours were measured against #1B1B1B, and on the dark card (#2D2D2D) the danger and information colours are 4.1:1 as words and 3.5:1 on a hovered or selected row. `ContrastTokensTests` holds every shade to 4.5:1 on the page, a card, a dialog and a hovered and a selected row, and fails a view that sets a text's colour to a status colour; the dashboard's queue length, a 28-pixel number and so large text, is the one exception.\n\nThe light theme's **tertiary text** is darker than Wpf.Ui's: `ThemeTokens` sets `TextFillColorTertiaryBrush` to black at 58% (`DesignTokens.LightTertiaryText`, #94000000), which composites to #696969 on the page (5.3:1) and #656565 on a hovered row (5.1:1). Wpf.Ui's black at 45% measured 3.3:1, and every field hint, \"Applies on save\" line and the *Related:* caption is set in it. The dark theme's is lighter than Wpf.Ui's, white at 60% (`DesignTokens.DarkTertiaryText`, #99FFFFFF), and a text field's placeholder takes it too (`DesignTokens.PlaceholderTextFor`, published as `TextControlPlaceholderForeground` and `TextPlaceholderColorBrush`): a placeholder sits on the field's own fill, #393939 on a dark card, where Wpf.Ui's measured 4.36:1 and white at 60% composites to #B0B0B0 (5.3:1). A placeholder is an example of what the box takes - \"e.g. mail.example.com\", from example.com and example.net - and never the field's name or what an empty box means, which are its caption's. High Contrast keeps the theme's own.\n\n**Focus** is a colour of its own, the violet the Control Deck and the webmail declare as `--focus` and `--focus-glow`: while a text box, password box, number box, combo box or date picker has the keyboard - clicked or tabbed into alike - `FocusGlow` draws a two-pixel ring in `AppFocusBrush` inside its edge, over its border, and a glow outside it that fades over six pixels. It is an adorner, so it keeps the field's size and corner radius and moves nothing, and it is one class handler for every field rather than a template per control. It is drawn on the adorner layer outside any scroll viewer the field is in - a viewer's own layer is clipped to its viewport, and cut the glow off at the sides of every dialog's fields - and clips itself to what of the field is in view, the glow going past only an edge that is (`DesignTokens.FocusGlowBounds`). The theme's own line under a focused field (`TextControlFocusedBorderBrush`, `ComboBoxBorderBrushFocused`) takes the same violet, which is what a field drawn as an underline - the search palette's - s",
         "hows. It is not drawn under High Contrast, where the theme's focus visuals and colours stand, nor on a disabled field or one with no box - a box is a border on the left, top and right, the bottom being free to be the theme's accent line, as Wpf.Ui's date picker's is; a read-only field takes the keyboard and is ringed; a field whose binding reports a validation error, or whose `FieldRow` carries a message (`FieldRow.HasError`), keeps the danger colour for its ring, with the violet glow outside it. The ring fades in over 120 ms unless Windows has animations off. **Every other control** that keeps a focus visual - a button, a check box, a radio button, a tab, a grid's cell, the tree - draws one ring, the same colour, two pixels wide and two pixels outside it (`Services/AccessibleFocus`, under WPF's focus visual key and under WPF-UI's own, `DefaultControlFocusVisualStyle`, so that WPF-UI's one-pixel rectangle in the text colour is gone), and the navigation's and the lists' rows draw it inside themselves, two pixels wide, their neighbours leaving no room outside, as a tab does (`AppFocusVisualInset`), whose strip's scroller cuts what is drawn outside it; under High Contrast the two-tone ring, a black line under white dashes, stands, which is visible on any colour the user chose. The focus was drawn four ways (review finding F296). `DesignTokens.Focus(theme, system)` is the table, and `DesignTokensTests` holds the ring to 3:1 beside every surface and its glow, and the web pages' copies to its values.\n\n**A text field at rest** is found by its bottom edge: the text box, password box, number box and date picker draw their border in `TextControlElevationBorderBrush`, which `ThemeTokens` republishes on the application's own dictionary as Fluent's own shape - the theme's subtle stroke round the top and sides, one pixel of the theme's strong stroke along the bottom (`DesignTokens.TextFieldStroke`: black at 45% in the light theme, white at 55% in the dark) - so that the bottom edge clears 3:1 against the card, the page, a dialog and the field's own fill, where Fluent's own outline measured 1.1:1 and its bottom edge 1.5 to 1.7:1, and an empty field was a faint band on its card. The date picker, which WPF-UI draws with the buttons' border and no bottom edge, takes the text fields' border on all four sides from an implicit style in `App.xaml`. A focused field keeps its accent line and `FocusGlow`'s ring, a disabled one the theme's faint border, and under High Contrast the key is taken away again so that the theme's own border stands. `ContrastTokensTests` holds the bottom edge to 3:1 on every surface a field sits on.\n\n**Selection** is one language across the application's grids and lists: the selected row of every `DataGrid`, every `ListBox` and every `ListView` - the Ctrl+K palette's results, the public folders, the dialogs' member lists, the four lists on the domains page - takes `AppSelectionFillBrush` behind it and the sidebar's 3 x 16 `AppBrandBrush` pill at its left edge, and keeps its own text: the fill is the brand at the notice tint's opacity, so near the card that the primary and secondary text clear 4.5:1 on it, which `DesignTokensTests` holds them to. The sidebar and the rail keep the theme's subtle grey behind the row they mark with the pill - the Fluent navigation convention, where the pill alone tells that row from a hovered one - so what the lists share with the shell is the pill; the tint is the lists' own, and `SelectionLanguageTests` holds the shell to its grey so that this paragraph stays true. In a list or a grid, hover is the subtle grey alone, so a hovered row and the selected one never look the same. The keyboard's row, in a list, wears a 2-pixel ring in `AppFocusBrush` in place of the stock focus visual; a grid keeps the stock visual on its focused cell, which is the one focus ring. Under High Contrast the fill is nothing and `AppSelectionOutlineBrush` draws a one-pixel outline in the highlight round the row, as the pi",
         "ll is. `DesignTokens.Selection(theme, system)` is the table; the implicit `DataGridRow`, `ListBoxItem` and `ListViewItem` styles in `App.xaml` draw it, and a list that gives its rows a style of its own bases it on the application's row and changes no more than the row's inset, height floor and gap - the live log's lines are the row made dense - since a style based on nothing takes the system's row: the live log's did until 9 October 2026, Windows' blue hover and selection and its grey selection out of focus, on which the dark log lines measured 3.8 to 3.9:1 selected and 2.9 to 3.0:1 once the list lost the keyboard. `ContrastTokensTests` fails a list item style with no `BasedOn`, in XAML or in code. A list row's content starts where a grid cell's does, ten pixels in and past the pill, in a `ListView` as in a `ListBox`; a page whose rows carry an inset of their own narrows the row's in its own resources, as the domains page does, never in `App.xaml`, where the domains page's six pixels once put the incoming relays' bare rows three pixels from the pill. Before this, a selected grid row was the hover grey (1.08:1 against its neighbours), the domains page's lists drew WPF-UI's grey with an outline, and the palette and the bare list boxes drew its solid accent band, on which the palette's own text measured 1.9 to 3.5:1.\n\nThe rule that goes with the palette: **a status is never carried by colour alone.** `StatusSemantics.For(level)` gives every level a colour key, a fill and fill-text key for a pill, a shape (`ShapeMarkVisuals` draws it) and a word, and the components below draw all three and put the word first in their accessible name. A page that paints a value in a status colour adds the shape and the word itself, as the dashboard's queue badge does.\n\nA list page's **destructive action** - Delete, Remove, Delete selected, Revoke, Take the role away, and the delete on a row, whether it is the word or the trash glyph - and a button that deletes, clears or replaces in bulk - Restore defaults, Remove expired, Clear greylisting triplets, Empty mailbox, Erase traces - is a secondary button with its words in the danger fill-text colour while it can be pressed and the theme's disabled grey while it cannot: the `DangerTextButton` style in `App.xaml`, or `ButtonStyles.DangerText(button)` for a button built in code. A style and not a local `Foreground`, because a local red outranks the button template's disabled state and left a disabled Delete looking live, and because the danger colour itself is measured against the page: as a local colour on a button's fill it measured 3.3 to 3.5:1 in the dark theme. An inactive domain or account on the domains page dims its glyph alone and says *Inactive* in a Normal `StatusPill` after its name, which takes the secondary text; a whole row at 0.55 made the word the least legible text on the page. The filled `Danger` appearance is the weight of the act itself, not of the button that leads to it: a confirmation's destructive choice, and the backup page's *Start restore* and *Restore the mailbox*, which are the restore. It is drawn from the tokens, `AppDangerSolidBrush` with `AppOnDangerSolidBrush` on it, by an implicit style on WPF-UI's button in `App.xaml` that changes nothing but that appearance, so a button needs `Appearance=\"Danger\"` and nothing else; disabled, it takes the theme's disabled fill and words as any button does. **Every question before an act** is `Dialogs.Confirm`: the question names the thing acted on and what follows (`Services/Confirmations` holds the longer ones, one whole sentence per case), the button names the act - *Delete account*, *Revoke key*, *Release* - with the verb the question asks with, in English and in every catalogue, so that a Russian *\xd0""\xa3""\xd0""\xb4""\xd0""\xb0""\xd0""\xbb""\xd0""\xb8""\xd1""\x82""\xd1""\x8c""* (delete) is not asked over the button *\xd0""\xa3""\xd0""\xb1""\xd1""\x80""\xd0""\xb0""\xd1""\x82""\xd1""\x8c""* (remove) - and the other button is Cancel or says what declining does (*Later*, *Keep working*); a destructive act is drawn in the danger appearance and never takes Enter, and two k",
         "inds of act that destroy nothing give Enter to the decline as well: one that cannot be taken back - a release, a sendout - and one that stops the server's services, so that every open session drops - a pause, a restart, a reinitialise, an update's install - whether the person pressed for it or it is offered after a save (`ConfirmationPatternTests` holds each of them). No page asks with Yes and No, or OK and Cancel (`ConfirmationPatternTests`).\n\n## Components\n\nAll in `hMailServer.ControlPanel.Views.Scaffold`; import it with `xmlns:scaffold=\"clr-namespace:hMailServer.ControlPanel.Views.Scaffold\"` in XAML or `using hMailServer.ControlPanel.Views.Scaffold;` in code (a file that also imports `Wpf.Ui.Controls` will find `Card` ambiguous; qualify it). Each is a control with a template in `Scaffold.xaml`, so it works in XAML and in code alike; each is never a tab stop itself; an empty string set on a text slot is the same as null and collapses the slot; and each is an element of its own in the automation tree with the name given below - a bare WPF `Control` has no automation peer, and a card built from one would reach a screen reader as loose text.\n\n| Component | What it is | Public surface |\n|---|---|---|\n| `PageHeader` | The top of every page: the title with an optional status pill beside it, the page's help button and the page's actions on the right, and an optional subtitle on a line of its own under them, across the header's width up to `AppSubtitleWidth` (840 pixels). The actions go under the subtitle, from the left, where the title would not fit beside them whole (`Services/PageHeaderLayout`). It is the page's level-1 heading. | `PageKey` (string: the page's navigation key, which sets `Title` to the page's title in `NavigationMap` - a page names its key and not its title, and `PageTitlesTests` holds every page to it), `Title`, `Subtitle` (string); `Status`, `Actions` (object slots); `TourId` (string, null for no help button - the shell sets it as it opens a page, from `TourCatalog`); `SubtitleInToolTip` (bool: the subtitle as the title's tool tip, which a page that pins its header above content that scrolls sets below 600 pixels of page, `PageHeaderLayout.SubtitleInToolTip` - the settings pages that ServerSettingsView builds, whose notice is above the first card of the tab on screen and scrolls with it); event `ShowTour`, which bubbles to the shell. Automation: Text, HeadingLevel1, Name = Title, HelpText = Subtitle; the help button is `page-help`. |\n| `Card` | The surface a page groups things on: an optional title and description above the content, an optional footer under a hairline, the card tokens for surface, border, radius and padding. Without a title it is the plain surface the old `Card` Border style gives. | `Title`, `Description` (string); `Footer` (object); `Content`; `Padding` (default `AppCardPadding`). Automation: Group, Name = Title. |\n| `Toolbar` | The row above a grid: a search box, a slot for filters beside it, a slot for the actions on the right. The actions are given the width that is left, so a `WrapPanel` of actions wraps in a narrow window rather than running off its edge, and once the widest of them does not fit there they go on a row of their own under the search and the filters, at the right edge; the filters go under the search, from the left, where the two are wider than the toolbar; actions that cannot wrap count as one (`Services/ToolbarRows`). | `SearchText` (string, two-way), `SearchPlaceholder` (string; also the box's accessible name), `ShowSearch` (bool); `Filters`, `Actions` (object slots); event `SearchTextChanged`; `FocusSearch()`. Automation: ToolBar. |\n| `EmptyState` | What a grid shows with nothing in it: a glyph, one sentence saying so and what to do, the action that does it. Shown in the grid's place; a polite live region, announced as it is shown and as its sentence changes. | `Icon` (`SymbolRegular`, `Empty` for none), `Text` (string); `Action` (object). Automation: Text, Name = Text. ",
         "|\n| `LoadingState` | An indeterminate ring and a line, centred where the data will be; a polite live region, announced as it is shown and as its text changes. | `Text` (string, default *Loading\xe2""\x80""\xa6""*). Automation: Text, Name = Text. |\n| `InlineNotice` | A notice in the flow of a page at one of the status levels: the mark, the severity word (collapsed at `Normal`, where the level says nothing and the word would read as a status that is not one), the sentence, room for an action; the level's colour as a tint behind and an outline around. Polite live region, announced as its text or level changes and as it comes into view; set `AutomationProperties.LiveSetting=\"Assertive\"` on one that must interrupt. | `Level` (`StatusLevel`, default Information), `Text` (string); `Content` (the action). Automation: Text, Name = \"Word: text\". |\n| `StatusPill` | A status as a filled, rounded box: the level's fill, and its mark and a short word in the fill-text shade; no outline except under High Contrast. The word ellipsizes when the pill is squeezed. | `Level` (`StatusLevel`), `Text` (string). Automation: Text, Name = \"Word: text\". |\n| `FieldRow` | One field of a form: the caption (body text, 14 px in the primary colour as a check box's caption is, with its Alt key, wired to the editor through `Mnemonic`), the editor, an optional validation message directly under the editor, drawn with the danger colour, a cross and the words - an assertive live region, announced as the message is set - and an optional hint under that, small and tertiary. While the message is set the editor is marked refused (`FieldRow.HasError`): its border at rest is `AppDangerBrush`, and `FocusGlow` rings it in that colour; an edit of the value takes the message away. The editor takes its accessible name from the caption and its help text from the hint and the message. Fields beside each other on one line - an inline Add form - share their captions' height through `LabelGroup` (the panel sets `Grid.IsSharedSizeScope`, and the button beside them sits under a grid row of the same `SharedSizeGroup`), so that their boxes line up and a message under one moves none of the others. A dialog whose fields can be refused calls `KeepRoomForFieldErrors(lines)` after its frame - a line for each field a press can refuse, or for each line its longest message wraps to - and keeps that many message lines of room under its body, so that a message does not move its footer (a dialog that fits its tabs to a height of their own, `DialogFields.FitTabs` or `FillTabs`, needs none: a message inside a tab does not change that height); the room follows the body - taken again from its height without its messages whenever that changes - so that a row shown or collapsed grows or shrinks the dialog as before, and with the rows that can be refused named (`KeepRoomForFieldErrors(1, subjectRow_)`) it is kept only while one of them is shown. | `Label`, `Hint`, `Error` (string; null clears the message), `LabelGroup`; `Content` (the editor). Automation: Group, Name = the caption. |\n| `SettingsSection` | A section of a settings page: a heading, a description, the fields. A level-2 heading under the page's level-1. Named SettingsSection because System.Windows.Documents has a Section and a dozen views import that namespace. | `Heading`, `Description` (string); `Content`. Automation: Group, HeadingLevel2, Name = Heading. |\n| `TourOverlay` | The ring a tour draws round the control a step points at, and the card beside it with the sentence, *Step n of m*, Back, Next (or Finish on the last stop) and Skip. Built in code rather than templated, because it is not laid out in a page but floats over one; it is a `Canvas` with no background, so it is hit-testable only where the card is. | `ShowStep(target, tourName, position, sentence, canGoBack, lastStep)`, `Hide()`, `Reposition()`, `FocusCard()`; `Showing`, `HasFocusWithin`; events `Next`, `Back`, `Skip`. Automation: the card is a polite live region named \"tour. position. sentence",
         "\". |\n| `DialogFrame` | The shape of a dialog's content: heading and description, a body that scrolls past the dialog's maximum height, a footer with a note on the left and the primary button before the secondary. | `Heading`, `Description` (string); `Body`, `Footer`, `PrimaryButton`, `SecondaryButton` (object). Automation: Pane, Name = Heading. |\n\nA **live region announces nothing by itself.** `AutomationProperties.LiveSetting` is a promise: a screen reader says the region's name when the element raises `LiveRegionChanged`, and until 8 October 2026 nothing in the Control Panel raised it, so every notice, loading state, empty state, field error and result line was silent. `Services/Announce.cs` is the one place the event is raised: `Announce.Request(element)` queues it at Loaded priority - after the layout that shows the element, so that a text set while the element was collapsed and the Visible that follows in the same handler are one announcement - and raises it only while the element is visible, is a live region and has a name, and only when an automation client is listening. The three components above and `FieldRow`'s message call it themselves; a page that builds a live region of its own from a `TextBlock` calls it where it sets the text, and gives the line no fixed accessible name, since the name is what is announced and what a reader says when moved to the line (`LiveRegionTests` holds every such line to both).\n\nThe dialogs' frame is reached through `FluentDialogWindow`, the base class of every dialog: `UseFrame(heading, body, primary, secondary, description = null, width = DesignTokens.Dialog.DefaultWidth)` builds a `DialogFrame`, makes it the window's content, gives Enter to the primary button and Escape to the secondary (or to closing the dialog when there is none), sizes the window to the content at that width within `DesignTokens.Dialog` (the height capped at 0.9 of the work area, the body scrolling past it) and puts the keyboard in the first field of the body on load. `SizeToContentWithin(width)` and `FocusFirstFieldOnLoad(root)` are its two halves, for a dialog that keeps its own layout. All eighteen dialogs are on the frame since 15 September 2026, each at a width `DesignTokens.Dialog` names - one of its four, or the domain and account dialogs' `ManyTabsWidth` - and as tall as its content within its bounds; the IP range and route dialogs start from `WideWidth` and widen to their tab strips (`WidthForTabs`, below). `Views/Dialogs.cs`, the themed message boxes, and `Views/NavigationPalette.cs` are on it too. A message box is as tall as its message: the message at the top of the body as a notice at its level - a question as a notice at Normal, its mark and no word, so that every box says what it is - or as body text for a bare message, and `SizeToContentWithin` takes away the 320-pixel floor WPF-UI's window style gives every window, which drew a box of one line 320 tall with its notice stretched into a block of tint (review findings F041, F296). A dialog whose footer holds Close alone - the folder permissions, the group members and a list's recipients, as the keyboard shortcuts and the message source - draws it Primary, the dialog's one, its lists' Add a secondary button, and gives it no Enter. Three helpers live in `Views/DialogFields.cs` beside them rather than in the base class, which is where they belong and where they should move: `FitTabs` sizes a tabbed dialog to its tallest tab, `FitManyTabs` does the same for the two dialogs of many tabs - the domain and account dialogs - within `DialogLayout.ManyTabsCap` (640 pixels, the strip included) and no lower than `DialogLayout.ListTabFloor` (360), and `WidthForTabs` widens a dialog of a few tabs to what its headers need in the language in force (`DialogLayout.WidthForStrip`, up to the dialog token's 900). Each measures each tab's content on its own - selecting a tab and measuring the `TabControl` reports the tab before it, because a control outside a visual tree re",
         "aches its content presenter a pass later, and that under-measurement hid the whole add-row of one dialog's address tab until a render caught it - counts the strip from its row of headers as well as from the control, gives the control the window's font before measuring (measured in the system's default font, the headers' row came out two pixels short and the tallest tab of the IP range and route dialogs scrolled by those two pixels), and rounds up, with four pixels for the layout rounding the window does at 150 % and the measure cannot. The domain and account dialogs are 800 pixels wide (`ManyTabsWidth`), where 640 showed six or seven of their seventeen and sixteen headers, and they took the whole height the work area allowed without measuring anything until 10 October 2026: 1,180 pixels on a large screen, the domain's General tab three fields above 780 pixels of nothing and Save at the foot of the screen (review findings F193, F194). A tab's fields scroll in `DialogFields.Scroll`, and the frame's body in `DialogFrame`'s own viewer; both keep clear of the scroll bar, which WPF-UI draws over the content, by `Services/ScrollBarInset` - twelve pixels on the right while the bar shows and none while the content fits, so that a tab that does not scroll ends its fields where the strip and the footer end - where the bar covered the right border of every box, the end of hint lines and half of the DKIM tab's *Browse\xe2""\x80""\xa6""* (F192, F073).\n\n**A button with nothing round it** - the breadcrumb's Back, Forward and groups, the Related links and their More, the command bar's sidebar, theme, help and administrator buttons - takes the `SubtleButton` style in `App.xaml`: WPF-UI's Transparent appearance with no border in any state, no fill while it is disabled, its glyph or words dimmed alone, and the theme's subtle fill under the pointer. Transparent alone keeps the control's one-pixel outline, and the trail and the Related links read as a row of chips; and WPF-UI fills a disabled button, which the dark theme's page shows, so that the breadcrumb's Forward, with no page to go forward to, was a grey box beside a Back with nothing round it (review finding F296).\n\nThe **tab strip** - every `TabControl`, through its template in `App.xaml` - is one row that scrolls rather than wraps, with a chevron at each end while it overflows (`Services/TabStripScrolling`). It moves by whole headers (`Services/TabStripSnap`): a chevron puts the first header cut or out of sight at the right at the strip's left edge, or goes back by as many whole headers as fit; a wheel notch is one header; a header given the keyboard or clicked is brought in by a whole-header step; and each chevron is live while a header is cut or out of sight on its side. By pixels the strip began \"\xe2""\x80""\xa6"" ing and disclaimer\" and ended \"Tagg\" (review findings F193, F107). An edge that still cuts a header - the right one as a rule, the left only at the strip's very end - fades over `TabStripSnap.FadeWidth` (24 pixels) through an opacity mask on the strip, never a colour laid over it, which would have to be the colour of whatever the strip is on. `TabStripSnapTests` holds the arithmetic and `DialogFitTests` the dialogs.\n\n`FillPanel` is the one panel in the namespace and has no template: a page's parts one under another, the parts marked `scaffold:FillPanel.Fill=\"True\"` - the page's lists, or the cards that hold them - taking the height the others leave in the window, down to their floors, below which the panel is taller than the window and the page scrolls. Every other part is given the height it asks for, so nothing on the page is ever cut off where scrolling cannot reach it. Several filling parts share the room by `scaffold:FillPanel.Weight` (1 unless set), as a grid's star rows share theirs, none of them below its floor. A filling part with a `MinHeight` is floored at it and measured at the height it is given, so that a list in it realises the rows it shows; the suppression list's titled list card is one, at 18",
         "1 pixels (`FillPanel.TitledListCardFloor`: its title, and three rows or an empty state of a glyph and a sentence), and the audit trail's untitled one at 157 (`FillPanel.ListCardFloor`). An empty state that also carries its action (review finding F207) needs more than that, and a fixed floor cut the action off in a short window: such a list card - the IP ranges, routes, TCP/IP ports, TLS certificates and quarantine pages', the generic list editor's and the domain white and black list's - is floored at what it holds, its grid the filling part of a `FillPanel` of its own at the grid's rows (`FillPanel.ListRowsFloor`, the column headers, three rows and the eight pixels a horizontal scroll bar draws over the last; four rows for the certificates) and the empty state laid over that panel, as the shape described below. Where layout rounding makes such a part ask for more than it was given - at 150 % scaling a `MinHeight` of 157 is 236 device pixels, 157.33 - it is floored at what it asks for (`PageFill.MeasuredFloor`), so that the parts under it are not drawn a device pixel too high. A filling part without one is floored at the height it asks for when nothing limits it and stretched to its share, which is the shape for a card that holds a toolbar, a list and a form: the card's content is a `FillPanel` of its own whose filling part is the list, with a `MinHeight` of three rows and a pixel for layout rounding, which at 125, 150 or 175 % can take one off (and two more on a `ListView`, whose template keeps one above and one below its rows; with fewer either holds two whole rows) - on a one-part `FillPanel` around the list where an `EmptyState` is laid over it - so that the card's floor is its toolbar, its form and its list's rows, and rises with them when the form wraps or the empty state's sentence needs more than the rows. The outermost `FillPanel` goes inside the page's `ScrollViewer` with `ViewportHeight=\"{Binding ViewportHeight, ElementName=PageScroll}\"`, which is the only way a panel measured inside a scroll viewer learns how high the window is; one inside a filling part needs no binding, since it is given its height when it is arranged. It replaces two shapes that each lost a page something (work item 79): the list in a grid's star row between rows sized to their content, which a window shorter than those rows gives no height at all, and a grid held to the viewport over a fixed `MinHeight`, which knows nothing of how tall the parts are and, in the narrowest window, cut off the IP ranges page's add form, the domains page's alias form and, in German, the rules page's last action button, and left the domains page's alias list and the rules page's actions no height. The domains page fills two columns, the domains card one and the accounts card and the row of alias and list cards the other, three to two as its grid's rows were; the rules page fills with its list of rules and the row of criteria and actions, one to 1.3. `Services/PageFill.cs` is the arithmetic, held by `PageFillTests`.\n\n`FormColumnPanel` is the column a settings page's cards stand in - the hMailServer.ini and database pages, each tab of a page of tabs, Directory authentication and synchronisation, Backup, restore & migration, and the pages of a form or of text, Server sendout, Diagnosing stalled mail, REST API keys and About: a vertical stack as wide as it is allowed to be, the page's width up to the form width (`AppFormWidth`), whatever its cards hold, so that every card of a page and of each of its tabs ends at one right edge; a `StackPanel` aligned left with a `MaxWidth` was as wide as its widest card's content, and the cards' right edges jumped from tab to tab, 761 pixels on one tab and 526 on the next (review finding F295). `Services/FormColumn.cs` is its arithmetic, held by `FormColumnTests`: never narrower than its content, and its content's width where nothing limits it.\n\nParts side by side keep a minimum width, and go one above the other where they would not (`S",
         "ervices/SideBySide.cs`, held by `SideBySideTests`): the domains page's columns, which need 280 and 400 pixels and share the width two to three, so that below a page of 700 pixels the domains card goes above the accounts card in the one `FillPanel`; its alias and list cards, 270 each (280 until the sidebar became 310 pixels at 1280, which left them 273); and the rules page's criteria and actions, 340 each, the IF caption and the 230-pixel match list. Side by side in the narrowest window (760 by 520) the domains page's columns were 268 and 232 pixels wide, *Create account* ran off its card and the alias and list cards were 104 and 116, and the rules page's match list ran off its card there and in the account dialog's Rules tab. A row of a list built from a `ListView` is the list's width, never wider (`ScrollViewer.HorizontalScrollBarVisibility=\"Disabled\"`), its name in `GridTrimmedText`, so that a long name ends in an ellipsis, whole in its tool tip, and the row's buttons stay on it: scrolled sideways, one long domain name put every domain's *Properties* and *Delete* off the list's right edge. Where a form's buttons do not fit beside its box, they go on a line of their own under it, as the domains page's *Generate* and *Create account* do below a password box of 120 pixels. In that page's one column the domains list is floored at the rows it shows, as they are drawn, up to 25 (`Services/WholeRowsFloor`, a row being its container and the margin the container keeps), so that the page alone scrolls, where at a floor of three rows the fourth domain needed a scroll bar of the list's own inside the page's; and each of its four lists keeps its rows clear of its own scroll bar while it shows one (`ScrollBarInset.ForList`), where the bar was drawn over the rows' delete buttons (review finding F101).\n\nGrids are styled implicitly in `App.xaml`: the `DataGrid` chains to WPF-UI's own and has no grid lines, column headers only, rows at least `AppNavRowHeight` tall and a full-row selection; the row, cell and header styles are the ones the grids already had (a hairline under each row, the hover grey, semibold low-key headers), the row's selection being the one described under *Selection* above. A grid built in code for a dialog's results - the Active Directory picker's - is a read-only `DataGrid` on the same styles, its columns sharing the width in stars through `GridStyles.Trimmed`, never a `ListView` with a `GridView`, which takes WPF's stock look and not the theme's. A number column sets `ElementStyle=\"{StaticResource GridNumberText}\"` and `HeaderStyle=\"{StaticResource GridNumberHeader}\"`, which right-align its cells and its header. A grid's two scroll bars must not depend on each other: where its rows fit only without a horizontal bar and layout rounding makes its columns a fraction of a pixel wider than the grid, as it can at 125 and 175 %, the bars take turns for ever, the page's layout never settles and the Control Panel takes no more input, as the rules page's lists did. A grid of one column whose text ends in an ellipsis does not scroll sideways (`ScrollViewer.HorizontalScrollBarVisibility=\"Disabled\"`), and one whose columns keep their widths and scroll sideways keeps its vertical bar (`ScrollViewer.VerticalScrollBarVisibility=\"Visible\"`), as the rules page's do.\n\nA list page's list answers the **keyboard** through `Views/GridKeys`: Enter and F2 open the selected row - its Edit, Properties, permissions or source - Delete deletes it with the question the page's Delete asks, and Insert adds a row, or goes to the page's add form where the form is on the page; a double-click opens a row and counts only on a row, never on a column header's divider or a button inside a row. Each key presses the page's own button for the verb, so a disabled button is not pressed by its key, and the keyboard goes back to the selected row once the list is read again. A key from a control inside a row - a button, a box - is that control's. The quarantine's grid ",
         "is one tab stop, whose selected row answers Space (check), R (release), Delete and P or Enter (preview; Enter folds a group), the row icons' tool tips naming the keys and the keys being their `AcceleratorKey`; the domains page's lists keep their own wiring, which adds a context menu (`DomainsView.Rows.cs`). A form laid out on a page answers like a dialog through `Views/FormSubmit`: Enter in any of its boxes presses its button - Add, Create account, Query, Run diagnostics - unless the box takes Enter itself (several lines, an open list), and Escape empties the boxes the page names, never one that holds a default such as a port's 25, and only when there is something to empty. `GridKeysTests` holds both helpers and every page's wiring.\n\nA row's **fact that a column cannot hold** - why a message was held, what the receiving server answered for a suppressed address, an audit row's whole detail, whether a traced event can be followed - is never in its tool tip alone, which a keyboard user never sees: `Views/RowDetailStrip` puts it in a compact card under the list, titled with what it is and in `TextBody`, shown while a row with the fact is selected and collapsed otherwise. It is a part of the page's scrolling `FillPanel` after the list's filling card, so that it never takes the list's three rows in a short window. A row says itself to a screen reader as one statement, `Services/RowSpeech`: its cells that have something in them, a bare value with its column's name; `RowSpeechTests` holds every class a list's columns are bound to to a `ToString` of its own.\n\n## List pages\n\nEvery page whose subject is a list has one shape since 10 October 2026 (review finding F288; there were four layouts of the actions and five ways to show a count): the collection lists (SURBL servers, DNS block lists, spam exemptions, blocked senders, the allow and block list, the greylisting exemptions and bypass list, blocked attachments, remote domains, server messages) and Groups, routes, TCP/IP ports, IP ranges, TLS certificates, rules, the delivery queue, the suppression list, allowed and blocked domains, the audit trail, the message trace, the quarantine and public folders. `ListPagePatternTests` holds them to it.\n\n- **The header** (`PageHeader`) carries the title, the subtitle and, beside the title, the count as a Normal `StatusPill`: \"4 routes\" through `Loc.Plural`, \"Active: 2 of 3\" where the list counts the entries that are on (`ActiveEntries`), \"Shown: 2 of 40\" while a search is typed in over the rows the page read (`Services/ListSearchCount`; the quarantine's counts the messages the server listed, which are what its search looks at), \"1 to 100 of 1284\" for a list read a page at a time; a list whose search asks the server - the suppression list, the audit trail - counts what the server found. A read that failed hides the pill with the empty state (`Services/ReadFailure`). The pill is a polite live region (`AutomationProperties.LiveSetting`), which announces itself as it changes and as it comes into view (`StatusPill`, through `Services/Announce`), so that a screen reader hears the count after a search or a page step as it heard the Information notices that said it before (WCAG 2.2 success criterion 4.1.3, Status Messages). On the right are the page's own verbs, which act on no row - *Digest settings* - and *Refresh*, which on the message trace runs the last search or the followed message again and cannot be pressed before the first.\n- **The toolbar** (`Toolbar`) over the list carries the search box, the filters beside it - *Sending domain*, *Group by* - and the list's verbs on the right: the selected row's (*Edit* or *Properties*, *View source*, *Permissions*), then *Delete* or *Remove* in `DangerTextButton`, then those that act on the whole list (*Restore defaults*, *Remove expired*), then *Add\xe2""\x80""\xa6""*, the Primary, at the right edge. In a narrow window they wrap, and go under the search where the widest does not fit beside it; the filters go under the search ",
         "where the two do not fit side by side.\n- **The search filters as it is typed in**, over every column the list shows (`Services/ListSearch`), and a search that hides every entry says so in the empty state - \"No entry in this list has \"qqzz\" in it.\" - without the action that adds a first one, which comes back as the search is cleared. The XAML lists do the filter, the count and the empty state in one call after every read that worked and every keystroke (`Services/ListSearchState`); the collection lists, the allowed and blocked domains, the public folders and the quarantine, which filter their rows themselves, count in the same words, and the public folders' and the quarantine's no-match sentences say what their search reads (\"No public folder has \"qqzz\" in its name.\"). Where the list is a query to the server - the suppression list, the audit trail - it runs once typing pauses (`Services/SearchDelay`, 300 ms) and at once on Enter. The message trace's address box is a query form whose *Search* button stands beside it.\n- **One way to add.** Either the toolbar's *Add\xe2""\x80""\xa6""*, which opens the page's dialog, or - where an entry is a handful of fields, as a route, a port, an IP range, a certificate and a rule are - a titled form under the list (*Add route*) whose button is the page's Primary; never both. The empty state's action and Insert lead to that one way.\n- **Words, not glyphs**, on the header's and the toolbar's buttons, as on every other button; a glyph belongs to a row's 24-pixel button, named for its row, and to an empty state. The collection lists and the delivery queue drew a glyph on each.\n- **A count is never a notice**, a caption under the list or the subtitle's sentence: \"3 addresses listed.\", \"5 messages held.\" and \"Entries that match the search: 1 of 4.\" were Information notices, \"4 public folders.\" a caption under the list, and \"4 messages waiting for delivery.\" and \"4 rules of the server\" subtitles. A notice says a state with a consequence - a list longer than the page reads (\"showing the 200 most recent\"), a switch that keeps the list from applying - or the outcome of an act.\n\nThe domains page is master and detail, four lists in one page, and keeps its own shape: a search over each of its two large lists, its rows' 24-pixel buttons and its forms at the top of their cards. A list embedded in a dialog's tab (the domain's aliases, the account's external accounts) has no header: its toolbar carries the count where the filters go, and *Refresh* before *Add*.\n\n## Rules\n\n- **No new text without its seventeen translations.** Every caption is looked up by its English text (`Services/Loc.cs`), and `build/check-localisation.py`, `build/check-mnemonics.py` and `build/check-catalogues.py` fail the build on a caption missing from any catalogue, on a caption without an Alt key or with a colliding one, and on a translation that drops a token the reader types back. The cheapest redesign changes no text; a component's defaults are the only texts the scaffold adds.\n- **No implicit TextBlock style**, for the reason under *Type*.\n- **Brushes by DynamicResource or by key**, never by a held instance, for the reason under *Colour*.\n- **Colour, shape and word** for every status, and the word first in the accessible name.\n- **Every element keeps its automation name.** A `PageHeader` names the page, a `Card` its group, a `FieldRow` its editor; the ids the shell and the pages carry (`nav-<key>`, `breadcrumb-*`, `dashboard-*`) are what `build/capture-cp.ps1` and any automation hold on to and do not change.\n- **A control's name starts with its caption** (WCAG 2.5.3, Label in Name), so that what a screen reader says is what is on screen and voice control can press it by the words on it. A sentence that explains a button is its help text and tool tip (`Views/ControlHelp.Explain`), never its name; a link whose caption says less than where it goes (\"Settings\xe2""\x80""\xa6""\" beside each of ten checks) is named by the caption and then the destination (`ControlHelp.Name",
         "Link`, `Services/LabelInName`); a row's button carries its row after its caption (\"Copy host of the MX record\"). `LabelInNameTests` reads every page and dialog for a name that does not start with its caption.\n- **Every caption has an Alt key, and a verb has one key everywhere.** A field's caption carries one as a button's does (`FieldRow`'s `Label`, `DialogFields.Field`), and its key puts the keyboard in the field's box - for a row of a box and a button, the box (`Mnemonic.Editor`). A verb keeps its key on every page - *Refresh* and *Reload* R, *Remove* M, *Browse\xe2""\x80""\xa6""* B, *Copy* C, *Search* S, *Resume* E, *Restore defaults* F - and another caption on the page moves rather than it. A page whose cards hold more captions than there are letters, as the Backup, restore & migration page does, sets `local:AccessKeyScope.ByCard=\"True\"` on its root: the card that holds the keyboard answers its own keys first, a key it does not have goes to the page as on any other, and so no two captions of one card may share a key and no caption outside the cards may share one with any card's. `build/check-mnemonics.py` holds all of it in English and in every catalogue - field captions in XAML and in code, captions set in code on a XAML page's buttons, a dialog tab's keys against the buttons under every tab, one key per verb, and each card of such a page on its own - and its `--self-test` proves each rule against a page made to break it; `AccessKeysTests` holds the pages.\n- **A caption ends in \"\xe2""\x80""\xa6""\" when its button asks for more before it acts** - opens a dialog for input, as *Add\xe2""\x80""\xa6""*, *Edit\xe2""\x80""\xa6""*, *Properties\xe2""\x80""\xa6""* on the list pages, *Browse\xe2""\x80""\xa6""* and *Change the administrator password\xe2""\x80""\xa6""* do - and a button that only opens another page names the page and has none: *Open Domains*, *Open Setup checklist*, the overviews' *Anti-virus settings \xe2""\x86""\x92""* (review findings F305, F042). A link inside a card that opens a page takes the `CompactPageLink` style (`App.xaml`; `ButtonStyles.CompactPageLink` in code): the Caption size, 28 pixels tall at the least, where seven pages drew their own 24-pixel links beside the card's 32-pixel buttons. A caption with an access key is shown in an `AccessText`, which WPF-UI's implicit style sets to the Body size over the size the button passes down, so the style carries a template for a string caption that sets its `AccessText` to the Caption size, as the check box's carries one that wraps; the Dashboard's *Open Setup checklist* was 14 pixels beside the 12 of the links without a key. `NavigationCaptionTests` holds the captions - every XAML button whose handler opens a dialog that asks for input, a viewer, the second factor of a sign-in and the domains page's row buttons aside - and the style.\n- **One dash and one ellipsis in text.** The dash is the spaced hyphen, \" - \" (the owner's choice of 9 October 2026), and no English text carries an em or an en dash; a range is written \"1-14\". A translation writes the hyphen too, except Russian and Ukrainian, whose typography and catalogues write the em dash, and Czech the en dash, which may write that dash or the hyphen but never both in one text. Progress text (\"Applying\xe2""\x80""\xa6""\"), a caption that opens a dialog and the rest of a list end in the ellipsis character; three dots stand only for omitted literal text the reader types or reads as it is - p=..., ip=..., https://host/..., Bearer .... A lone em dash in a cell or a figure is not a dash but the mark for no value, as the Dashboard's figures show it before the statistics are read. German, Czech and Polish quotations close with their language's own mark. No text pads itself with spaces to line up with the next - \"Type:       TXT\" under \"Host/Name:  {0}\" lined up in no translation, whose words are longer or shorter and whose CJK characters are two columns wide - so a text has one space after its colon and the layout does the aligning, and a join made in code writes \" - \" as the catalogues do. `build/check-catalogues.py` holds all of it in every catalogue (review find",
         "ings F037, F038, F046, F299), and its `--self-test` proves each rule; `PaddedJoinTests` holds the joins in the code.\n- **A choice of one among a few is a radio group**, never a row of buttons that marks the chosen one by its colour: the dashboard's chart range is radio buttons styled on WPF-UI's toggle button, the chosen one drawn as the primary button, in a panel whose automation peer is a named Group, one tab stop whose arrow keys move the choice, each saying its place (\"1 of 4\"); a choice is acted on when it becomes the chosen one, so pressing it again does nothing. `ChartRangeGroupTests` holds it.\n- **One button order** in every row of buttons on a page - a header's actions, a toolbar's and a card's footer: the secondary and the destructive buttons first, then *Refresh* or *Reload*, then the Primary - or the act drawn in the filled Danger appearance, *Restore the mailbox* - at the right edge (review finding F289; the Primary came first on the REST API keys page, the suppression list and the audit trail, in the middle of the delivery queue's and the event scripts' rows, and the backup page's cards changed their order from one to the next). A dialog's footer keeps the Windows order, its primary button before Cancel (`DialogFrame`), and a query form's button stands beside its box. `ButtonOrderTests` holds every row in XAML and in code.\n- **A page's subtitle is two sentences at most**: what the page shows or does. The rest of what it has to say is in the page's help, or beside what it explains - the TCP/IP ports page says where its Listening column's word comes from in that column's header. Beside the actions, the subtitle once ran to ten lines in English and fourteen in German on the greylisting bypass list and pushed the list below the window (review finding F279). `PageSubtitleTests` counts every subtitle's sentences, and lists the six still longer with why.\n- **One page, one `ScrollViewer`, padded with `AppPagePadding`** - except a page of tabs, whose header stays above the strip and whose tabs each scroll under it, so that Save is in view however long a tab is: `ServerSettingsView`'s pages, and a `FeatureSettingsView` section whose cards name a tab (`CardDef.Tab`, framed by `FrameTabs_`) - Transport security's Encryption, Signing, Forwarding and Reports. Only the header and the strip are pinned: the page's notice, and on a `FeatureSettingsView` page the line that names where its settings are kept, are above the first card of the tab on screen and scroll with it, and below 600 pixels of page the subtitle is the title's tool tip (`PlaceTabHead_` and `FitTabbedHeader_` there).\n- **A link that names a page's tab opens the page on that tab**, through `MainWindow.NavigateToTab(key, tab)` with the tab's English header: a signpost row that names its tab (`ElsewhereSetting`), the stalled-mail guide's scanner tests. So does every other way in that knows the tab its subject is on: a setup checklist item (`SetupItem.Tab`, on the checklist and the Dashboard), a Ctrl+K task (`IntentEntry.Tab`) and a Ctrl+K search that finds a page of tabs by an alias (`NavigationMap.AliasTabs`, which names a tab for each alias of Transport security). A link to a page without tabs, or to its first, is `NavigateTo`.\n- **Settings the server reads only while a switch is on follow the switch**: their rows are disabled while it is off - the box greyed, and its caption, its Applies line and its hint in the disabled text colour, so that the row reads as one that cannot be used - and come back as soon as it is ticked. Their values stay as they are and are saved as they are, and a value under a switch that is off is not refused. Where the server uses none of a card's other settings while its switch is off, a note under the switch says so (`Setting.DependsOn` on the hMailServer.ini pages and `DialogFields.EnabledBy` in the dialogs, which disable the row and not its editor, so that what else decides the editor - a key the environment sets - keeps deciding it; the ",
         "ACME and OAuth2 cards, a relay's credentials, a domain's limits).\n- **It is the Control Deck**, never the Web Control Deck or the Deck alone, and a text on a page, in a dialog or on the setup checklist that sends the reader there has the Open the Control Deck button beside it (`Views/ControlDeckLink`: `Button` in a header, `Row` on a card, `DialogRow` under a dialog's note, `SetupFinding.NamesControlDeck` on the checklist), except a text that sends the reader to the Deck on the server itself, which a Control Panel on another machine cannot open (a settings tab's notice for a REST API that listens on the server alone). The button opens the REST listener's root, where the Deck is served, and while the REST API cannot serve it here - off, its listener's settings saved and not yet applied, on the loopback address seen from another machine, or beyond it without a certificate - it is disabled and says why (`Services/ControlDeck`). `ControlDeckTests` holds every English text to the one name and the dialogs' notes to their button.\n- **A tour is never the only way to do anything, and never blocks the product while it runs.** See *Tours* below.\n\n## Words\n\nOne name for one thing, in every text the Control Panel shows, so that a reader who learns a word on one page meets the same word on the next (review findings F143 and F301). The catalogues translate these words once each; a translator who meets a second English word for the same thing writes a second word too.\n\n| Say | Not | Notes |\n|---|---|---|\n| **sign in** (the verb), **sign-in** (the noun), for people and mail clients alike | log on, logon, log in, login | *Logon* stays for Windows alone: Windows logon, `LogonUser`, a Windows account's logon restrictions, the service's *Log on as a service* right. A protocol's own word stays as the protocol spells it (`AUTH LOGIN`), as does a product's (Entra ID's *Login URL*) and a database's (a SQL Server login). Japanese says \xe3""\x82""\xb5""\xe3""\x82""\xa4""\xe3""\x83""\xb3""\xe3""\x82""\xa4""\xe3""\x83""\xb3"", and \xe3""\x83""\xad""\xe3""\x82""\xb0""\xe3""\x82""\xaa""\xe3""\x83""\xb3"" only after Windows. |\n| **mail** or **message** in prose, **email address** for the field | e-mail | The page *Delivery of e-mail* keeps its title until the page names are decided again; the texts that name it say its title. |\n| **smart host** | relayer, relay (for the server-wide host) | The Delivery of e-mail page's *Smart host* tab; a domain's own *Relay host* and a route are relays to a host of their own. |\n| **two-factor authentication** for the scheme, a **second factor** for what is enrolled | | *Two-factor* is the Administrative access page's tab. |\n| **hMailServer.ini**, the file's own name | hMailServer.INI | Its sections and keys as the file writes them: `[LDAP]`, `[Settings] SmtpTarpitCount`. |\n| **server error NNNN** | error NNNN, error HMNNNN | The number the server's log and event log give the condition. |\n| **Could not ...** | Couldn't ... | Whole words in every message. |\n| **client ID**, **key ID** | client id, key id | |\n| **connection security** | security | The setting a port, a route, a domain's relay and the directory share, and the TCP/IP ports grid's column. |\n| **TLS** in prose | SSL | The two pages are *TLS certificates* and *TLS versions & ciphers* since the owner's decision of 12 October 2026, their old titles *SSL certificates* and *SSL/TLS* search aliases, and so are the certificates the first lists and a port's *TLS certificate* box; *SSL/TLS* stays the connection security that encrypts from the first byte, and OpenSSL is a name. |\n\nA field's caption ends with no colon: the row's layout already says that the editor below belongs to it.\n\nA field's caption names the field, and its last bracket says only the unit, the range and what an edge value means, in one form - \"Ban the sending address for (minutes, up to 525,600; 0 = no ban)\", \"Warning text (empty = the server message)\" - since the caption is the editor's accessible name: what the field is for, how to fill it in and what follows from it are its hint, under the box (review findings F105, F148 and F300). `CaptionWordingTes",
         "ts` holds the dialogs' captions to it, and `CaptionFormTests` every caption to the form.\n\nNo word is written in capitals to stress it, in English or in a translation: the sentence carries the stress (\"Applied when a password is chosen - ... - and never when one is checked at sign-in\"), and a risk the reader must not miss is a Warning notice. Capitals are for names - a protocol, a command, a record type, a unit, the rule editor's IF, THEN, (AND) and (OR) - and `EmphasisCapitalsTests` holds every catalogue to them (review finding F297).\n\n## Tours\n\nA tour teaches a page while somebody uses it. One mechanism, the same shape in all three surfaces - this\nControl Panel, the browser Control Deck (`hmailserver/installation/WebAdmin/index.html`) and the webmail\n(`Server/Common/Util/Portal.html` and `Portal.js`) - so that the three can be reasoned about, and read in\nthe store, with one explanation.\n\n**A step** is an element, one sentence, an optional condition that ends the step when the thing has\nhappened, and what to do when the element is not there. The element is named by its automation id in the\nControl Panel and by its element id (or, in the Deck, a stable selector) in the two pages - never by a\nposition, because a step pinned to \"the third button\" survives nothing. A step that cannot find its element\nis **skipped**, unless it is marked as one the rest of the tour is meaningless without, in which case the\ntour ends *saying so*. A step never acts: it does not press the button it points at and it does not fill\nthe field. The only thing it does besides pointing is open the page or view its element lives on, because\nan element on a page nobody has opened is in no visual tree to be found in.\n\n**A tour** is an ordered list of steps with an id, a name and a version. The id is what the finished record\nis kept under, so renaming one abandons every reader's record of having done it; the version is raised when\nthe steps change enough that somebody who finished the old tour has not seen the new one.\n\n**Where it is kept.** Which tours this person has finished, and where they stopped in one they left, as one\nline: `firstrun=1|firstrun@3v1` - the finished tours with the version each was finished at, then the resume\npoints as *step* `v` *version*. The Control Panel keeps it in `HKCU\\Software\\hMailServer\\ControlPanel`\nunder `TourProgress`, beside the window bounds, the palette's history and the sidebar's collapsed state; the\nDeck in `localStorage` under `hmsTours`; the webmail in the account's preferences under `tours`\n(`/api/v1/me/preferences`), so that a reader who starts the walk on a laptop finishes it on a phone. Every\nkind of damage to the stored line is survivable and silent in all three: the worst it can cost is being\noffered a tour twice.\n\n**The rules**, which are the feature rather than decoration:\n\n- **Never modal, and never in the way.** The ring takes no pointer events and the card is the only thing on\n  its layer that does, so every control underneath - the very one being pointed at - stays usable while a\n  tour runs. A tour is never the only way to do anything.\n- **Never takes the keyboard.** Nothing is focused when a step opens, and the focus the tour found is put\n  back when it ends. The card's buttons are ordinary tab stops; `F6` reaches them from anywhere, and\n  `Escape` ends the tour **only** while the keyboard is already inside the card - everywhere else Escape\n  still belongs to the page.\n- **No Alt keys on the card.** The one place in this application where a caption carries no access key. The\n  card floats over whichever page the step is on, so any key it claimed would sooner or later be a key that\n  page already owns, and an access key that does the wrong thing on one page in forty-seven is worse than\n  none.\n- **The highlight is not colour alone.** A solid ring in the accent and a dashed one inside it in the text\n  colour, so the highlight survives greyscale, colour blindness and High Contrast - where the accent becomes\n  ",
         "the system highlight and the dashes are what still separate the ring from an ordinary focus rectangle.\n- **The sentence is announced.** The card is a polite live region and the Control Panel raises a UI\n  Automation notification with the sentence as each step opens, so the tour is usable without sight and\n  without the focus being taken to make that happen.\n- **It is the change that ends a step, not the state.** A step whose condition was already true when the\n  reader arrived stays until they press Next; somebody who already has a domain should read the sentence\n  about domains rather than watch the step vanish before they have read it.\n- **Every ending says something.** Finished, left, or stopped because a step it needed was not on the\n  screen - each is a sentence. Silence after a tour that stopped on its own is how somebody decides the\n  feature is broken.\n- **Every sentence goes through the catalogues**, like every other text: seventeen languages here, twenty in\n  the Deck, twenty-three in the webmail.\n\n**Where the logic lives.** In the Control Panel, everything that can be decided without a window is in\n`Services/Tour.cs` (the steps and the catalogue), `Services/TourSession.cs` (the stepper - which step is\nshowing, what Next and Back do, when and why the tour ended) and `Services/TourProgress.cs` (the record),\nall three compiled into `ControlPanel.Core` and tested without WPF, exactly as `ConnectionStatus` and\n`SettingsPageStatus` are. `MainWindow.Tour.cs` is the half that genuinely needs a window: opening the page a\nstep is on, finding the control by its automation id, keeping the ring on it while the page moves\nunderneath, and answering the conditions from the server. The two browser pages keep the same division by\nconvention rather than by compilation, and `build/deck-script-test.js` and `build/portal-script-test.js`\nexecute theirs.\n\n**The ways in.** A tour is offered on the Welcome page (a button in the header and, until the walk has been\nfinished, a notice above the intents), in the Ctrl+K palette as a row of its own above the page list, and by\na help button in the header of any page a tour visits - which starts that tour at its first stop *on that\npage*, so the button teaches the page it is on. In the Control Panel that button and the Welcome page's are a\nlight bulb (`Lightbulb24`) with *Show me around* beside it, the light bulb alone where the header has no room\nfor the words (`PageHeaderLayout.TourCaptionShows`); the question mark is the command bar's help pane alone,\nsince both were the same glyph 250 pixels apart and did different things (review findings F024, F081; the\nowner's decision of 9 October 2026). Like the card, the header's button carries no Alt key: it is on\nwhichever page a tour visits. The Deck's is the `?` button in its header and the webmail's is *Show me\naround* in the account menu; both behave the same way.\n\n## Migrating a page\n\nThe dashboard (`Views/DashboardView.xaml`) is the pattern for a XAML page and the sign-in page (`Views/ConnectView.xaml`) for a form. A page changes in this order: the `ScrollViewer` takes `AppPagePadding`; the title and subtitle become a `PageHeader` whose `PageKey` names the page (its `Subtitle` is set from code where the page updates it - the dashboard writes the time of the last refresh there); the page's buttons move into the header's `Actions`; every `Border` with the `Card` style becomes a `Card`, with its heading as the `Title`; a state shown as a mark and a word becomes a `StatusPill`; an error line becomes an `InlineNotice`; a label above an editor becomes a `FieldRow`, and the editor loses its placeholder where the caption says the same. A code-built page does the same with `new Card { Title = L(\"...\"), Content = ... }`. Nothing about what the page reads or writes changes, and the tests that read the page's source for its settings (`SettingClaimsTests`, `SettingsSearchIndexTests`) keep passing because the setting keys and labels are not what moves.\n\n## Where ",
         "the migration stands\n\nEvery page is on the frame since 15 September 2026, in three waves after the first: the twelve remaining XAML pages and the code-built pages (wave 2), `ServerSettingsView` with its 31 tabs and 53 cards (wave 3), and `FeatureSettingsView` with all eighteen dialogs, the message boxes and the palette (wave 4). No page title, no tab header, no setting caption and no automation id moved in any of them, which is what kept the search index, the Ctrl+K palette and `SettingClaimsTests`/`SettingsSearchIndexTests` passing throughout.\n\nWhat the waves added beside the components, because a page needed it and a component did not offer it:\n\n* `Services/StatusText.cs` and `Services/GridStyles.cs` - the second because the keyed grid styles are XAML resources and a third of the pages build their columns in C#; `GridStyles.Number(column)` is the C# way to say `GridNumberText`. Both are candidates for the scaffold.\n* `Services/SettingsPageStatus.cs` and `Services/ServerSettingsPageStatus.cs` - the word and the severity of a settings page's pill, per state, by **one save-state model**: no pill while the page shows what it read (*Clean*); *Unsaved changes*, a Warning, from the first edit; and after a save, until the next edit, *Saved* (Good) when every setting written is in effect, *Reinitialise to apply* (Information) when some wait for the window's **Apply now**, or *Restart to apply* (Information) when some wait for a service restart - or the server cannot say, being one from before a save could publish, whose settings apply at its next start ([SettingsApply.md](SettingsApply.md)). A save that wrote nothing says why in a Critical notice - under the header on the INI pages, and on the server settings pages above the first card of the tab on screen, scrolled into view - never in grey caption text or a message box, and the sentence that goes with a state is a notice at its level. Two types because there are two lifecycles: an hMailServer.INI page - the feature settings pages, *Directory authentication (LDAP)* and *Directory synchronisation*, which put *Reload* and *Save changes* in the page header and the file they edit in a caption under it like the rest - may go on to restart the service (*Restarting, RestartFailed, NotConnected, Applied*) or find no file (*Unavailable*), and the server settings page is read and written over COM - and over the REST API for the settings no COM property carries - in a pass that can fail row by row (*Saving, PartlySaved*). The states they share have one word and one level, which `SettingsPageStatusTests` holds; both types are WPF-free and held to a distinct word per state. `Views/IniPageSaveState.cs` keeps the INI pages' pill and notice: the pill follows the page's own `HasUnsavedChanges`, so an edit undone by hand takes it back to what the save said, and it is the same answer the question before leaving the page reads.\n* `Views/DialogFields.cs` - `Field`, the editors, and the two tab-sizing helpers described above.\n* `Services/DialogLayout.cs` - the arithmetic that sizes a tabbed dialog within the work area: its height, the cap of a dialog of many tabs, and the width a strip's headers need.\n\nWhat the waves asked for and did not get, in the order they are worth doing:\n\n1. **`FitTabs`, `FitManyTabs`, `WidthForTabs` and the framed-prompt helper belong in `FluentDialogWindow`.** They are in `DialogFields` only because `UseFrame` is protected and the static prompts build a bare window.\n2. **`Card` has no status slot and no heading level.** `PageHeader` has a status slot; a card that needs one puts the pill in the first row of its content. A settings card *is* a section, so it is a title-less `Card` wrapping a `SettingsSection` to get the level-2 heading - one unnamed group around one named one.\n3. **`SettingsSection` and `FieldRow` carry a default margin** (`AppSectionGap`, `AppFieldGap`) that a caller inside a card has to zero. A default most callers clear is in the wrong place.\n4. **`FieldRow` ca",
         "nnot say \"the check box is its own caption\".** Passing `Label = null` leaves the row's automation group unnamed.\n5. **A few controls keep a width of their own.** The toolbar puts its actions under the search box once the widest does not fit beside it (since 10 October 2026), but a filter is not moved: in a page much narrower than the narrowest window's 512 pixels - the help pane open beside it leaves about 250 - a combo box wider than the width left runs off the toolbar's right edge, as the rules page's scope list does; at that width the rules page's 230-pixel match list is wider than its card, and the IP ranges page's permission boxes are cut at their right edges. Every list and every form can still be scrolled to.\n\nTwo things are deliberately not migrated and are not defects: `AccessibleChartCard` and `AccountTwoFactorPanel` still use the old `Card` Border style, which is why `App.xaml` keeps it - a title-less `Card` draws the same surface, so they are identical on screen (`AppPasswordsPanel` moved to the component on 8 October 2026, with its name in a `FieldRow` and its row actions under its list); and the `LogsView`'s severity palette (`Log*Brush`) is the log viewer's own and is not a design token - its colours are in `DesignTokens.Log(theme, system)` all the same, where `ContrastTokensTests` holds every line to 4.5:1 on the page, the log's card, a line under the pointer and a selected line, the surfaces of the application's list row, which each line of the log is (the dark error, IMAP and application lines are #FF7B72, #BC8CFF and #58A6FF, the light SMTP, application and POP3 lines #116329, #1F62D0 and #8A5C00).\n",
         nullptr
      };

