      const char *const Page101Pieces[] =
      {
         "The service account\n===================\n\nThe Windows service reads mail from anybody on the internet, in C++, and for as\nlong as this project has existed it has done that as LocalSystem, the most\nprivileged account on the machine. A flaw reachable through SMTP, IMAP or POP3\nwas a flaw with full control of the computer. Since 25 September 2026 a **new**\ninstallation runs the service as `NT SERVICE\\hMailServer` instead: the service's\nown virtual account, which Windows makes from the service's name - no password,\nno account to create - and which reaches only the folders it is granted. The\nLinux packages of this same server already run as a user of their own\n(`hmailserver.service`: `User=hmailserver`); this is the Windows half.\n\nWhat this page covers: what the installer asks and does, what the account is granted\nand by what, why nothing the service can write decides that, what the service may no\nlonger write - `hMailServer.INI` - and what became of each thing that wrote it, what\nthe other users of the computer may read of its folders, and change of its programs\n(`/ProtectFolders`), the table of everything LocalSystem could do that the virtual\naccount cannot, the live update's answer, where the Control Panel, the Control Deck\nand the checklist say which account the service runs as, and how to go back.\n\nWhat it does not cover, because it stays declined: an installation under an\nordinary account for shared hosting (discussion #267). The secret store is\nmachine-scope DPAPI (`DataProtector.cpp`, `CRYPTPROTECT_LOCAL_MACHINE`), which\nanyone with administrator rights on the machine can read; the COM registration is\nmachine-wide; and the message store is guarded by file permissions that an\nordinary user cannot set against the other users of the same machine - who, on\nshared hosting, are the other tenants.\n\n## The rule the rest follows\n\nA service that can make an elevated program act on something it chose has given\nback what the account took away. Three elevated programs act on this service's\nbehalf - the installer, `hMailServer.exe /GrantServiceAccess` and\n`hMailServer.exe /Register` - so none of them takes a folder, a file or an\naccount from anywhere the service can write:\n\n* **`hMailServer.INI` is not the service's to write.** It holds `[Database]`,\n  `[Directories]` and `[Security]` - how the server reaches its database, where\n  its folders are, and who may administer it - and the elevated programs read it.\n  The service runs on it read-only (below).\n* **The grant takes its folders from trusted places alone**: where the program\n  is, and a list only administrators may write. A folder the configuration names\n  is reported, never granted, until an administrator approves it after a question\n  that names it.\n* **`/Register` takes the account from its command line alone**, never from\n  `ServiceAccountName`.\n\n## What the installer does\n\nThe installer has a page for it, after the server profile, whenever the server\ncomponent is installed. It says which case applies:\n\n| The machine | The page offers | The default |\n|---|---|---|\n| No hMailServer service yet - a new installation; or the LocalSystem service a new installation left when its database step did not complete (below) | `NT SERVICE\\hMailServer`, or LocalSystem as earlier versions ran it | `NT SERVICE\\hMailServer` |\n| A service running as LocalSystem - an upgrade | keep LocalSystem, or move it to `NT SERVICE\\hMailServer` | keep LocalSystem |\n| A service running as any other account - an upgrade | keep that account, or move it back to LocalSystem | keep the account |\n\nAn upgrade never changes the account unless the page is answered to. A silent\ninstallation takes the same choice as `/serviceaccount=virtual`,\n`/serviceaccount=localsystem` or `/serviceaccount=keep`; without it, a new\ninstallation gets the virtual account and an upgrade keeps what it has. When the\ndatabase is SQL Server reached with Windows authentication, the move page says\nthat the moved service needs a login there, which ",
         "setup gives it first (step 4).\n\nThe order is the point:\n\n1. The service is registered - `hMailServer.exe --service-account <word>\n   /Register` - as **LocalSystem** when it is to be moved (a new installation, or\n   an upgrade of a LocalSystem service that chose the move), and as its chosen or\n   kept account otherwise.\n2. A service that already runs as an account of its own is granted its folders\n   again, for anything this version added.\n3. The database step runs - DBSetupQuick for the built-in database, DBSetup for an\n   external one - and the chosen profile is applied. That step writes `[Database]`\n   into `hMailServer.INI` through the service, over COM, and sets the service's\n   dependency on a local database server; LocalSystem may do both, and the\n   virtual account may do neither.\n4. For a move, when the database is SQL Server reached with Windows\n   authentication, `hMailServer.exe --grant-database-login` gives\n   `NT SERVICE\\hMailServer` a login there, a user in the hMailServer database and\n   `db_owner` (below) - as the administrator running setup, on a connection of its\n   own, while the service is still LocalSystem's. The database step made or opened\n   the database as LocalSystem; the moved service signs in as the virtual account,\n   and without these it cannot open the database. When they cannot be given on a\n   database this installation set up, the service is not moved: it stays on\n   LocalSystem, working, the message gives the statements a SQL Server\n   administrator runs, and setup ends with **10** (Upgrading.md, \"If the upgrade\n   fails\"). Then the service is stopped and `hMailServer.exe /GrantServiceAccess`\n   runs - with `--confirm` in an attended installation. By now the database names\n   every certificate and key file, so the grant can say which are outside the\n   folders it grants. Only when it ends in 0 is the service registered as\n   `NT SERVICE\\hMailServer`. Anything else - a folder nobody approved in a silent\n   installation, a folder that can never be granted, No to the question, a grant\n   that failed - leaves the service on LocalSystem, working, and says why; the\n   installation still succeeds. A database step that failed leaves it on\n   LocalSystem as well, and a new installation's leaves the mark that lets it be\n   finished later (below).\n5. The service is started. A service this installation has just moved is then held\n   to opening its database as `NT SERVICE\\hMailServer` - the database's version is\n   read through it, as the installer reads it for an upgrade - since a service\n   reports itself running before it has opened anything. One that did not start,\n   or did not open its database, is moved back to LocalSystem and started again,\n   and setup says why and ends with 10 (11 when its database step changed the\n   database): until 6.3.6 such a server, which listened on no port, ended setup\n   with 0.\n\nA grant that fails at step 2 fails the installation - that service may not start\n- and says so in its own words, not the database's.\n\n**A new installation whose database step did not complete** - DBSetup refused a\nsetting, the database server refused the sign-in, the wizard was closed - has its\nfiles, a service registered as LocalSystem for the database step, and no database;\nnone of what follows that step has happened: the secure defaults\n([SecureDefaults.md](SecureDefaults.md)), the profile, the stored-secrets key\n([SecretsAtRest.md](SecretsAtRest.md)) and the move. Setup ends with 10 and leaves\na mark, `NewInstallationPending` (the account chosen, `virtual` or `localsystem`)\nand `NewInstallationProfile` (the profile, if one was) under\n`HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\hMailServer` in the 32-bit view, writable by\nadministrators only. Either way the message offers then finishes it as a new\ninstallation:\n\n* **Run setup again**, with the setting corrected. It finds the mark - or the\n  LocalSystem service beside a `hMailServer.INI` that names no database, which an\n  earlier version's setup leaves - an",
         "d sets it up as a new installation's: the\n  pages offer, and a silent installation defaults to, the account and the profile\n  the mark holds (`NT SERVICE\\hMailServer` without one), `/serviceaccount=` and\n  `/profile=` choosing otherwise, and the steps after the database run as for any\n  new installation. A\n  LocalSystem service whose `hMailServer.INI` names a database, or whose file is\n  not found, is an upgrade's, and keeps LocalSystem unless the move is chosen.\n  On the built-in database the same holds: setup run again finishes the\n  installation, where `DBSetupQuick.exe` would set the database up and nothing\n  after it, so the message after a new installation's failed step names setup.\n* **Run `DBSetup.exe` as an administrator** from the `Bin` folder - its wizard, or\n  `/unattended` with the settings in its environment\n  ([WindowsOnArm.md](WindowsOnArm.md)). With the mark there, once the database is\n  set up it runs the installer's steps itself, in the installer's order, with\n  hMailServer.exe's verbs: the service stopped, `--apply-new-install-defaults`,\n  the profile (`--apply-profile NAME --once --if-new --confirm`),\n  `--create-stored-secrets-key`, and for the virtual account\n  `--grant-database-login`, the grant and `--service-account virtual /Register`;\n  then the service started and, when moved, held to opening its database, moved\n  back to LocalSystem when it does not. Each step is said as it goes, and the mark\n  is removed - kept only when the service would not stop, so that none of them\n  ran. The wizard asks Windows for elevation itself when the mark is there;\n  `/unattended` without elevation refuses, before it changes anything, with exit\n  code 2.\n\n**A new installation whose administrator cannot open the database** - SQL Server\nwith Windows authentication, where the database step made the database as\nLocalSystem, a SQL Server administrator, and the person running Setup is none -\nhas its database, and `--apply-new-install-defaults` (exit 2) and\n`--create-stored-secrets-key` (exit 1), run as that person, could not open it. Until\n6.3.6 neither was ever applied: the recovery the message gives - the statements run\nby a SQL Server administrator, then Setup again - runs as an upgrade. Now Setup,\nand DBSetup finishing an installation, start the service with the argument that has\nit apply them itself: `sc start hMailServer --apply-new-install-defaults`. The\nservice opens its database, applies a new installation's defaults with the verb's\nown rules - the database's `NewInstallDefaultsPending` mark required, the shape of a\nnew database, the mark cleared once decided - and, for the new installation it\nfound and applied them to, makes the stored-secrets key when it runs as LocalSystem, which may write `hMailServer.INI` (the virtual account may\nnot, and says so, HM7498), before its listeners start. What it could not do is in the\nerror log (HM7498, HM7510, HM7511), each step in the application log when that is\non, and the Control Deck's setup checklist shows the outcome. An administrator can start it so at any time after; the mark is\nacted on whatever its age, since the argument is the request. The profile chosen is\nnot applied this way: the Control Deck's checklist offers it.\n\nSetup clears the mark before its own database step, so that the DBSetup it runs\nleaves those steps to it, and writes it again only if that step does not complete\neither; the uninstaller removes it. An update hMailServer.Updater runs - the live\nupdate, or *Install update* in the Control Panel - leaves the mark alone and\nupgrades the installation as it is: unattended, it keeps the account and changes\nnothing a new installation would, and its database step is held to the version\nbefore it, as any upgrade's is. Until 6.3.6 setup run again kept the service on\nLocalSystem, and DBSetup set the database up and nothing else - no self-signed\ncertificate (the database kept its `NewInstallDefaultsPending` mark), no\nstored-secrets key, LocalSystem (the installer smo",
         "ke VM, 2 October 2026).\n\nEvery registration of an account other than LocalSystem also sets the service's\nSID type to *unrestricted*, so that the service's token carries\n`NT SERVICE\\hMailServer` whatever account it logs on as - which is what lets one\nset of grants serve the virtual account and a group managed service account alike.\nA registration that leaves the service on LocalSystem leaves the SID type alone.\n\n## What the account is granted: `hMailServer.exe /GrantServiceAccess`\n\nRun elevated - by the installer, by the Control Panel's buttons, or by hand from\nan elevated Command Prompt in the `Bin` folder. It grants the service's SID,\n`NT SERVICE\\hMailServer`\n(`S-1-5-80-3504853920-2627644491-1723263464-2546768561-1700046809`, derived from the\nname as Windows derives it), exactly this:\n\n| Where | What | Why |\n|---|---|---|\n| The program folder's own `Data`, and everything in it | Modify | The message store, and its ACME, Certificates, DKIM, Discovery, Files, Recoverable, Blobs, Lists, Updates and other folders |\n| The program folder's own `Logs`, and everything in it | Modify | The logs, and the crash dumps and the crash record, which are written there |\n| The program folder's own `Temp`, and everything in it | Modify | Messages being received and scanned, and the virus scanners' copies |\n| The program folder's own `Database`, and everything in it | Modify | The built-in SQL Server Compact database |\n| Each folder approved in `HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\hMailServer\\ServiceAccessPaths`, and everything in it | Modify, or Read | A data, log or backup folder, an archive folder, a certificate folder elsewhere - what an administrator said the service may reach |\n| Each other folder in the program folder - `Bin`, `DBScripts`, `Events`, `Languages`, `WebAdmin`, `Addons` and the rest - and everything in it | Read | The program, its scripts, its languages and the Deck. Not the program folder itself: every service holds *bypass traverse checking*, so the account reaches the folders in it without, and an entry on it would make Windows walk everything below it - the message store included, a tree the service writes in once it has moved |\n| Each file with protected permissions in the DKIM key folder and the Updates folder, when those are in a folder granted Modify | Modify, on the file | The DKIM rotation's keys and the live update's token are written with permissions of their own (`ProtectedFile`) that inherit nothing, so a service moved off LocalSystem would otherwise lose the keys it signs with |\n| A certificate or key file the configuration names inside a folder above, whose own permissions keep that folder's grant from it | Read, on the file | TLS and DKIM signing |\n| `Bin\\hMailServerApiKeys.ini` | Modify, on the file | The REST API's key store, which the service writes when a key is made or revoked. Made empty when it is not there yet - which the REST API reads as no keys - so that the service can write into it without being able to create files in `Bin`. It is the one file there the service may write, because nothing elevated takes a path, an account or a command from it: it holds key hashes the REST listener checks, and the service could mint a key through its own API anyway |\n| `hMailServer.INI` | Nothing | Read-only to the service - see below |\n\n**The program folder is where the program is**: the folder above the `Bin`\nfolder `hMailServer.exe` runs from, not `[Directories] ProgramFolder`. A binary\nthat is not in a folder named `Bin` grants nothing (exit 2).\n\n**What the configuration names is only reported.** The folders of\n`[Directories]` (DataFolder, LogFolder, TempFolder, DatabaseFolder for the\nbuilt-in database, EventFolder), the backup folder, `ArchiveDir` and\n`AcmeCertificateDirectory`, and the certificate and key files - the SSL\ncertificates, the domains' DKIM keys, the REST API's, the metrics listener's and\nthe web services' - are read from the file and from the database (the settings\nfrom `hm_inisettings` itself, not the ",
         "file's copy), and each is checked against\nthe grants above. One the grants reach needs nothing. Otherwise:\n\n* a folder nobody has approved is listed `NOT APPROVED`;\n* a certificate or key file is listed `OUTSIDE`, with the way to fix it: move it\n  into `Data\\Certificates`, which the data folder's grant reaches, or approve its\n  folder for Read. It is never given Read where it is: the configuration could\n  name any file on the machine;\n* a folder that may never be granted is listed `REFUSED`, with why;\n* a backup folder on a network share is noted: the service reaches a share as the\n  computer account (`DOMAIN\\COMPUTER$`), and that is granted on the share.\n\n**Approving a folder.** `--approve-modify \"<folder>\"` or `--approve-read\n\"<folder>\"` on the command line; or `--confirm`, with which the verb asks - in a\nbox of its own, on the desktop of the elevated process, which nothing running at\na lower integrity can answer - naming each folder not approved, each key file\noutside and each refusal, before it changes anything. Yes approves the folders,\nNo changes nothing at all. An approval is recorded in\n`HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\hMailServer\\ServiceAccessPaths` (64-bit view), one\nvalue per folder - its full path as the name, `Modify` or `Read` as the data - in\na key the verb makes owned by Administrators and writable by Administrators and\nSYSTEM alone. If anybody else may change that key, or owns it, nothing in it is\ngranted and the run fails with the reason. Approve only a folder you recognise:\nthe service may then write in it.\n\n**Never granted, approved or not**, a folder that:\n\n* is not a full path on a fixed local drive - a share, a device path (`\\\\?\\`,\n  `\\\\.\\`), a relative path or a stream;\n* is a drive's root;\n* is, or holds, the program folder; or is in it but outside its own `Data`,\n  `Logs`, `Temp` and `Database` - `Bin` above all;\n* is, holds or is in the Windows folder, a Program Files folder, the user\n  profiles folder or `ProgramData\\Microsoft` (every user's Start menu and startup\n  folder);\n* is or holds `ProgramData` (a folder of its own in it may be approved);\n* is, or runs through, a junction, a symbolic link or a mount point that the\n  administrators do not alone control - one the service's account, or anybody\n  else who is not an administrator, made or could turn elsewhere. The refusal\n  names who may change the link, or who owns it.\n\nEvery path is resolved first - `..` taken out, short names made long - so none of\nthese can be named another way.\n\n**A link the administrators alone control is gone through**, by the rule the store\ncommands follow (\"What an administrator's command writes\", below): keeping `Data`,\n`Logs`, a folder above them or a folder of the program such as `Events` on another\ndrive behind a junction or a volume mount point an administrator made is an\nordinary layout. Such a link - owned by `BUILTIN\\Administrators`, `SYSTEM` or\n`TrustedInstaller`, which nobody else may change or remove from the folder it is\nin (`OnlyAdministratorsControl`) - is followed, and the path it holds is held from\nthat volume's root in the same way - every link on it, the folder it ends at\nincluded, asked about in its turn, so a junction the service's account put where\nan administrator's link leads is refused - must be on a fixed local drive, and is\nheld to the refusals above as though it had been named itself: a junction from\n`Data` to a drive's root, the Windows folder or `Bin` is refused there. A volume\nmounted at `Data` or `Logs` with no drive letter of its own is gone through, its\nroot granted; one that also has a drive letter is that drive's root, and refused\nas one - take the letter away (`mountvol <letter>: /D`), or make the link a\njunction to a folder on that drive instead. The grant is written on the folder the\nlink leads to, by its volume's name (`\\\\?\\Volume{...}\\`), so that what it inherits\nis that folder's own parent's, not the link's; the line says where the link leads.\nA link to a share is refused as not on a ",
         "fixed local drive, the line naming the\nshare it leads to. Until 6.3.6 every link on the way was refused, and the upgrade\nof an installation whose data folder an administrator had moved behind a junction\nfailed its grant. The icacls that gives a link to the administrators alone is\nunder \"What an administrator's command writes\".\n\n**How a grant is written.** One allow entry for the service's SID is added and\nnothing is removed - no other entry is changed, an administrator's deny entry for\nthe service included; an explicit deny outranks any allow, so the line says the\ngrant FAILED and names it, and an inherited deny is named as the entry the new\none now comes before. While a folder is granted, it and every folder above it are\nheld open without sharing delete, so none of them can be renamed, removed or\nswapped for a link while the permission is written through the path - a link the\nadministrators alone control included, and every folder from the root of the\nvolume it leads to down. A file is granted through a handle opened without\nfollowing links, and only when it has one name - a hard link elsewhere would\ncarry the grant to wherever else it is. A\nsecond run finds the entries there and changes nothing: each line says\n`granted`, `already there` or `FAILED` with the reason, and the last line counts\nthem. Windows hands a folder's entry down to everything below it as part of the\ngrant, which on a large message store takes minutes the first time and nothing\nafter that.\n\n**What it checks, and only says.** When `Everyone`, `Authenticated Users`,\n`Users`, `SERVICE` or the service itself may write the program folder, `Bin` or\n`hMailServer.INI` - add, change or delete what is in it, or change who may - a\n`CHECK` line names the entry. The grant removes nobody's entry. An installation\noutside Program Files could inherit such an entry from a drive's root until 6.3.7;\n`hMailServer.exe /ProtectFolders`, which setup runs at every installation and\nupgrade before the grant, now takes away an entry any of the three inherits (below),\nso what is left for the line to name is an entry of its own, which an administrator\ngave, and the `icacls ... /remove:g` that takes it away.\n\n`--report-only` says all of this - what would be approved and granted, and what\nis not reached - and changes nothing.\n\nExit codes: 0 every grant made and everything the configuration names reached, or\nconfirmed; 1 a grant or an approval failed; 2 not in an installation's `Bin`\nfolder; 3 not run as an administrator; 4 folders not approved or key files\noutside, not confirmed; 5 the configuration names a folder that is never granted;\n6 the administrator answered No, and nothing was changed; 7 the grants were made,\nbut this account could not read the database, so the certificate and key files and\nthe backup and PST import folders it names are not known, and nobody confirmed. The\ninstaller and the Control Panel move the service only on 0.\n\n**A database it cannot read.** On SQL Server with Windows authentication, where\nLocalSystem is a SQL Server administrator and the person running the grant is not,\nthe grant cannot read what the database names. It says so on a `NOT READ` line and\nends in 7 - until 6.3.6 it said so in a note and ended in 0, and the service was\nmoved without anybody knowing whether a key file it reads lay outside its folders.\nWith `--confirm` the question names the database it could not read, and Yes is a\n0. A silent installation's move then waits, as for a folder nobody approved; an\nupgrade of a service already moved goes on, since nothing is moved. A database not\nset up yet names nothing, and is no 7.\n\n## What the other users of the computer may read: `hMailServer.exe /ProtectFolders`\n\nUntil 6.3.6 the program folder's own `Data`, `Logs`, `Temp`, `Database` and `Events`\nfolders, and `Bin\\hMailServer.INI`, took their permissions from the folder they are\nin. Under `C:\\Program Files` that gives `BUILTIN\\Users` - every account that can sign\nin to the computer: a Remote Deskt",
         "op server's users, a shared machine's, a service\nrunning as an ordinary account - Read on all of them, and `ALL APPLICATION PACKAGES`\nthe same. Any of them could read every message; the logs, with their addresses,\nsubjects and whole SMTP, IMAP and POP3 conversations; the built-in database, with the\naccounts' password hashes and the stored secrets' sealed values, whenever the service\nthat keeps its file open is stopped; a key file an administrator copied into\n`Data\\Certificates`; the event scripts; and `hMailServer.INI` - the database's\npassword, sealed with the machine's DPAPI key, which any account on the machine may\nuse to unseal it (`PasswordEncryption=6`), the administrator password's hash and its\nsecond factor. The grant (above) adds the service's entries and removes nobody's. A\ndata folder moved under a folder at a drive's root took that folder's permissions\ninstead once anything rewrote its own - on Windows Server, Users' Read and the right\nto make files and folders in it; on a Windows client, Authenticated Users' Modify.\nOn the installer smoke VM on 3 October 2026 a user in the `Users` group alone, signed\nin interactively, listed the five folders of a 6.3.5 installation and read two\nmessages, a log naming seven addresses, the event script, an imported key file and\n`hMailServer.INI`, and unsealed the database's password from it.\n\nSince 6.3.6 the installer runs `hMailServer.exe /ProtectFolders`, elevated, at every\ninstallation and upgrade of the server - whatever account the service runs as, before\nthe database step - and an administrator may run it from an elevated prompt in the\n`Bin` folder at any time. Each of the six is made private:\n\n| What | Who reaches it afterwards |\n|---|---|\n| `Data`, `Logs`, `Temp`, `Database`, and everything in them | SYSTEM and the Administrators, full control; `NT SERVICE\\hMailServer` Modify, the grant's, when the service runs as anything but LocalSystem |\n| `Events`, and everything in it | SYSTEM and the Administrators; the service Read, the grant's |\n| `Bin\\hMailServer.INI` | SYSTEM and the Administrators; `NT SERVICE\\hMailServer` Read, under every account - the service reads its configuration whatever it runs as, and writes it under none but LocalSystem |\n\n**How.** The permissions stop inheriting - a protected DACL - so that nothing the\nfolder above hands down reaches it. Every deny entry is kept, an inherited one as the\nfolder's own; every entry of the folder's own is kept, an administrator's and the\ngrant's; SYSTEM's and the Administrators' inherited entries become full control,\nhanded down; an inherited entry for the service's SID - the Read on `hMailServer.INI`\nthat `Bin`'s grant hands down - is kept as the file's own; and every other inherited\nallow entry is dropped and named on the line: `BUILTIN\\Users`, `ALL APPLICATION\nPACKAGES`, `ALL RESTRICTED APPLICATION PACKAGES`, `CREATOR OWNER`, and on a moved data\nfolder `Authenticated Users`. Windows hands the new permissions down to everything in\nthe folder as part of the change, which on a large message store takes minutes the\nfirst time; a second run finds them `already private` and changes nothing. A file or\nfolder below with permissions of its own - the DKIM keys and the update token\n(`ProtectedFile`), the self-signed certificate's key - keeps them.\n\n**Where it acts, and where it only says.** The six are found from where the program\nis, as the grant's folders are, never from `hMailServer.INI` or the database; a\njunction or a mount point among them is gone through only when the administrators\nalone control it, and held from that volume's root while it is changed, as the grant\ngoes through one - so a data folder an administrator moved to `C:\\MailStore\\Data`\nbehind such a junction is made private there. Anything else is checked and said,\nnever changed: a folder elsewhere may be shared on purpose, and an elevated program\ntakes no path to change from the configuration.\n\n* A folder `hMailServer.INI` names outside the program folder (`[Dire",
         "ctories]`), a\n  folder approved for the service, the backup, archive and PST import folders, and\n  the key and certificate files the database names: a `CHECK` line when Users,\n  Authenticated Users, Everyone, INTERACTIVE or another group of ordinary accounts may\n  read it, with the two `icacls` that close it.\n* A key or certificate file in `Data\\Certificates`, `Data\\DKIM` or `Data\\ACME` with\n  permissions of its own that such a group may read - a file *moved* there keeps the\n  permissions it had where it was: a `CHECK` line, and `icacls \"<file>\" /reset`, which\n  gives it the data folder's.\n* An entry of one of the six's own that lets such a group read it, which only an\n  administrator can have given: kept, and a `CHECK` line with the `icacls /remove:g`\n  that takes it away.\n\n`Bin\\hMailServerApiKeys.ini` is left as it is, readable as the rest of `Bin` is: it\nholds each REST API key's label and the SHA-256 of its 32 random bytes, which tells\nnobody the key, and the Control Panel lists the keys from it.\n\n**The program's own folders: what the other users may change.** Until 6.3.7 the\nprogram folder itself, `Bin` and `ControlPanel` took their permissions from the folder\nthey are in, as the six did. Under `C:\\Program Files` that is right: SYSTEM, the\nAdministrators and TrustedInstaller may change them and every other account may read\nand run them. In a folder at the root of a drive it is not. On a Windows client the\nroot hands every folder made below it `Authenticated Users` Modify, so every account\nthat can sign in to the computer could replace `hMailServer.exe`, or put a library in\n`Bin` that Windows loads into it before its own (a program's own folder is searched\nfirst), and the service - LocalSystem by default and after every upgrade that keeps\nit, or `NT SERVICE\\hMailServer` - ran it at its next start, as did the next\nadministrator to run `DBSetup`, the Control Panel's elevated steps or the uninstaller.\nOn Windows Server the root lets `Users` make files and folders in it, which is enough\nfor the library. Every release from 6.0.0 to 6.3.6 installed so at a drive's root\n(work item 78); the default folder, Program Files, was never affected.\n\nSince 6.3.7 `/ProtectFolders` hardens the three, after the six:\n\n| What | Who reaches it afterwards |\n|---|---|\n| The program folder itself - the uninstaller, and what the folders in it inherit (`DBScripts`, `Languages`, `WebAdmin`, `Addons`) | SYSTEM and the Administrators, full control; `BUILTIN\\Users` Read & execute where every user could read it before (Program Files, a drive's root), and no entry where no user could; the Administrators its owner |\n| `Bin`, and everything in it | The same; `NT SERVICE\\hMailServer` Read & execute, the grant's, and Modify on `Bin\\hMailServerApiKeys.ini` alone, the grant's, an entry of the file's own that stays |\n| `ControlPanel`, and everything in it | The same; `NT SERVICE\\hMailServer` Read & execute, the grant's |\n\n**How.** Each stops inheriting, as the six do; every deny entry and every entry of\nits own is kept - the grant's Read for the service among them; SYSTEM and the\nAdministrators get full control and `BUILTIN\\Users` Read & execute, each handed down,\nthe Users' a fresh entry whatever the folder above handed them, so that the Control\nPanel and `DBSetup` still run for an administrator whose programs run unelevated -\nbut only where `Everyone`, `Authenticated Users` or `Users` could read the folder\nbefore, as under Program Files and at a drive's root: in a folder made for the\npurpose that only the administrators may open, the Users get no entry, so that what\nthe program folder holds besides the six (a backup, a key file, a data folder of\nanother name) does not become every user's to read; and every inherited allow entry\nis dropped, the line naming those that gave a right to change it: `Authenticated\nUsers` from a client's drive root, `Users` and `CREATOR OWNER` from a Windows\nServer's, and under Program Files TrustedInstaller and `CREATOR OWNER`. A fold",
         "er\nsomebody other than SYSTEM, the Administrators or TrustedInstaller owns (one a user\nmade at a drive's root before the installation, which any user can) is given to the\nAdministrators, and the line names the owner it had: an owner may change the\npermissions whatever they say, and so could have written the folder's own entries\ntoo - those that let anybody but SYSTEM, the Administrators and TrustedInstaller\nchange it go with that owner, and are named. A file in `Bin` or `ControlPanel` with\npermissions of its own keeps them. A second run finds the three `already hardened`\nand changes nothing; under Program Files the first run of 6.3.7 makes the three's\nentries their own, and no account can then do anything there it could not before. The\nprogram folder is not changed while one of the five folders in it could not be made\nprivate, since Windows hands its change down through that folder; `Bin` and\n`ControlPanel` are hardened all the same.\n\n**Then the rule of an administrator's install.** After the three, the installation is\nheld to the rule an administrator's install of an update holds it to before it runs\nanything from it (\"The live update\", below): the folders above the program folder,\nthe program folder, `Bin` and `ControlPanel` with every folder, program and library in\nthem, `hMailServer.INI` and `AdminUpdate`. What the hardening makes passes it, so a\ndrive-root installation that the live update refused before 6.3.7 passes once\n`/ProtectFolders` has run. What it leaves - an entry of a file's or a folder's own that\nan administrator gave, an owner that is not SYSTEM, the Administrators or\nTrustedInstaller, or a folder *above* the program folder that others may delete or\nrename (an installation in `C:\\Apps\\hMailServer`, where `C:\\Apps` inherits\n`Authenticated Users` Modify from the drive's root) - is a `CHECK` line with the\n`icacls` that removes it, the first the rule finds, and is never changed here: a folder\nabove the program folder may hold other programs that rely on what it allows.\n\n**Setup.** Before it copies anything, setup reads the permissions of the folder chosen\non its folder page - or, when that is not there yet, of the nearest folder above it\nthat is - and when `Everyone`, `Authenticated Users`, `Users`, `INTERACTIVE` or\nanother group of every user may change what an installation there would hold, it says\nso, in a box and in the setup log (the log alone for a silent installation, and for an\nupgrade, whose folder page is not shown). It never refuses the folder: one made for\nthe purpose off a drive's root is an administrator's choice, and the hardening is what\ncloses it. A program folder setup makes is given SYSTEM's and the Administrators' full\ncontrol and - where every user could read it as it was made - the Users' Read &\nexecute, inheriting nothing, by `icacls` on the SIDs,\nbefore any other folder is made in it or any file copied - so the programs are never\nthere, even for the seconds before `/ProtectFolders`, with a weaker list.\n\n**The Control Panel alone.** An installation of the Control Panel without the server\n(the remote administration support with it or not) runs no `/ProtectFolders`: it has\nno server. Into a folder setup makes, that changes nothing - the folder is given its\npermissions before the files, as above. Into a folder that was there - one an\nadministrator made at a drive's root, or an earlier installation's - setup hardens it\nin place before it copies anything: the program folder, then `ControlPanel` and `Bin`\nwhere they are there, each as `/ProtectFolders` hardens a server's - SYSTEM and the\nAdministrators full control, the Users' Read & execute where every user could read it,\nnothing inherited, the entries of its own kept while SYSTEM, the Administrators or\nTrustedInstaller owns it, and otherwise the Administrators made its owner and no entry\nof its own left. It leaves alone, and says in the setup log, a drive's root; a folder\nthat holds a server installation (`Data` or `Bin\\hMailServer.INI` there),",
         " which that\ninstallation's own `/ProtectFolders` hardens once its data folders are private; and a\nfolder that is a link - a junction, a symbolic link, a mount point - or is in one,\nwhose permissions would be written wherever it leads. Every folder from below the\ndrive's root down to the one it changes is held open, with nobody else allowed to\ndelete or rename it, from before it is looked at until after it is changed, so that no\nuser can put a link in its place in between.\n\n**Before upgrading an installation at a drive's root.** From an elevated Command\nPrompt, with the installation's own folder in place of `C:\\hMailServer`, first see\nwho owns what is there. Of the two listings below, the first names the program\nfolder's owner on its line `.`, and the owner of each file and folder directly in\nit - `Bin`, `ControlPanel` and the uninstaller among them - on theirs; the second\nnames the owner of everything in the folders that hold the programs and the event\nscript, hidden files included, and of nothing in the data folders:\n\n```\ndir /q /a \"C:\\hMailServer\"\ndir /q /s /a \"C:\\hMailServer\\Bin\" \"C:\\hMailServer\\ControlPanel\" \"C:\\hMailServer\\Events\" \"C:\\hMailServer\\WebAdmin\" \"C:\\hMailServer\\DBScripts\" \"C:\\hMailServer\\Addons\" \"C:\\hMailServer\\Languages\"\n```\n\n`dir` cuts an owner's name to 23 characters (`NT SERVICE\\TrustedInsta`), shows\n`...` for an owner it cannot read, and says `File Not Found` for a folder the\ninstallation does not have - the Control Panel alone has no `Events`, `WebAdmin`,\n`DBScripts` or `Addons` - and lists the others. Each `icacls` line below must say\nthat it processed one file - `Successfully processed 1 files` on an English\nWindows: one that says 0 has changed nothing, and from `/remove:g` that is the only\nsign that it found no account for a SID or a name it was given. Then, as\n`/ProtectFolders` does, for the program folder, then `Bin`, then `ControlPanel`:\n\n1. Make the Administrators its owner - an owner may change the permissions whatever\n   they say. The folder alone: `/T` would reach the message store too.\n\n   ```\n   icacls \"C:\\hMailServer\" /setowner *S-1-5-32-544\n   ```\n\n2. List its entries with `icacls \"C:\\hMailServer\"`; one without `(I)` is the folder's\n   own. Take away each entry of its own but SYSTEM's and the Administrators'. The\n   Users' and the service's go by their SIDs, a line each, whatever they give -\n   steps 3 and 4 give back what they need:\n\n   ```\n   icacls \"C:\\hMailServer\" /remove:g *S-1-5-32-545\n   icacls \"C:\\hMailServer\" /remove:g *S-1-5-80-3504853920-2627644491-1723263464-2546768561-1700046809\n   ```\n\n   On an installation without the server - the Control Panel alone - leave out the\n   second line, and step 4: there is no service, so its SID names no account, and\n   `icacls` refuses a SID that names no account, and the rest of its line with it\n   (exit 1332). Anybody else's goes by the name the list gives, in quotes - but the\n   two application package groups by their SIDs, `*S-1-15-2-1` and `*S-1-15-2-2`,\n   since `icacls` does not take them by the names it gives them,\n   `APPLICATION PACKAGE AUTHORITY\\ALL APPLICATION PACKAGES` and\n   `APPLICATION PACKAGE AUTHORITY\\ALL RESTRICTED APPLICATION PACKAGES`. A `(DENY)`\n   entry only takes away, and stays.\n\n   ```\n   icacls \"C:\\hMailServer\" /remove:g \"<name>\"\n   ```\n\n   Where the list gives a SID and no name, Windows found no account for it - one\n   deleted, one of a domain it cannot reach, the service's where there is no\n   service - and `icacls` cannot take the entry away. PowerShell can, with the SID in\n   place of `<SID>`; the line takes away that SID's allow entries of the folder's\n   own, and nothing else:\n\n   ```\n   powershell -NoProfile -Command \"$d = Get-Item -LiteralPath 'C:\\hMailServer'; $a = $d.GetAccessControl('Access'); $a.RemoveAccessRuleAll((New-Object Security.AccessControl.FileSystemAccessRule ([Security.Principal.SecurityIdentifier]'<SID>'), 'FullControl', 'Allow')); $d.SetAccessControl($a)\"\n   ```\n\n3. Replace what it inherits, an",
         "d give SYSTEM and the Administrators full control and\n   the Users Read & execute:\n\n   ```\n   icacls \"C:\\hMailServer\" /inheritance:r /grant:r *S-1-5-18:(OI)(CI)F *S-1-5-32-544:(OI)(CI)F *S-1-5-32-545:(OI)(CI)RX\n   ```\n\n4. On `Bin` and `ControlPanel`, where the server is installed, give the service back\n   its Read & execute, the grant's:\n\n   ```\n   icacls \"C:\\hMailServer\\Bin\" /grant *S-1-5-80-3504853920-2627644491-1723263464-2546768561-1700046809:(OI)(CI)RX\n   ```\n\n`icacls` on each of the three then lists SYSTEM's and the Administrators'\n`(OI)(CI)(F)`, the Users' `(OI)(CI)(RX)`, on `Bin` and `ControlPanel` the service's\n`(OI)(CI)(RX)` where the server is installed, and any deny entry, which stays -\nnothing marked `(I)` and nobody else's. The SIDs are SYSTEM's, the Administrators',\nthe Users', the service's and the two application package groups', the same in\nevery language of Windows; where the folder above lets no user read the\ninstallation, leave out the Users' grant at the end of step 3, as `/ProtectFolders`\ndoes. On the program folder each change of its entries reaches everything in it,\nthe message store included where 6.3.5 or earlier left `Data` inheriting - minutes\nthere, and `Data` then readable by Users until the upgrade's `/ProtectFolders` makes\nit private. The service's Modify on `Bin\\hMailServerApiKeys.ini` is the file's own,\nand stays.\n\nThe steps keep anybody else from changing the three folders themselves from then\non; they undo nothing changed before, and a file or folder in them keeps its owner\nand any entry of its own. Where accounts that are not administrators could sign in\nto the computer while the three took the root's permissions; where the listings\nnamed an owner other than `BUILTIN\\Administrators`, `NT AUTHORITY\\SYSTEM` or\n`NT SERVICE\\TrustedInstaller` - `...` among them - for the program folder, a file\ndirectly in it, or `Bin`, `ControlPanel`, `Events`, `WebAdmin`, `DBScripts`,\n`Addons` or `Languages` or anything in them; or where step 2 took away an entry that\ngave anybody more than `(RX)`, the Users' and the service's included, or could not\ntake one away: a program in them may have been changed already, and so may the\nevent script in `Events`, which the service runs. Reinstall the programs from the\nrelease into a new folder that only the administrators may change - under Program\nFiles, say - not over this installation, rather than rely on the steps: setup leaves\nin place every file it does not ship, a library put in `Bin` among them, and puts the\nevent script and the scripts in `Addons` only where none is there.\n\n**Reinstalling into a new folder.** Run nothing from the old folder, its uninstaller\nincluded, which runs the old `Bin\\hMailServer.exe` as an administrator. Setup run\nover an installation shows no folder page and installs into the folder it finds, so\nit is given the new one with `/DIR`; and setup run into a folder without a settings\nfile sets up a new server there - an empty one, without this installation's mail,\naccounts or settings - so the settings file and the data go into the new folder\nfirst. Bring nothing else from the old folder, and read what you bring, as you would\nread a program. (Where nobody but the administrators could have changed the old\nfolder's programs, [RelocatingAnInstallation.md](RelocatingAnInstallation.md) moves\nthe whole installation instead, its programs copied with it: its step 6 runs\n`/ProtectFolders` on the copy, which a copy by hand does not get otherwise, and its\nstep 11 runs setup at the new folder as step 3 below does.) From the elevated\nprompt, with the new folder in place of `C:\\Program Files\\hMailServer` (the Control\nPanel alone brings nothing: step 3 is all of it):\n\n1. Stop the service. Make the new folder's `Bin`, `Data`, `Database` and `Events` -\n   the last three private to SYSTEM and the Administrators before anything is in\n   them - and copy the mail, the built-in database's folder and the settings file,\n   which is made private too. Copy, not move: a",
         " file moved within a drive keeps its\n   owner and its permissions.\n\n   ```\n   net stop hMailServer\n   mkdir \"C:\\Program Files\\hMailServer\\Bin\" \"C:\\Program Files\\hMailServer\\Data\" \"C:\\Program Files\\hMailServer\\Database\" \"C:\\Program Files\\hMailServer\\Events\"\n   icacls \"C:\\Program Files\\hMailServer\\Data\" /inheritance:r /grant:r *S-1-5-18:(OI)(CI)F *S-1-5-32-544:(OI)(CI)F\n   icacls \"C:\\Program Files\\hMailServer\\Database\" /inheritance:r /grant:r *S-1-5-18:(OI)(CI)F *S-1-5-32-544:(OI)(CI)F\n   icacls \"C:\\Program Files\\hMailServer\\Events\" /inheritance:r /grant:r *S-1-5-18:(OI)(CI)F *S-1-5-32-544:(OI)(CI)F\n   robocopy \"C:\\hMailServer\\Data\" \"C:\\Program Files\\hMailServer\\Data\" /E\n   robocopy \"C:\\hMailServer\\Database\" \"C:\\Program Files\\hMailServer\\Database\" /E\n   copy \"C:\\hMailServer\\Bin\\hMailServer.INI\" \"C:\\Program Files\\hMailServer\\Bin\"\n   icacls \"C:\\Program Files\\hMailServer\\Bin\\hMailServer.INI\" /inheritance:r /grant:r *S-1-5-18:F *S-1-5-32-544:F\n   ```\n\n2. Read the copy of the settings file - in Notepad, say. It took `Bin`'s permissions\n   until 6.3.6, and it decides what the service runs and who may administer it:\n\n   - `[Directories]` names the folders the service takes the database scripts and\n     `7za.exe` from (`ProgramFolder`) and the event scripts from (`EventFolder`),\n     and those of its data, logs and temporary files. Delete each of its lines that\n     names a folder in the old installation; setup writes the new folder's instead.\n     Setup keeps every other line but `ProgramFolder`, which it always writes, so\n     delete as well a line that names a folder outside both installations and that\n     you did not set - an `EventFolder` of somebody's own, say, which anybody who\n     could change `Bin` could have written.\n   - `AdministratorPassword`, in `[Security]`, is the administrator password's\n     hash. Delete it, and setup asks for a new password; a silent setup takes it\n     from `HM_ADMIN_PASSWORD`.\n   - `[Database]` names the database the server opens, and `AutoBanCommand`, in\n     `[Settings]` or `[SettingsOverride]`, a program it runs.\n   - Any other line with a path into the old installation still names it after\n     setup, and the new service goes on reading that file from the old folder:\n     `PostgreSQLSslRootCert` and `TlsCaFile`, in `[Database]`, the CA files the\n     database server's certificate is checked against; `UpdateTrustRootsFile`\n     and `UpdateLogPublicKeyFile`, in `[Settings]`, which are read from this file\n     alone; and a path in `[SettingsOverride]`. Change each as step 4 of\n     [RelocatingAnInstallation.md](RelocatingAnInstallation.md) changes it, to a\n     copy in the new folder of what it names - read before it is copied, as the\n     event script is below, or taken again from where it came from. The two CA\n     files and the two trust files go where nobody but the administrators may\n     change them, the new `Bin` say, and not in `Data`, which the service may\n     change: an administrator's install of an update refuses a trust file anybody\n     else may change (\"The live update\", below). The two trust files are the live\n     update's trust with `UpdateSigningIdentity`, `UpdateSigningIssuer` and\n     `UpdateSourceRepository` beside them in `[Settings]`, and decide whose\n     signature an update is installed on: delete any of the five your site did\n     not set, and an update is checked against the project's own signers\n     ([UpdateFeed.md](UpdateFeed.md), \"Where the trust is read from\").\n   - Delete as well any other line your site did not set in `[SettingsOverride]`,\n     each of which takes the place of the database's value of its `[Settings]`\n     key at every start, any key but those five; in `[LDAP]`, which the server\n     reads from this file alone, and which decides where and how the password of\n     a directory-linked account is checked; and in `[SendingLimits]` and\n     `[SendingLimitsOverrides]`, read from it alone too, which limit what an\n     account may send.\n\n   Read what is i",
         "n the old `Events` - the event script - as well, and only then\n   copy it:\n\n   ```\n   robocopy \"C:\\hMailServer\\Events\" \"C:\\Program Files\\hMailServer\\Events\" /E\n   ```\n\n3. Where the first line below finds an entry - a 32-bit installation's - setup\n   installs into the folder that entry names, whatever `/DIR` says: take it away with\n   the second. Then run the release's setup with the new folder. For the server, it\n   finds the settings file there and goes on as an upgrade of this installation, the\n   service's account kept, with the programs its own; `sc qc hMailServer` then names\n   the new `Bin`.\n\n   ```\n   reg query \"HKLM\\SOFTWARE\\Microsoft\\Windows\\CurrentVersion\\Uninstall\\hMailServer_is1\" /reg:32\n   reg delete \"HKLM\\SOFTWARE\\Microsoft\\Windows\\CurrentVersion\\Uninstall\\hMailServer_is1\" /reg:32 /f\n   hMailServer-<version>-x64.exe /DIR=\"C:\\Program Files\\hMailServer\"\n   ```\n\n4. The database comes across as it was - on the built-in database in a file that\n   took the program folder's permissions until 6.3.6. In the new Control Panel,\n   read the programs its settings name: ClamWin's and the custom scanner's under\n   *Anti-virus settings*, and `AutoBanCommand` under *Auto-ban*. Make the REST API's\n   keys again rather than copy `Bin\\hMailServerApiKeys.ini`, to which anybody who\n   could change `Bin` could have added one. A path the database holds into the old\n   folder - a certificate's, a DKIM key's, the backup folder - still names it: step\n   9 of [RelocatingAnInstallation.md](RelocatingAnInstallation.md) moves each.\n   Until nothing names it, keep the old folder private to SYSTEM and the\n   Administrators: where 6.3.5 or earlier left its `Data`, `Logs`, `Database` and\n   `Bin\\hMailServer.INI` taking the permissions of the folder they are in, every\n   user may still read the mail, the logs, the built-in database and the settings\n   file there, and nothing runs `/ProtectFolders` on a folder no installation is\n   in. As soon as step 1 has copied what it copies - for a service running as\n   `NT SERVICE\\hMailServer`, once the paths named at the end of this step are\n   moved too - make the Administrators the old folder's owner and replace what it\n   inherits. The folder alone has its owner changed, and on a large message store\n   the second line takes minutes, as above:\n\n   ```\n   icacls \"C:\\hMailServer\" /setowner *S-1-5-32-544\n   icacls \"C:\\hMailServer\" /inheritance:r /grant:r *S-1-5-18:(OI)(CI)F *S-1-5-32-544:(OI)(CI)F\n   ```\n\n   Then take away each entry of its own but SYSTEM's and the Administrators', as\n   step 2 of *Before upgrading an installation at a drive's root* does. Where\n   those steps, or the `/ProtectFolders` of 6.3.7 or later, were run on the old\n   folder, `Bin` and `ControlPanel` inherit nothing from it and keep any Read &\n   execute the Users were given, and a settings file that 6.3.5 or earlier left\n   taking `Bin`'s permissions takes it from `Bin`: take that entry away from both\n   as well, from `ControlPanel` where the installation has one:\n\n   ```\n   icacls \"C:\\hMailServer\\Bin\" /remove:g *S-1-5-32-545\n   icacls \"C:\\hMailServer\\ControlPanel\" /remove:g *S-1-5-32-545\n   ```\n\n   Any other entry of its own that a folder in it has stays - the grant's for the\n   service among them, on each folder that was in it when the old installation's\n   grant last ran - so the service still reads what the database names in those,\n   and writes what it names in Data, Logs, Temp and Database.\n   A service running as `NT SERVICE\\hMailServer` may have reached what the\n   database names directly in the old folder, or in a folder made in it since\n   then, and a folder it writes in anywhere else in it - a backup or archive\n   folder, which the grant gives no more than Read there (\"Never granted,\n   approved or not\", above) - only through what the Users and Authenticated\n   Users were given there, which this step takes away: move each first, by step 9 of\n   [RelocatingAnInstallation.md](RelocatingAnInstallation.md). Then, once nothing\n ",
         "  names it, delete the old folder - by hand, never with its uninstaller.\n\nExit codes: 0 each made private or hardened, or so already (`CHECK` lines do not change\nit); 1 one could not be, and its line says why - a link somebody else controls,\npermissions that could not be written; 2 not in an installation's `Bin` folder; 3 not\nrun as an administrator. `--report-only` says what it would make private and who may\nread each now, and what it would harden and who may change each now, and changes\nnothing; it comes before the verb, as the mode of the other commands does:\n`hMailServer.exe --report-only /ProtectFolders` (after the verb it is not read, and\nnothing is changed or reported). A run that does not end in 0 does not fail the installation:\nsetup says so, with the command to run again, and what could not be changed is as it\nwas.\n\n**What an administrator notices.** An account that is not an administrator - and an\nadministrator's own programs not run as administrator - can no longer open these\nfolders or `hMailServer.INI`:\n\n* Explorer asks *You don't currently have permission to access this folder*, and\n  *Continue* adds an entry for the administrator's own account to the folder, which\n  later runs keep. Open the logs with an editor run as administrator instead, to\n  leave the folder as it is.\n* The Control Panel not run as administrator - on a Windows client, where an\n  administrator's programs run unelevated - says *Could not read the file* on its live\n  log page and its message-store check, where it would have said there was no log\n  folder; what it does over COM is as before. On Windows Server the built-in\n  Administrator account runs it elevated already.\n* A script, a backup program or a monitoring agent that read the logs or the message\n  store as an ordinary account needs an entry of its own -\n  `icacls \"<folder>\" /grant \"<account>:(OI)(CI)RX\"` - which the next run keeps.\n* An editor that saves `hMailServer.INI` by writing a new file and renaming it over the\n  old one gives it `Bin`'s permissions again: run `hMailServer.exe /ProtectFolders`\n  after, or the next upgrade does.\n\n## Its login on SQL Server: `hMailServer.exe --grant-database-login`\n\nOn SQL Server reached with Windows authentication - `[Database] Type=MSSQL` and no\n`Username` - the server signs in as the account the service runs as. LocalSystem\nis `NT AUTHORITY\\SYSTEM` to a SQL Server on the same machine; the virtual account\nis `NT SERVICE\\hMailServer`, which has no login there until one is made, and no\nuser in the hMailServer database, which can only be made once the database\nexists. The verb makes what is missing of the three, on a connection of its own,\nas the account that runs it:\n\n```\nIF NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM sys.server_principals WHERE name = N'NT SERVICE\\hMailServer') CREATE LOGIN [NT SERVICE\\hMailServer] FROM WINDOWS;\nUSE [hMailServer];\nIF NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM sys.database_principals WHERE name = N'NT SERVICE\\hMailServer') CREATE USER [NT SERVICE\\hMailServer] FOR LOGIN [NT SERVICE\\hMailServer];\nALTER ROLE db_owner ADD MEMBER [NT SERVICE\\hMailServer];\n```\n\n(`hMailServer` being the database `[Database] Database` names.) It looks first and\nleaves what is there - a login, a user under another name for the same login, or\nthe login as the database's owner - and takes nothing away. Each step is one line:\n`made`, or `there already`. It makes no user and changes no role in a database\nthat has DDL triggers of its own (`sys.triggers`, enabled, on the database): such\na trigger runs with the rights on SQL Server of whoever's statement fires it, so\nanybody who was ever `db_owner` there - an earlier server's login, another\nprogram's - could have left one to run as the administrator, server-wide. The\nverb then says so, and a SQL Server administrator reads the triggers before\nrunning the statements. Run it from an elevated prompt in the `Bin` folder as\nan account that may make logins on that SQL Server (`securityadmin` or `sysadmin`)\nand that can open the database;",
         " an administrator who is a SQL Server administrator\nthrough the `Administrators` group is one only when elevated. The installer runs it\nas the administrator running setup, before it moves the service (above), and so\ndoes DBSetup when it finishes a new installation. When it cannot do a step, it says\nwhich and why, and prints the statements above for a SQL Server administrator to\nrun, with the `sqlcmd` line that runs them. It says everything on its own output\nand writes nothing to the error log or the Application log: a connection the\naccount running it cannot make would otherwise be reported there as the server's\nown failure to reach its database (HM5028), and the log folder is the service's.\n\nA SQL Server on **another computer** is left alone: there the virtual account signs\nin as this computer's own account, `DOMAIN\\COMPUTER$`, as LocalSystem does, so the\nlogin that worked keeps working, and a login named `NT SERVICE\\hMailServer` could\nnot even be made there. The verb compares SQL Server's `MachineName` and\n`ComputerNamePhysicalNetBIOS` with this computer's name, and says which it found.\n\nExit codes: 0 the service's account can open the database - its login, user and\n`db_owner` made now or there already - or needs nothing here, which the lines say\n(not SQL Server with Windows authentication, or SQL Server on another computer); 1\none of them could not be made; 2 `hMailServer.INI` names no database, or the\ndatabase could not be opened as the account that ran it. Run before\n`--service-account virtual /Register` with `&&`, it moves only a service that will\nopen its database.\n\n## `hMailServer.INI` is read-only to the service\n\nThe file keeps four kinds of thing, and under `NT SERVICE\\hMailServer` the service\nwrites none of them. What each writer does instead:\n\n| What wrote the file | Under the virtual account | How |\n|---|---|---|\n| The `[Settings]` copy of the settings store, at every start, reinitialise and save (`IniSettingStore`) | Not written. The database is the settings store and its values are in use, as ever | Said in the application log, not as an error, with the settings whose line there is older than the stored value counted and named, at a reinitialise where application logging is on. At the service's start the line is dropped: the settings store is reconciled before the server has read its logging settings (`Application::InitInstance` runs `IniSettingStore::Synchronize` before `Configuration::Load`). A setting removed while the file still has its line is kept stored as its default - which the server treats the same - because a dropped row would be put back from the line at the next start; a key this build does not read is refused with a sentence. Each row records what the file really holds, so an old copy is never taken for an edit, and an edit is reported once, as `HM5804`. A backup restored stores its settings in the database |\n| `PasswordPepper`, kept in the file only | Refused with a sentence | An administrator sets it in the file with an elevated editor and restarts the service |\n| The live update's trust - `UpdateSigningIdentity`, `UpdateSigningIssuer`, `UpdateSourceRepository`, `UpdateTrustRootsFile` and `UpdateLogPublicKeyFile` - read from the file alone from 6.3.6 | Refused with a sentence, under every account: the process an update is checked for may not choose what it is checked against ([UpdateFeed.md](UpdateFeed.md), \"Where the trust is read from\") | An administrator sets them in the file with an elevated editor and restarts the service |\n| `[Directories]` - COM `Directories.*` | Refused with a sentence | An administrator changes the file and approves a new folder for the service |\n| `[Database]` - COM `Database.CreateInternalDatabase`, `CreateExternalDatabase`, `SetDefaultDatabase`, which DBSetup and DBSetupQuick call | Refused before anything is made | The installer does its database step under LocalSystem (above). To change the database later, move the service to LocalSystem for the change and back, or",
         " edit `[Database]` and restart |\n| `[Security] AdministratorPassword` - COM `Settings.SetAdministratorPassword`, the Control Panel's *Administrator password* card | Refused with a sentence | `hMailServer.exe /SetAdministratorPassword`, from an elevated prompt, reads the password from standard input and writes the hash the card writes; restart the service. From PowerShell: `$p = Read-Host -AsSecureString 'New administrator password'; [Net.NetworkCredential]::new('', $p).Password \\| & '.\\hMailServer.exe' /SetAdministratorPassword` |\n| `[Security] AdministratorTotpSecret` - COM `Settings.EnrolAdministratorTOTP` and `DisableAdministratorTOTP`, `POST` and `DELETE /api/v1/administrator/second-factor` | Refused with a sentence (409 over REST) | To turn it off, delete the line with an elevated editor and restart. **Enrolling needs an account that may write the file**: the server makes the secret and hands it out once, so move the service to LocalSystem for the enrolment and back |\n\n`Bin\\hMailServerApiKeys.ini`, a file of its own, is still written (above).\n\nThe server decides by asking the file - opened for writing and closed at once -\nnot by the account's name, so a service that may write it (LocalSystem, an\naccount an administrator granted) behaves as before. The Linux packages already\ninstall `/etc/hmailserver/hMailServer.ini` as `0640 root:hmailserver`; the same\nhandling applies there, and a setting saved is no longer an error at every start.\nThe `[Settings]` copy is stale for anything that reads the file itself rather\nthan the database: the server when its table cannot be read, the installer (for\n`ShutdownDrainSeconds`), the Control Panel when it has no COM connection, and a\nperson opening the file.\n\n## `/Register` takes the account from its command line\n\n`hMailServer.exe --service-account virtual|localsystem|keep|DOMAIN\\name$ /Register`.\nWithout `--service-account` it keeps the account a service has, and makes a new\none LocalSystem. `DOMAIN\\name$` is a group managed service account, which has no\npassword. Any other account has one, and is refused: it is set in `services.msc`\n(the service's *Log on* tab), and every upgrade keeps it.\n\n`ServiceAccountName` and `ServiceAccountPassword` are no longer used: the server still\nloads them with its other settings (`IniFileSettings.cpp`), and nothing reads them. They were\nwhat `/Register` used, and they are written by others: they are rows of the\nsettings store, which hMailServer administrators - who need not be Windows\nadministrators - set over REST and COM, and which the service writes; and the file\nholding their copy is written by whichever account last could. A registration\nthat read them would let any of those make the next elevated `/Register` give the\nservice LocalSystem back. Asking whether the file is writable by administrators\nalone would answer for one of the two places the value comes from, so the value is\nnot read at all. The Control Panel no longer edits them; its *Setup checklist* page\nasks for a stored password to be deleted.\n\n## What an administrator's command writes\n\n`hMailServer.exe` is the service, and also the commands an administrator runs:\nSetup, DBSetup and the Control Panel run `/Register`, `/RegisterTypeLib`,\n`/Unregister`, `/GrantServiceAccess`, `/ProtectFolders`, `--grant-database-login`,\n`--apply-new-install-defaults`, `--apply-profile`, `--create-stored-secrets-key`,\n`--set-admin-password` and the live update's two elevated, and an administrator\nruns the rest - `--upgrade-database`, the stored-secrets commands, the store and\nobject-store commands, `--apply-environment`, `--check-database` - from an\nelevated prompt. Each reads `[Database]` or `[Security]` from `hMailServer.INI`, or\nchanges the service, so each runs with the administrator's rights; and the folders\nthe service is granted Modify on - `Logs`, `Data`, `Temp`, `Database` - are folders\na program running as `NT SERVICE\\hMailServer` can put a junction or a mount point\nin. A file an administrator's process",
         " wrote there by its path could be written\nwherever that leads.\n\nSo, since 6.3.6:\n\n* **No log, no event, no crash record.** Every command line but `RunAsService`,\n  `/Debug` and `/Test` forbids the logger's writes as the first thing it does\n  (`ForbidServiceFolderWrites` in `hMailServer.cpp`, `Logger::\n  ForbidWritesForThisProcess`): no line reaches the error log or any other log, the\n  Application log, the `OnError` event script or the SQL log device; and it arms no\n  crash oracle, whose record and dump go to the log folder. What each command did,\n  and why it failed, is on its own standard output and standard error, where it\n  already said it; Setup, DBSetup and the Control Panel keep that in their logs.\n  `/Register` says there why a registration failed. Until 6.3.6 only the live\n  update's verbs, the grant and `--grant-database-login` did this; the others wrote\n  their errors to the error log, and every one armed the crash oracle.\n* **No junction followed where Windows can refuse it.** On Windows 11 22H2 and\n  Windows Server 2025 and later the same commands follow no junction that a user\n  who is not an administrator made (`ProcessRedirectionTrustPolicy`); an older\n  Windows refuses the request, and the next two rules hold there alone.\n* **The new installation's certificate is made through held folders.**\n  `--apply-new-install-defaults` writes the self-signed certificate and its private\n  key into the data folder's `Certificates` folder. It holds every folder of that\n  path open from the drive's root down - none renamed or removed while held, and\n  none a link but one the administrators alone control (below) - makes\n  `Certificates` and each file relative to the folder above, never through a link\n  of its name, and checks each file it made to be in that folder before writing a\n  byte to it. The key's permissions - SYSTEM, the Administrators, the account\n  running it and Read for the service's account - are given when it is made, not\n  set by its path later.\n* **A store command finds no link in the data folder that somebody else controls.**\n  `--compress-store` (and `--decompress`), `--encrypt-store`, `--decrypt-store`,\n  `--recount-store`, `--measure-store`, `--copy-store-from-object-storage` and\n  `--copy-store-to-object-storage` reach each message by its path. Before anything\n  is read or changed the data folder is held from its drive's root down and every\n  folder and file in it listed once; a junction, a mount point or a symbolic link\n  on the way to it or in it that the administrators do not alone control stops the\n  command, exit 1, naming it and why. A file that is only tiered or deduplicated is\n  not a link. A copy to the object store that removes nothing is held to it too: it\n  reads with an administrator's rights and hands what it read to a store the\n  service's account reads. So is `--measure-store` since the re-check of 6.3.6,\n  although it changes nothing and says what it read to the administrator alone: it\n  reads every file with an administrator's rights, and one rule for every store\n  command leaves no exception to reason about.\n* **A link the administrators alone control is gone through.** Keeping the data on\n  another drive through a junction or a volume mount point an administrator made -\n  `Data` itself, a folder above it, or a domain's folder in it - is an ordinary\n  layout. Such a link is gone through when it is owned by `BUILTIN\\Administrators`,\n  `SYSTEM` or `TrustedInstaller` - an account without an administrator's rights\n  cannot make one so owned - nobody else may change it (write its data or\n  attributes, which is how a link is pointed elsewhere, add to it, delete it, or\n  change its permissions or owner), and no entry in the permissions of the folder it\n  is in lets anybody else remove it from there or change that folder's permissions\n  or owner\n  (`OnlyAdministratorsControl`, `ProtectedFile.h`). The path it holds - where it\n  says it leads, read from the link - is then held in the same way fro",
         "m that\n  volume's own root and checked to be the folder the link opened, so a link\n  somebody else made further along is refused there, the folder the link leads to\n  itself included: until 6.3.6's re-check the folder an open through the link\n  ended at was walked instead, and that open follows a second link unasked. A\n  link to a file is never gone through. What a refusal says names who may change\n  the link, or who owns it. A link made in the data folder inherits the write rights setup\n  grants the service's account there, and one made in a folder at a drive's root\n  inherits them for `Users` or `Authenticated Users`, the service's account among\n  them: either way the service could turn it elsewhere, and it is refused until it\n  is given permissions only the administrators may change. An elevated prompt on\n  Windows Server makes a link owned by `BUILTIN\\Administrators` already; where the\n  owner is the administrator's own account instead (a Windows client without UAC's\n  split token, for one), give it to the Administrators too. For the link (and, for\n  a mount point, the folder it is mounted on) - `/L` acts on a symbolic link itself\n  rather than where it leads:\n  `icacls <link> /setowner *S-1-5-32-544 /L` and\n  `icacls <link> /inheritance:r /grant:r *S-1-5-32-544:(F) *S-1-5-18:(F) *S-1-5-32-545:(RX) /L`\n  - or make it below a folder only they may write, such as the program folder. The\n  service still reaches the mail through it: what is below the link is reached with\n  the permissions of the folder it leads to; only the link itself, which the service\n  then may not remove or change, is held to the link's.\n  A folder no administrator may list - `System Volume Information`, when the data\n  folder is a drive's root - is left unlisted when the administrators alone control\n  it in the same way: nobody else can have put anything in it. Until the re-check of\n  6.3.6 every link and every such folder stopped the command.\n* **A store command reaches no file outside the data folder.** The names it\n  rewrites, removes and copies over are the database's - each message's file name,\n  each deleted item's, each held part's - and, on an object store, its keys: both\n  are the service's to write. A row whose file is outside the data folder - a full\n  path elsewhere, or a part that is `..` - stops every store command, naming the\n  message, before it reads or changes a file; a key whose name would lead out of it\n  is not listed; and a held part's name is its SHA-256 in hex or names no file. A\n  full path below the folder a junction or mount point of the data folder leads to\n  - written when the data was there by that name - is in it. A full path elsewhere\n  is usually one a server before 5.4 wrote: put `DataFolder` back to the folder it\n  names, run the Data Directory Synchronizer there (`Addons\\DataDirectorySynchronizer`;\n  the diagnostic *Test message file locations* says whether any are left), which\n  turns each into a name below the data folder, and move the data as\n  [RelocatingAnInstallation.md](RelocatingAnInstallation.md) says; or delete the\n  message.\n\nWhat is left: a program that still runs as the service's account after the service\nhas stopped could make a link while a long store command runs, after the folder was\nlisted - or, owning a folder in the data folder, as it owns those it made, give\nitself the right to remove an administrator's link from it and put one of its own\nthere; on Windows 11 22H2, Windows Server 2025 and later the command does not follow\nit, and the next command's listing refuses it everywhere. An account holding the right to restore files (`SeRestorePrivilege`, Backup\nOperators) can give a file any owner; the service's account holds none, and one\nconfigured to would be an administrator in all but name. The SQL Server Compact database file in `Database` is opened by its path by the\ncommands that open the database; that engine opens only a file that is a database,\nand creates none.\n\n`build/check-elevated-verbs.py` h",
         "olds the commands to these rules and lists them:\nit refuses a command line added to the three that may log, a dispatch before the\ndecision, the crash oracle armed for any other, the certificate written by its path,\nand a store command - `--measure-store` included - or a copy dispatched without the\ncheck or behind a condition. `--self-test` breaks each rule in a copy and proves the\ncheck reports it.\n\n### On Linux, a command run as root\n\nThe package runs the service as `hmailserver` (the unit's `User=`), which owns the\ndata directory `/var/lib/hmailserver` and the log directory `/var/log/hmailserver`\nand so can put a symbolic link anywhere in them. Since the re-check of 6.3.6:\n\n* **The commands that work in those folders run as the service's account.** The\n  store commands (`--measure-store`, `--compress-store` and `--decompress`,\n  `--recount-store`, `--encrypt-store`, `--decrypt-store`), the copies to and from an\n  object store, `--sample-domain`, `--apply-profile`, `--revert-profile` and\n  `--apply-environment` are run as that account, as their help says. Run as root\n  (`sudo hmailserver --compress-store`), each now becomes it - the data directory's\n  owner, with that account's groups - before it opens anything, says so on standard\n  error, and so reaches only what the service reaches, through any link, and leaves\n  no file behind that the service cannot open. A data directory that cannot be\n  examined refuses, exit 2. So does one whose way is not root's and its owner's alone - a folder above\n  it, or above where a symbolic link on the way points, that belongs to another\n  account or that another account or group may write - since an account able to put\n  a link there could choose which folder, and so which owner, the path leads to; the\n  refusal names the folder. The package's `/var/lib/hmailserver` is below folders\n  only root writes. Until then they ran as root, held only to name no file outside the data\n  directory, and followed a symbolic link the service's account put in it.\n* **Over a data directory that belongs to root, they run as root where nothing but\n  root could have put a link.** A service run as root - a source build or a\n  container without `User=`, or the Linux regression suite - has a data directory\n  of root's, and a command run as root there crosses no boundary, provided no other\n  account could have put a symbolic link where it goes. So it runs on as root, and\n  says so on standard error (\"Run as root: ... runs as root, the data directory ...\n  and everything under it being root's alone ...\"), when every one of these is\n  root's alone: the data directory, every folder under it, and every folder a\n  symbolic link under it leads to; the log, temporary, event and database folders\n  (`[Directories]` `LogFolder`, `TempFolder`, `EventFolder`, `DatabaseFolder`, each\n  that is set - every command opens the database through the server's start,\n  which can write into any of them, and the store commands take their lock in the\n  log directory), and everything under each; the way to each, as above with\n  root for the owner; and the configuration that names them all - `--config`'s\n  file, or the one found - and the way to it, since the command reads it again\n  as it starts and writes its settings back to it, and an account that could\n  change it could point the folders at a tree of root's of its choosing. Root's\n  alone means each folder (and the configuration) belongs to root and no other\n  account may write it: no write for others, and for its group only when that is\n  root's group and no access control list stands behind the group's bits; no\n  folder carries a default access control list that gives another account - or,\n  on a set-group-ID folder, a group other than root's - a write to what is made\n  in it; and each symbolic link belongs to root and leads where the way is root's\n  alone. The folders are walked with `lstat`, following only links that pass; a\n  file is not judged, since in such a folder no other accou",
         "nt can put one there\n  or replace one with a link. A folder that is not there yet is judged by the\n  folder that would hold it. What the command then makes is root's alone too: it\n  runs with at least every other account's write taken away by its umask (022),\n  whatever umask it was started under. Anything else - a folder of another\n  account's in the store, one a group other than root's or every account may\n  write, a link another account owns, a log directory of the service's account's\n  beside a data directory of root's, a configuration another account owns or may\n  write, a log directory below Ubuntu's `/var/log`, which the `syslog` group may\n  write - refuses, exit 2, naming the first folder, file or link that fails and\n  what to do: give it to root and take the other accounts' write from it (`chown\n  root:root`, `chmod go-w`, `setfacl -bk`), name folders that are root's alone in\n  the configuration, or give the data directory to the service's account, as the\n  package does, so that the command runs as that account. Until the re-check of\n  6.3.6 a data directory of root's refused every one of these commands, which\n  stopped administrators of such a service and kept nobody out.\n* **Every other command line run as root writes nothing in the log directory.**\n  `--set-setting`, the stored-secrets commands, `--create-database`,\n  `--upgrade-database`, `--set-admin-password`, `--check-database`,\n  `--verify-update` and the rest need root for the configuration or say only what\n  they read; each says what it did, and why it failed, on its own output. What\n  would have gone to the error log is not written, but for the stored-secrets\n  code's `HM7496` and `HM7497`, which go to standard error\n  ([SecretsAtRest.md](SecretsAtRest.md)). The server itself, and a command run as\n  the service's account, log as before.\n* **`--create-database` makes `.new-installation` new.** Anything at that name in\n  the data directory is removed first - a symbolic link itself, not what it names -\n  and the file is then created exclusively, never through a link\n  (`WriteNewFileNoFollow`); the stored-secrets key file was made so already\n  (`DataProtector::CreateKeyFileFor`).\n\n## What LocalSystem could do and the virtual account cannot\n\nEvery right the service used that only LocalSystem - or an administrator - has,\nand what changed:\n\n| What | Under LocalSystem | Under `NT SERVICE\\hMailServer` | How |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Its own folders | Anything on the machine | The grants above | `/GrantServiceAccess` |\n| `hMailServer.INI` | Written | Read only | The section above |\n| A file you name that is outside them - a certificate, a key, a DKIM key file, a message to import, a backup to restore | Read wherever it is | Read only where the account may | The grant lists the configured ones as `OUTSIDE`; move them into `Data\\Certificates`, or approve their folder for Read |\n| The Windows event log's source (`HKLM\\SYSTEM\\...\\EventLog\\Application\\hMailServer`) | Registered at the first error forwarded | Cannot write HKLM | `/Register`, which runs elevated, registers it; the running service only reads it (`WindowsEventLog::RegisterSource`). Writing to the Application log itself is allowed to every service |\n| The COM AppID (`HKCR\\AppID`) | Re-written at every start | That write fails, silently and harmlessly | `/Register` writes it, as it always has |\n| The DCOM access and launch permissions | Unchanged | Unchanged | They are about the callers - the Control Panel, scripts - who stay the administrators |\n| Changing its own service entry (COM `Utilities.MakeDependent`, which DBSetup uses to depend on a local database service) | Worked | Refused | The installer sets the database up while the service is LocalSystem; DBSetup run later falls back to `sc.exe config hMailServer depend= RPCSS/<service>` itself, as an administrator. The right is deliberately not granted: a service that may rewrite its own entry may make itself LocalSystem again |\n| The live update | The helper runs",
         " the installer | The service cannot run it | The update is still checked, downloaded and verified; an administrator installs it with *Install update* in the Control Panel on the server, after Windows' prompt (below) |\n| The auto-ban's Windows Defender Firewall rules (`AutoBanFirewall`) | Written | Refused | Writing a firewall rule takes an administrator and there is no narrower right to grant. Said once in the error log (HM7460) and in the checklist; banned addresses are still refused by the server. `AutoBanCommand` still runs - as the service account, so a command that asks a router or a cloud firewall works and one that runs `netsh` locally does not. Rules a LocalSystem run left behind cannot be removed by the new account, and are named once (HM7461) with the PowerShell that removes them |\n| A port | Any | Any | Windows lets any account bind any port, and the firewall's rule is per program: nothing to grant |\n| The secret store | Machine-scope DPAPI | The same | Any account on the machine can decrypt it - which is why shared hosting stays declined |\n| SQL Server with integrated authentication, on the same machine | The login `NT AUTHORITY\\SYSTEM` | The login `NT SERVICE\\hMailServer` | `hMailServer.exe --grant-database-login` (above) makes it, a user for it in the hMailServer database and `db_owner` - which the installer runs before the move, as the administrator running setup; where that account may not, the service stays on LocalSystem and setup gives the statements for a SQL Server administrator |\n| SQL Server with integrated authentication, on another machine | The computer account, `DOMAIN\\COMPUTER$` | The computer account, as before | A virtual account is the computer account on the network, so the login that worked keeps working. For an identity of the server's own rather than the whole computer's, run the service as a group managed service account (`--service-account DOMAIN\\hmail$ /Register`) and give that a login |\n| MySQL, MariaDB, PostgreSQL | A user and password | The same | Nothing changes |\n| A backup written to a network share | The computer account | The computer account | Grant `DOMAIN\\COMPUTER$` on the share and its folder, as for LocalSystem |\n| A backup or archive folder on this machine, set after the move | Written | Written once approved | Grant access lists it as not approved and asks |\n| A custom virus scanner, ClamWin | Ran as LocalSystem | Runs as the service account | It reads the message in the temp folder, which is granted, and its own program and signatures, which the Users group can normally read. A scanner that needs more has to be granted it |\n| ClamAV's `clamd`, SpamAssassin's `spamd` | Over TCP | Over TCP | Nothing changes: the message is streamed, not named by path |\n| Event scripts | Ran with LocalSystem's rights | Run with the service account's | A script that writes files, starts programs or reaches a database with integrated authentication can do only what the account can |\n| Active Directory sign-in (`LogonUser`, LDAP bind) | Worked | The same | No privilege is needed for a network logon. Kerberos acceptance with no explicit account (`GssapiAcceptor`) uses the computer account's keys under both; this is expected and not yet exercised on a domain-joined bench |\n| Crash dumps | Written to the log folder | The same | `hMailServer.Minidump.exe` runs as the service account and writes to the granted log folder |\n| \"Log on as a service\" | Implicit | Held through `NT SERVICE\\ALL SERVICES` | A domain policy that replaces the right has to keep that group, or the service reports error 1069 and does not start |\n\n## The live update\n\nThe helper that applies an update (`hMailServer.Updater.exe`) is started by the\nservice and runs as the service's account, and the installer it runs needs an\nadministrator. On LocalSystem - an upgraded installation that kept it - nothing\nabout the live update changes: the service hands the installer to the helper, in\nthe update window or when *Install update* is clicked",
         ", as\n[UpdateFeed.md](UpdateFeed.md) describes.\n\nUnder an account that is not an administrator the service still checks for the\nupdate, downloads it and verifies it, and from 6.3.6 **an administrator installs\nit with one click**: *Install update* on the Updates page of the Control Panel on\nthe server (on Server status in 6.3.6). Windows asks for permission once;\neverything after that happens by\nitself, and the Control Panel comes back signed in, on the new version, when the\nservice is running. The service never runs anything as an administrator, and\nnothing it can write decides what the administrator's side runs.\n\n### What happens after the click\n\n1. **Windows' prompt.** The Control Panel starts `Bin\\hMailServer.exe\n   /InstallUpdate` of its own installation elevated (`runas`), for the release the\n   Status page shows. Only a Control Panel on the server does this: one connected\n   to another computer says to open the Control Panel there, and so do the Control\n   Deck and `POST /api/v1/update/install` (409, code\n   `update_install_by_administrator`). A Control Panel that is not the\n   installation's own - a copy run from elsewhere - says to open the installation's.\n   If the prompt is declined, nothing has changed anywhere.\n2. **The elevated stage checks the installation.** It finds the installation from the\n   service's registration and `hMailServer.INI`, which only administrators can write,\n   never from what the service says, and checks that nobody but SYSTEM, the\n   Administrators and TrustedInstaller can change what an administrator's process\n   will run or read there: the folders above the program folder and the program\n   folder itself (deleting in them, deleting them, or changing their permissions or\n   owner), and `Bin` and `ControlPanel` with every folder, program and library in\n   them, `hMailServer.INI`, `AdminUpdate` and the trust files `hMailServer.INI` names\n   (any write). Something others can change stops the install before anything has\n   changed, and the Control Panel names the folder, the account, the right and the\n   `icacls` command that removes it: a program run from there as an administrator\n   could be one that account put there. `hMailServer.exe /ProtectFolders` gives the\n   program folder, `Bin` and `ControlPanel` the permissions this passes, and says the\n   rest of what it finds (\"The program's own folders\", above). The same rule is\n   checked by the Control Panel before the prompt, from a table of cases both sides\n   are tested against (`build/testdata/install-permissions-cases.json`).\n3. **It copies the installer out of the service's reach.** `AdminUpdate\\<id>` in the\n   program folder - owned by the Administrators, SYSTEM and Administrators alone may\n   change it, the administrator who clicked may read it - is made new for each\n   install, and the installer and its Sigstore bundle are copied into it from\n   `Data\\Updates`, which the service writes. `AdminUpdate` itself lets anyone read\n   its permissions, and nothing else of it, so that the Control Panel of every later\n   install can hold it to the rule before the prompt. A name that leads through a\n   link or a junction, a folder where a file should be, or a copy bigger than 1 GB\n   is refused. The copies are held open, so that nothing can change them between\n   the check and the install, and everything below is checked on the held bytes.\n4. **It checks the installer itself**, trusting nothing the service said about it:\n   - the **Authenticode signature**, the primary one, with Windows' own\n     verification, and the **publisher pinned**: the certificate names exactly\n     `CN=Progressive Robot Ltd, O=Progressive Robot Ltd, L=Chester, S=Cheshire West\n     and Chester, C=GB`, its chain ends, by SHA-256, in Microsoft ID Verified Code\n     Signing PCA 2021 and Microsoft Identity Verification Root Certificate Authority\n     2020, it is issued for code signing, and the signature carries a timestamp. A\n     file that Windows trusts but another publisher",
         " signed is refused. Where the\n     installed `hMailServer.exe` is signed by somebody else - a site that builds and\n     signs its own - the installer is held to that signer instead, the same name\n     through the same root, and where the installed one is not signed at all there\n     is nothing to hold it to, and the one-click install is refused. A name is\n     compared whole: one with a NUL in it is refused, never read up to the NUL;\n   - **revocation**, online, of the whole chain, within 30 seconds: a certificate\n     that is revoked is refused, and so is one whose revocation could not be\n     checked - said as the revocation server not being reachable, never as revoked.\n     Windows' own answer is not enough for that: its Authenticode policy takes a\n     revocation server that does not answer as good, so the stage reads the\n     revocation status of each certificate in the chain Windows built, the root\n     aside, and refuses where one is unknown or was checked offline (on the\n     installer smoke VM before 6.3.6 was released, a revocation server held\n     unanswered let an installer through as \"revocation checked online\"; with\n     its outbound HTTP blocked, Windows answered 0 for the real 6.3.5 while each\n     certificate of its chain below the root said its revocation was unknown and\n     checked offline, and with HTTP open and nothing cached the same check\n     fetched Microsoft's lists and passed). The chain read is the signer's; the\n     timestamp's own chain is left to Windows' answer. So whoever can keep this\n     computer from the revocation server - `www.microsoft.com`, where Microsoft\n     publishes the lists for the project's certificates - can stop the one-click\n     install: a firewall that lets out only the download, a proxy the check\n     cannot pass, or somebody on the network between. That is a denial, refused\n     before anything is changed, never an install of anything else; the\n     installer can still be run by hand, where Windows' own check, which takes\n     an unreachable revocation server as good, is what applies;\n   - the **version** in its version resource is the release offered, the\n     **architecture** in its description (`hMailServer Setup (x64)` or `(ARM64)`)\n     is this installation's, and the release is **newer** than the running\n     server, whose version is compiled into the stage;\n   - the **Sigstore bundle** - the pipeline for that release's tag, and the\n     transparency log - checked by a second copy of `hMailServer.exe` that the\n     stage starts with no privilege but change-notify, the Administrators group\n     and every other group UAC takes from an administrator (Backup Operators,\n     Domain Admins and the rest) made deny-only, at low integrity, in a job that\n     holds it to one process and 512 MB, and that is given the bundle and the\n     installer's SHA-256 through a pipe and nothing else. The bundle is parsed\n     there, not in the elevated process, and a child that a crafted bundle took\n     over could write nothing of the administrator's: at medium integrity, the\n     administrator's own unelevated level, it could still have written their\n     profile and registry, which their programs and Windows' auto-elevating ones\n     read. Its answer is not what the install rests on - the Authenticode pin is.\n5. **The service prepares, on the administrator's request** (COM\n   `Status.PrepareUpdateForAdministrator`, asked from this computer only): it\n   checks its own download again, takes the backup (`UpdateBackupBeforeApply`, to\n   the destination *Backup, restore & migration* sets) and fetches the running version's\n   installer as the rollback image. The stage copies the rollback image out and\n   checks it as it checked the installer, held to the running version. A backup\n   that fails asks the administrator whether to install without one; with no\n   destination set, the window says the rollback puts the program back and not the\n   database. Nothing elevated takes anything from the ",
         "service's answer: the\n   service's part is the backup, the drain and the token.\n6. **The install begins** (`Status.BeginUpdateForAdministrator`): the service\n   issues the database upgrade's single-use token, closes its listeners and lets\n   the mail in progress finish (the drain), and the stage hands over to a copy of\n   `hMailServer.Updater.exe` in the work folder, checked by its SHA-256. The\n   Control Panel closes, so that the installer can replace it.\n7. **The Updater installs** as the administrator: it ends every program still\n   running from the installation, runs the held installer silently - the token in\n   its environment, not on its command line - with its temporary files in the work\n   folder, waits for the service to come back, runs the held rollback image if it\n   does not, or if the installer ends with 10, but never once the installer's\n   database step has changed the database (exit code 11, `upgraded-incomplete`;\n   [Upgrading.md](Upgrading.md), \"If the upgrade fails\"), and asks Windows to hold\n   off a sign-out or a shutdown while it works\n   (the reason shows on the sign-out screen). It writes the\n   outcome into the work folder, which only administrators can write, puts an\n   entry in the Application log (HM7466 when it starts, HM7467 with the outcome),\n   and opens the Control Panel again as the administrator who clicked, not\n   elevated: as a child of that user's desktop shell, `explorer.exe` in the same\n   session, with the shell's own token.\n8. **The Control Panel comes back** signed in, from a handoff it wrote before it\n   closed - DPAPI for the Windows user who clicked, bound to a key only that user\n   and the Administrators can read in the work folder, good for 30 minutes and\n   deleted when it is read - and shows the outcome. Where the administrator\n   password has a second factor it asks for the sign-in instead. The service\n   reports the outcome at its start, in the application log and the audit trail\n   (object type `update`, interface `Windows`, the Windows account that approved\n   it; [AlertsAndAuditTrail.md](AlertsAndAuditTrail.md)).\n\nThe whole of the elevated side writes only into the work folder, never into\n`Data`, `Logs` or anything else the service can change, and `hMailServer.exe` loads\nthe libraries it links from `Bin` and System32 only (`/DEPENDENTLOADFLAG`). A\nstage that finds another administrator's install under way on the server says so\nand does nothing. `{app}\\AdminUpdate` is removed by the uninstaller.\n\n### The token\n\nThe token the installer's database upgrade presents is in the `Updates` folder, in a\nfile the service can write (`ProtectedFile`: SYSTEM, the Administrators and the\nservice), and a token lets its holder sign in over COM as the server's\nadministrator. That is not a way for the service to reach an administrator: a\nservice that could forge it holds everything the server's administrator can do\nalready, and redeeming it runs nothing elevated. In an administrator's install a\nservice that rewrote it could at worst make the genuine installer's database\nupgrade fail, and the Updater put the previous version back - a denial, not a\ncompromise. The Updater reads the file only if it is no link, sits where it is\nexpected and holds exactly 64 lowercase hexadecimal characters, and gives it to a\n6.3.6 or later installer in its environment (`HM_UPGRADE_TOKEN`), so that it never\nappears on a command line - nor in the installer's log, which Inno Setup begins with\nthe command line it was started with; the installer reads it and removes it from its\nown environment at once. The live update of a service running as LocalSystem gives it\nthe same way from 6.3.6; up to 6.3.5 it was on the installer's command line, and so\nin `Updates\\install-<version>.log` - a single-use token, spent once the installer's\ndatabase step had run, in a folder only SYSTEM, the Administrators and the service\ncan read.\n\n### Why this and not a scheduled task\n\nA scheduled task running as SYSTEM, which the service could t",
         "rigger, was\nconsidered and not built. The only file it could run is one in a folder the\nservice writes, so to be safe it would have to copy the installer somewhere the\nservice cannot reach, verify it there itself - not trusting the service's word,\nwhich is the whole point - pin the publisher, refuse an older version, and write\nits log and outcome nowhere the service could turn into a link to a system file.\nThat is a second updater running as SYSTEM, and a service that can make SYSTEM run\na file has given back what the account took away.\n\nThe administrator's install does every one of those checks, and differs from the\ntask in what starts it: an administrator's click at the server, and Windows'\nprompt, which the service can neither cause, show nor answer. No elevated program\nwaits for the service or listens to it; the elevated stage starts from the click\nor not at all, and what it runs is what it checked itself.\n\n### From 6.3.4 and 6.3.5\n\nA server on 6.3.4 or 6.3.5 under this account cannot install 6.3.6 or a later\nrelease this way: the refusal is compiled into those versions, and their\n*Install update* asks the service alone. It is installed by hand once - download\nthe installer from the release page, check that `Get-AuthenticodeSignature`\nreports it `Valid` and signed by `CN=Progressive Robot Ltd`, and run it as an\nadministrator, keeping the service account on the installer's page. Windows\nSmartScreen may warn about a file downloaded with a browser that it has not yet\nseen often; the signature is what to check. From 6.3.6 on, each update is the one\nclick above, and never meets SmartScreen: the installer is not downloaded by a\nbrowser, and the Updater starts it directly.\n\nThe unattended update window does not install under this account: when the window\nis open and an installer is verified, the error log says once per process that an\nadministrator installs it with *Install update* in the Control Panel on this server\n(HM7463), and no backup is taken for an install that cannot happen.\n\n## Where you see it\n\n* **The checklist** (`GET /api/v1/setup/checklist`, [SetupChecklist.md](SetupChecklist.md)):\n  the tenth item, `service_account`. `service_account_local_system` (on Linux\n  `service_account_root`) is *action needed* while the server runs as the most\n  privileged account - a recommendation, as the missing profile is, never an\n  error; `service_account_own` names any other account and is done. Under an\n  account that is not an administrator, `autoban_firewall_not_permitted` is added\n  while `AutoBanFirewall` is on, and `update_apply_by_administrator` while the\n  update check is. The server judges from its own token, not from what the\n  service manager was asked for.\n* **The Control Deck's dashboard** shows the same finding, as information, while\n  the server runs as LocalSystem or root, with a way to the checklist.\n* **The Control Panel**, *Server limits & expert settings*, card *Windows service\n  account*: what the service runs as, and three buttons, each run after the\n  elevation prompt with what it said shown afterwards - **Grant access**\n  (`--confirm /GrantServiceAccess`), **Move to NT SERVICE\\hMailServer** (the same\n  grant, then `--service-account virtual /Register` only if it ended in 0) and\n  **Move to LocalSystem** (`--service-account localsystem /Register`). *External\n  setup* carries the same item, and asks for a stored `ServiceAccountPassword` to\n  be deleted.\n\n## Moving it, and going back\n\nTo move a service that runs as LocalSystem to the virtual account: run the\ninstaller again and choose it, use the Control Panel's button, or from an elevated\nCommand Prompt in the `Bin` folder\n\n```\nhMailServer.exe --grant-database-login\nhMailServer.exe --confirm /GrantServiceAccess\nhMailServer.exe --service-account virtual /Register\nnet stop hMailServer && net start hMailServer\n```\n\nrunning each line only when the one before it ended in 0. The first matters only\nfor SQL Server with Windows authentication, and says \"Nothing to do\" o",
         "therwise;\nthe Control Panel's button does not run it, so on such a database run it, or the\nstatements it gives, before the button. To go back to LocalSystem:\nrun the installer again and choose *Move it back to LocalSystem*\n(`/serviceaccount=localsystem` silently), use the Control Panel's button, or\n\n```\nhMailServer.exe --service-account localsystem /Register\nnet stop hMailServer && net start hMailServer\n```\n\nThe grants and approvals stay behind and do nothing under LocalSystem;\n`icacls <folder> /remove \"NT SERVICE\\hMailServer\" /T` takes the grants away, and\ndeleting the `ServiceAccessPaths` key the approvals, if you want them gone.\n\n## Error codes\n\n| Code | Severity | Said when |\n|---|---|---|\n| HM7460 | Medium, once per process | `AutoBanFirewall` is on and the account cannot write firewall rules |\n| HM7461 | High, once per process | Auto-ban rules a LocalSystem run left in the firewall cannot be removed by this account |\n| HM7462 | Medium | `/Register` could not set the service's SID type to unrestricted |\n| HM7463 | Medium, once per process | The update window is open and the update cannot be applied unattended by this account: an administrator installs it with *Install update* in the Control Panel on this server |\n| HM7464 | Medium | `/Register` was given an account it does not set - one with a password - and registered nothing |\n| HM7465 | Medium | An administrator's install did not stop the service within 10 minutes of its start: the drain is left, the token revoked, and the server accepts mail again. The Updater's log in `AdminUpdate` says how far it got |\n| HM7466 | Information, in the Windows Application log | An administrator's install has started: who, which version over which, and the work folder. Written by `hMailServer.Updater.exe`, with the administrator's account as the entry's user |\n| HM7467 | Information, a warning or an error, in the Windows Application log | An administrator's install ended: installed, cancelled or refused (a warning), or not installed, rolled back, with its rollback failed, or installed with its database upgraded and a step not complete - `upgraded-incomplete`, not rolled back - (an error), with the reason |\n| HM7468 | Medium | The service's preparation of an administrator's install failed: the step (the check of its download, the backup, the rollback image) and the reason |\n| HM7469 | Medium, once per process | The outcome of the last administrator's install could not be read, or an install that began more than an hour ago wrote none - the Windows session it ran in may have been signed out |\n\nNone of HM7460 to HM7464 fires on a server as it is shipped: the firewall half of the\nauto-ban and the unattended update window are both off until they are switched on.\nHM7465 to HM7469 come only from an administrator's install. An administrator's install\nthat did not succeed is also reported as HM5480, as the unattended apply's is - and one\nwhose database step changed the database before a step did not complete, which nothing\nputs back, as HM5481 ([Upgrading.md](Upgrading.md), \"If the upgrade fails\").\n\n## Tests\n\n* `ServiceAccountTester` (`Common/Util/ServiceAccount.cpp`), in the internal suite\n  `Utilities.RunTestSuite` runs: the service SID against the one Windows publishes\n  for TrustedInstaller; which folders a grant may reach, on a machine made up for\n  the purpose - `[Directories] DataFolder` pointed at `Bin`, at the program folder\n  and at `Data\\..\\Bin`, the Windows folder, Program Files, a profile, a share, a\n  device path, a stream, a drive's root, all refused; the program's own four and a\n  folder of its own on another drive, not - and the plan made from them: a\n  malicious data folder refused and not granted, an unapproved backup folder\n  listed, an approved one granted, an approval of the Windows folder refused, a\n  key file outside listed and one inside read on the file alone; the logon\n  `/Register` passes for each `--service-account`, an account with a password and\n  LocalSystem under",
         " another name refused; a grant on a directory of its own under\n  the temp folder, made, made again with nothing changed, the one explicit entry\n  it leaves, handed down to a file that was there before and one made after, an\n  administrator's own entry kept; a file with protected permissions granted\n  without losing its protection or its other entries; a hard-linked file refused;\n  a path through a symbolic link refused where one can be made; a path through a\n  junction only the administrators control held to where it leads, the junction\n  and that folder not removable while held and the grant's path the volume's\n  name of it, one the service's account may change refused naming that account,\n  also where the administrators' junction leads to it, one leading to a folder no\n  grant may reach refused, a folder missing beyond one found missing, and a grant\n  through one made on the folder it leads to, handed down, that folder keeping\n  what it inherits from its own folder and the junction still the\n  administrators' alone (skipped only where the tester is no administrator); a\n  held folder that cannot be removed; and `/ProtectFolders` (`TestProtect_`) on\n  folders of its own that hand down what Program Files hands down - BUILTIN\\Users'\n  Read - beside a deny entry and Read for the service's SID: Users no longer reach\n  the folder, a file already in it or one made after; the deny kept as the\n  folder's own and first; an administrator's entry and the service's grant kept;\n  SYSTEM and the Administrators given full control handed down; a file keeping the\n  service's Read as its own, or given it where it had none; a second run changing\n  nothing; a file with two names refused; and an entry of the folder's own for\n  Users, given after, kept and still seen. And the program's own folders it\n  hardens: in memory (`TestHardenedShape_`, which needs no administrator), the\n  permissions built from what a Windows client's drive root, a Windows Server's,\n  Program Files, an inherited Modify or Read for the service, a deny entry beside an\n  administrator's entry, no list at all and a folder only the administrators may\n  open hand a folder down, each held to the rule of an administrator's install of an\n  update and passing it, nobody but SYSTEM, the Administrators and TrustedInstaller\n  left to write, the Users' Read & execute given where every user could read before\n  and no entry for them where none could, the dropped entries that gave a right to\n  write named and the Users' Read not, the grant's Read, the deny (first) and the\n  administrator's entry kept, and taken for hardened only when protected and the\n  Administrators' to own; a folder a user made with entries of its own for itself\n  and Everyone keeping them while the Administrators own it and losing them, named,\n  when its owner is not trusted; and on the disk (`TestHarden_`), an\n  installation of the tester's own under a folder that hands down what a client's\n  drive root does - the program folder with the uninstaller, `Bin` with the server, a\n  library, `hMailServer.INI` private and the key store, `ControlPanel` - which\n  Authenticated Users may change and the rule refuses before, hardened: nobody but\n  SYSTEM and the Administrators may change a folder, a file in it or one made after,\n  Users still read and run them, the grant's entries and the key store's Modify kept,\n  the rule passing every folder, program and library, a second run changing nothing,\n  an entry an administrator gives Users afterwards kept and named by the rule, and a\n  `Bin` a user owns given to the Administrators, the Modify that user gave itself\n  taken away (where the tester runs as an administrator's own account).\n* `Installation/InstallerPackaging.cs`: the page's three forms and its defaults,\n  `/serviceaccount=`, the word `/Register` is given and that `hMailServer.exe`\n  knows it, that nothing reads `ServiceAccountName` any more, the registration\n  before the database step and the move after it, the move only on",
         " a grant that\n  ended in 0, `--confirm` in an attended installation, and a failed grant's own\n  message; and `/ProtectFolders` run at every installation and upgrade, under no\n  condition, after the registration and before the grant and the database step,\n  its output in the setup log and its message suppressible, the verb taking the\n  program folder's own six from where it is, holding each from the drive's root,\n  and giving the service Read on `hMailServer.INI`; and the verb hardening the\n  program folder, `Bin` and `ControlPanel` after the six, through the pin, the program\n  folder not while one of the six failed, with a fresh Read & execute for Users\n  where every user could read before, nothing inherited and the Administrators as\n  owner, then holding the installation to\n  the rule of an administrator's install; setup's message naming them, the program\n  folder the first `[Dirs]` makes and given its permissions by SID before anything\n  else, and the folder page saying, in a suppressible box and the log, and never\n  refusing, when the chosen folder lets every user change what is in it.\n* `API/RestApiSetupChecklist.cs`: the tenth item, judged from the server's own\n  token and, beside a running service, checked against the account `sc.exe qc`\n  reports.\n* The administrator's install: `AdminUpdateTester`\n  (`Common/Util/AdminUpdateTester.cpp`) holds every check of the elevated stage to\n  its negative - the permission rule against the table of cases the Control Panel's\n  `InstallationPermissionsTests` reads too, `Bin` at a client's and at a Windows\n  Server's drive root refused and `Bin` and a program in it as `/ProtectFolders`\n  hardens them passing among them; the work folder made new and an old one, one with\n  others' rights and a junction refused; the copy through a junction, a link and a\n  stream refused; the architecture marker; the pin alone, a subject with an attribute\n  more or less, another chain, no code-signing use; and the real signed installers of\n  6.3.1 and 6.3.3 to 6.3.5 (put on the bench by `build/fetch-installer-fixtures.ps1`,\n  checked by their SHA-256) passing, with 6.3.0 (unsigned), a byte changed, a file\n  cut short or grown, the .NET runtime's installer (another publisher, trusted by\n  Windows) and a catalogue-signed system file refused, the site-signer path held to\n  the installed signer's name and root (the same name through another root refused),\n  a name with a NUL in it refused, a revocation check with no time to run refused as\n  not reachable, and a chain whose revocation status is unknown or offline below its\n  root refused while one with only its root unchecked passes; and the lowered child's\n  token read back - the Administrators and every group UAC filters deny-only, no\n  privilege but change-notify, low integrity - its job's limit, its timeout and its\n  output cap. `AdminUpdateStageTester` holds the stage's request to its child (a\n  round trip, and every malformed frame refused), the child's answers, the Updater's\n  outcome file, and the real 6.3.5 bundle verified through the child as the stage\n  runs it. The Control Panel's `AdministratorUpdateTests`, `AfterUpdateHandoffTests`\n  (a handoff without its key, another user's, an old one and a second read refused)\n  and `UpdateStatusTextTests`; `API/UpdateApply.cs`, which on the bench's LocalSystem\n  service holds the administrator's path shut, and where the service is another\n  account holds the 409 and the state; and `build/check-elevated-verbs.py`, which\n  fails when a verb that runs elevated reaches the log or the crash handler. The\n  whole install, with Windows' prompt, is run by hand on the installer smoke VM.\n* The live registration - a new installation under the virtual account, an upgrade\n  that keeps LocalSystem, a move each way, the question the grant asks - is\n  exercised by hand on a throwaway machine; nothing here registers a service on\n  the bench.\n",
         nullptr
      };

