      const char *const Page105Pieces[] =
      {
         "# The first-hour checklist\n\nWhat a new server still needs before it is a mail server anybody can use, judged\nby the server itself rather than by whoever is reading the documentation. Thirteen\nitems, in the order a new installation meets them:\n\n| Key | Item | Done when |\n|---|---|---|\n| `profile` | A profile for this server | a setting profile has been applied ([Profiles.md](Profiles.md)); asked for on a young server - fewer than ten accounts, the sample domain's not counted - and not needed on one past that, where a profile that closes a listener or turns a filter off is not something to suggest in a list of things left to do, nor where the shipped settings were chosen instead; asked for again when a profile could not be written in full |\n| `domain` | A domain | at least one domain exists and is active, and none has the name of a directory the server keeps its own files in under the data directory (below) |\n| `account` | An account | at least one account exists in any domain |\n| `submission` | TLS on the submission port | every SMTP listener other than port 25 has STARTTLS or implicit TLS and a certificate that exists, other than the self-signed one a new installation made ([SecureDefaults.md](SecureDefaults.md)), which mail clients warn about |\n| `sign_in_tls` | Passwords only over TLS | every IP range that lets a client connect from an address other than this computer's requires TLS for authentication, or reaches only listeners that start TLS before a password ([SecureDefaults.md](SecureDefaults.md)) |\n| `delivery_port_auth` | No password on port 25 | every SMTP listener on port 25 - the port other servers deliver to - has SMTP authentication Honeypot or Off, or is named by `DisableAUTHList`; a listener bound to a loopback address only is not judged, and without a listener on 25 the item is not needed ([SmtpAuthOnDeliveryPorts.md](SmtpAuthOnDeliveryPorts.md)). A new installation's port 25 is a honeypot; an upgraded server's takes AUTH until it is changed, and the finding carries no fix, since changing it is what arms the ban |\n| `spf` | An SPF record | every active domain has a TXT record beginning `v=spf1` |\n| `dkim` | DKIM signing | every active domain signs, its selector and key file are set, the key file exists, and a TXT record with a `p=` tag is at `<selector>._domainkey.<domain>` |\n| `dmarc` | A DMARC record | every active domain has a TXT record beginning `v=DMARC1` at `_dmarc.<domain>` |\n| `backup` | A backup destination | the backup destination is set and the folder exists on the server |\n| `second_factor` | A second factor for the administrator | the administrator password is enrolled with a one-time code |\n| `stored_secrets_key` | The stored-secrets key | this server holds the key the database records, and an export of it has been recorded - the key an account's second factor and the `[Settings]` secrets are sealed under, which exists on the servers that hold it and in the files exported from them, so that a database restored without it opens none of them ([SecretsAtRest.md](SecretsAtRest.md)); not needed with `ProtectStoredSecretsWithDPAPI` at 0 |\n| `service_account` | The account the server runs as | the server runs as an account that is not the most privileged one on the machine - not LocalSystem on Windows, not root on Linux ([ServiceAccount.md](ServiceAccount.md)). Judged from the server's own token. The most privileged account is action needed as the missing profile is: a recommendation, the account an upgraded Windows installation keeps, never an error. Under an account that is not an administrator, what it cannot do is said beside it while the setting that needs it is on |\n\nEach item is in one of four states, and an item is as bad as its worst finding:\n\n| State | Meaning |\n|---|---|\n| `done` | the server found the thing in place |\n| `action_needed` | it is missing or wrong, and the finding says what |\n| `cannot_tell` | the server could not find out: a DNS lookup failed, or the caller asked for none. This ",
         "says nothing either way |\n| `not_needed` | there is nothing to do, because nothing depends on it yet (the three record items, with no active domain; the passwords item, on a server only this computer may connect to; the stored-secrets key, with sealing switched off) |\n\nThe judgement is made from what the server can see: its own configuration, the\nfiles it would open, and DNS lookups made the way a receiving server makes them.\nPort 25 is never the submission port, whatever its security setting: it is the\nserver-to-server port, and clients are not sent there. A plain submission port\nbeside a secured one still needs action, because the plain one is the one a\nbadly configured client will use. A listener whose certificate is the\nself-signed one a new installation made needs action too: its TLS works, and\nevery mail client warns about it until a certificate authority's replaces it -\nACME's does, and takes the listener over when it arrives. The passwords item\njudges every IP range that is not all loopback and lets a client connect over\nSMTP, POP3 or IMAP: done when the range requires TLS for authentication, or when\nevery listener it may use - a listener bound to a loopback address only is not\none - has implicit TLS or requires STARTTLS; otherwise it\nneeds action and names the listeners - no TLS, or STARTTLS a client is free not\nto start - on which a password from there would cross the network in the clear.\nIt is `sign_in_plain`, with the fix below, when every one of those listeners\noffers STARTTLS with a certificate that is there, and `sign_in_no_tls`, with no\nfix, when one of them has no TLS to start: requiring TLS for sign-in then would\nrefuse every client there on that listener, so the finding says to give those\nlisteners a certificate and STARTTLS first.\nA server whose ranges are all on the loopback takes no password from a network,\nand the item is not needed. The DKIM item checks that a record is\npublished; whether it carries the key the domain signs with is the DKIM\nrotation check's business (`ReputationWatch.md`'s neighbour, `DkimRotation.h`),\nwhich compares the record with the private key file.\n\n## Where it is\n\n**Over REST**, `GET /api/v1/setup/checklist`. The route is the server's rather\nthan a domain's - the submission port, the backup destination and the\nadministrator's second factor belong to no domain - so a credential confined to\nnamed domains is refused it, and reading needs `view_configuration`. It is a\nread: nothing changes, and the only traffic is the DNS lookups. `?dns=0` skips\nthe lookups; the three record items then say which host to look up\n(`record_not_looked_up`) and are `cannot_tell`.\n\n**Over COM**, `Utilities.GetSetupChecklist(LookUpDns)`, the same document, for\nthe server administrator.\n\n**In the Control Deck**, the *Checklist* view, first in the navigation after the\ndashboard, with a card per item and *Check again*.\n\n**In the Control Panel**, the *Setup checklist* page - first under *Set up this\nserver*, the group after the dashboard, and called *External setup* up to 6.3.4 -\nand the dashboard's \"needs attention\" card carry the first-hour items ahead of\nthe twelve external prerequisites they already had, numbered in this order, the\nfirst item that needs action marked as the next step, and each opening the page\nthat does it. The Setup checklist page marks no next step while values could not\nbe read: the first item that needs action among those read need not be the first\nto do. The panel asks the server for the list without\nDNS lookups, so that its window is not held for the resolver's timeout, and\nlooks the SPF and DMARC records up itself off the window thread, as it already\ndid for DKIM; the server's DKIM item is left out there, because the panel's own\nDKIM item verifies the record against the key file, and so is its\n`service_account` item, because the panel's own reads the service manager and\nsays what the service runs as, and raises a service account password an earlier\nversion left in `hMailServer.INI`.",
         "\n\nOn the page, the summary's *Go to the next step* brings the marked item on\nscreen with the keyboard on its button, and an item that is done or not needed\nis one line - its number, its state, its title and its button - that a click\nopens to the rest; an item that needs action, or that cannot be told, is shown\nwhole. The dashboard's card lists the next step and the two after it, then one\nline that says how many more the checklist holds and opens it, under the\nconfiguration warnings of the *Server status* page - an IP range that relays for\nanybody, no host name, refused bounces, localhost banned - each with the page\nthat puts it right. No host name is also the checklist's host-name step: while\nthat step is open, the card lists the step alone and counts it once.\n\nThe Control Panel adds one item of its own to the first hour, after the account:\n**the host name**, `Settings.HostName`, the name the server announces in\nHELO/EHLO and every receiver compares with DNS. It is done when the name is a\nfull DNS name under a public suffix, such as `mail.example.com`, and needs action\nwhen it is empty (the server then announces its Windows computer name), a single\nlabel, an address, or a name under a suffix no public DNS answers for - `.local`,\n`.lan`, `.internal`, `.corp`, `.home.arpa`, `.test`, `.example`, `.invalid` and\nthe like. Whether the PTR record of the server's public address names it cannot\nbe told from the Control Panel, and the item says so. Its button opens the\n*Protocols* page, whose *SMTP* tab has the host name.\n\nThe Control Panel's *DNS records* page begins with the record that brings a\ndomain's mail here at all: an **MX** record at the domain, `10 <host name>`,\noffered once the host name is a name the Internet can look up, with the reminder\nthat the host name needs an address record pointing at the server's public\naddress. *Check* reads the domain's MX records through the Windows resolver and\nsays whether one names the host name, or which server they name instead - which\nis right when a filtering service takes the mail first.\n\n**On the Control Deck's dashboard**, the `service_account` item's findings that\nneed action are shown as information, with a way to the checklist, while the\nserver runs as LocalSystem or root.\n\n## The document\n\n```json\n{\"generated\": 1789000000, \"dns_looked_up\": true,\n \"done\": 3, \"action_needed\": 4, \"cannot_tell\": 0, \"not_needed\": 1,\n \"items\": [\n   {\"key\": \"domain\", \"state\": \"done\",\n    \"findings\": [{\"state\": \"done\", \"code\": \"domain_count\", \"args\": [\"1\", \"1\"],\n                  \"text\": \"Active domains: 1 of 1.\"}]},\n   ...]}\n```\n\nEvery finding carries a code and its arguments beside an English sentence, so\nthat the Deck and the Control Panel say the same thing in their own languages\nwhile a script switches on the code. The arguments are strings whatever they\ncount.\n\nA finding the server can put right in one step also carries **`fix`**: the REST\nrequest that takes the step, as `{\"method\", \"path\", \"body\"}`. Only\n`sign_in_plain` has one - `PUT /api/v1/ipranges/{id}` with\n`{\"require_tls_for_auth\": true}`, offered only when every listener it names can\nstart TLS - and the Deck draws it as a button, *Require TLS for sign-in from this\nrange*, which asks first and then sends exactly that. For `sign_in_no_tls` the\nDeck draws a button to its Ports view instead.\nThe route answers it as it answers any caller: `manage_settings` is needed, and\nthe Deck draws the button only for a sign-in that has it. A script may send it\ntoo. The Control Panel shows the item and opens its IP ranges page.\n\n```json\n{\"state\": \"action_needed\", \"code\": \"sign_in_plain\", \"args\": [\"Internet\", \"25, 110, 143, 587\"],\n \"text\": \"The IP range Internet takes a password without TLS on port 25, 110, 143, 587, ...\",\n \"fix\": {\"method\": \"PUT\", \"path\": \"/api/v1/ipranges/2\", \"body\": {\"require_tls_for_auth\": true}}}\n```\n\n| Code | Arguments | Sentence |\n|---|---|---|\n| `profile_none` | | No profile has been applied. A profile sets about thirty settings for th",
         "e kind of server this is - a small office, behind a filtering gateway, an outbound relay, a high-volume sender - and shows every change before it is made. |\n| `profile_in_service` | accounts | This server holds {0} accounts, so it is in service: a profile is offered to a new server, and any of the four can still be compared with this one. (not_needed) |\n| `profile_applied` | profile, date | The {0} profile was applied on {1}. |\n| `profile_kept` | profile, date | The {0} profile was applied on {1}; its way back was discarded and its settings kept. |\n| `profile_reverted` | profile, date | The {0} profile was applied and then reverted on {1}; the settings are the ones it replaced. (not_needed) |\n| `profile_declined` | date | The settings this version ships with were chosen on {0}, and no profile applied; any of the four can still be compared with this server. (not_needed) |\n| `profile_failed` | profile, date | The {0} profile could not be applied in full on {1}: some of its settings were not written. Put back what it wrote, or keep it, and apply it again. |\n| `domain_none` | | No domain has been added yet. |\n| `domain_none_active` | total | {0} domains, none of them active. |\n| `domain_count` | active, total | Active domains: {0} of {1}. |\n| `domain_name_reserved` | domain, directory | {0} has the name the server reserves for its own directory {1}, and the domain's mail is kept in the directory named after it: the two share it, which a backup, a restore and the store's clean-up do not expect. Rename the domain; its mailboxes move and the store's files stay. (action_needed) |\n| `account_none` | | No account has been added in any domain. |\n| `account_count` | count | Accounts: {0}. |\n| `submission_none` | | No port takes message submission, so mail clients cannot send through this server. |\n| `submission_plain` | port | Port {0} takes submission without TLS, so passwords and mail would cross the network in the clear. |\n| `submission_no_certificate` | port | Port {0} is set to TLS but has no certificate, so TLS cannot start. |\n| `submission_tls` | port, certificate | Port {0} offers TLS with the certificate {1}. |\n| `submission_self_signed` | port, certificate | Port {0} offers TLS with {1}, the self-signed certificate this installation made: mail clients warn about it until it is replaced with one a certificate authority issued, which ACME can fetch from Let's Encrypt. (action_needed) |\n| `sign_in_plain` | range, ports | The IP range {0} takes a password without TLS on port {1}, so a client there that signs in before STARTTLS sends it across the network in the clear. (action_needed, with `fix`) |\n| `sign_in_no_tls` | range, ports | The IP range {0} takes a password without TLS, and port {1} has no TLS a client could start: give those ports a certificate and STARTTLS first, then require TLS for sign-in from the range. (action_needed, no `fix`) |\n| `sign_in_tls_required` | range | The IP range {0} takes a password only over TLS. |\n| `sign_in_tls_ports` | range | Every port the IP range {0} may use starts TLS before it takes a password. |\n| `sign_in_local_only` | | Only this computer may connect, so no password crosses a network. (not_needed) |\n| `delivery_port_auth_on` | port | Port {0} still accepts AUTH: it is where other servers deliver mail, and a password tried there is a guess. Set its SMTP authentication to Honeypot or Off, and sign mail clients in on 587. (action_needed, no `fix`) |\n| `delivery_port_auth_honeypot` | port | Port {0} takes no password: EHLO offers AUTH there as bait, and an address that sends one is refused and banned. |\n| `delivery_port_auth_off` | port | Port {0} takes no password: EHLO does not offer AUTH there, and an address that sends one anyway is refused and banned. |\n| `delivery_port_auth_no_ban` | port | Port {0} takes no password, but AutoBanMinutes is 0, so an address that sends AUTH there is refused and not banned. (done) |\n| `delivery_port_auth_disabled` | port | Port {0} does not offer AUTH: Disa",
         "bleAUTHList names it, and an AUTH sent there is refused without a ban. (done) |\n| `delivery_port_none` | | No SMTP listener is on port 25, so no delivery port takes a password. (not_needed) |\n| `no_active_domain` | | No active domain, so there is nothing to publish yet. |\n| `record_published` | domain, kind, host | {0}: the {1} record at {2} is published. |\n| `record_missing` | domain, kind, host | {0}: no {1} record at {2}. |\n| `record_not_looked_up` | domain, kind, host | {0}: the {1} record at {2} was not looked up. |\n| `lookup_failed` | domain, kind, host | {0}: the lookup for {2} failed, which says nothing about the {1} record. |\n| `domains_not_looked_up` | count | {0} more active domains were not looked up. |\n| `dkim_off` | domain | {0}: signing is off, so receivers cannot verify the mail as this domain's. |\n| `dkim_no_selector` | domain | {0}: signing is on but no selector is set. |\n| `dkim_key_missing` | domain, file | {0}: signing is on but the key file {1} was not found. |\n| `backup_none` | | No backup destination is set. |\n| `backup_folder_missing` | folder | The backup destination {0} does not exist on the server. |\n| `backup_set` | folder | Backups go to {0}. |\n| `second_factor_on` | | The administrator's password is backed by a one-time code. |\n| `second_factor_off` | | The administrator signs in with a password alone. |\n| `stored_secrets_off` | | ProtectStoredSecretsWithDPAPI is 0, so an account's second factor and the [Settings] secrets are stored as written, and no stored-secrets key is needed. |\n| `stored_secrets_key_none` | verb | No stored-secrets key has been made, so an account's second factor and the [Settings] secrets are stored as written. Make one on the server with {0}, then export it. |\n| `stored_secrets_key_not_held` | key ids, verb | The database's second factors and [Settings] secrets are sealed under the stored-secrets key {0}, which this server does not hold, so they do not open here. Import it on the server from one that holds it: {1}. |\n| `stored_secrets_key_not_exported` | key id, verb | The stored-secrets key {0} has never been exported. Export it on the server with {1} and keep the file with the database backups: a database restored without it opens no second factor and no [Settings] secret. |\n| `stored_secrets_key_exported` | key id, date | The stored-secrets key {0} was exported on {1}; keep that file with the database backups. |\n| `stored_secrets_key_unreadable` | | The database's record of its stored-secrets key could not be read, so whether this server holds the database's key cannot be told. |\n| `service_account_local_system` | | The service runs as LocalSystem, the most privileged account on the machine, so a flaw reachable through SMTP, IMAP or POP3 would have full control of it. The virtual account a new installation uses contains that; running the installer again, or the Control Panel, moves the service to it. (Windows) |\n| `service_account_root` | | The server runs as root, so a flaw reachable through SMTP, IMAP or POP3 would have full control of the machine. The packages run it as a user of its own. (Linux) |\n| `service_account_own` | account | The service runs as {0}. (done) |\n| `autoban_firewall_not_permitted` | account | AutoBanFirewall is on, but {0} cannot write Windows Defender Firewall rules: banned addresses are refused by the server and not blocked in the firewall. Run the service as LocalSystem, or reach a firewall through AutoBanCommand. (Windows, action needed) |\n| `update_apply_by_administrator` | account | {0} cannot run an installer, so updates are checked, downloaded and verified here and installed by an administrator with Install update in the Control Panel on this server. (Windows, while the update check is on; not_needed - it says how updates are installed; the sentence up to 6.3.5 ended \"and an administrator installs them\") |\n\nThe three record items look the first twenty active domains up; the rest are\ncounted in `domains_not_looked_up`, and the Deck's DNS r",
         "ecords view checks any\none of them on request. Each missing record costs the resolver's timeout, which\nis why a server hosting hundreds of domains - a server past its first hour - is\nnot asked to wait for all of them.\n\n### Domain names the server keeps for itself\n\nA domain's mail is kept in the directory named after it directly under the data\ndirectory, and the server keeps its own stores in the same place: `ACME`, `Blobs`,\n`Certificates`, `Confidential`, `DKIM`, `Discovery`, `Files`, `Lists`, `Protected`,\n`Quarantine`, `Recoverable`, `Sieve` and `Updates`, and `temp`, `events` and\n`database`, which the Linux packages' `hMailServer.ini` puts there. A domain whose\nname is one of these - a single label, in any case - or the name of any directory\n`hMailServer.ini` places directly under the data directory, would share the store's\ndirectory. A backup leaves out the mail of a domain named as a store it does not\ncarry (`Recoverable`, `Discovery`, `Confidential`, `Protected`, and from 6.3.6\n`Updates`; [Backup.md](Backup.md)),\nin any case, and a restore does not put it back. Where the two are one directory -\nalways on Windows, whose file system does not tell `protected` from `Protected`, and\non Linux, where a domain's directory is named in lower case, for `temp`, `events` and\n`database` -\nthe clean-up of the recoverable, confidential and protected stores removes a\ndirectory named by a number whose account is gone, which a mailbox named by a number\nis, and a rename carried the whole directory, the store's files with it, into the\nrenamed domain.\n\nSince 6.3.4 such a name is refused for a new domain, and for a rename, over COM\n(`Domain.Save`), the REST API (`POST /api/v1/domains`, `PUT /api/v1/domains/{domain}`)\nand so the Deck, with the sentence *The name ... is reserved*. The Control Panel\nrefused every name without a dot already. A domain that already has one - made by\nan earlier version over COM or REST, or put back by a restore - is still saved, so\nthat it can be managed; it is `domain_name_reserved` here and HM7597 in the error\nlog each time the servers start. Rename it: the rename moves each of its accounts'\nmailboxes and leaves the rest of the directory, the store's files, where it is.\nThe list is `PreSaveLimitationsCheck::IsReservedDomainName`; the regression tests\nare `test/RegressionTests/Infrastructure/Persistence/DomainNameValidation.cs` (COM:\nrefused, renamed to, and a domain given such a name in the database reported and\nrenamed away with the store's files left in place) and\n`API/RestApiDomains.cs`'s `TheNameOfAStoreDirectoryIsRefused`.\n\n## Where the code is\n\n`Common/Util/SetupChecklist.cpp` builds the document; `RestApiSetupChecklist.cpp`\nis the route; `InterfaceUtilities::GetSetupChecklist` the COM method; the Deck's\nview is `renderChecklist` in `installation/WebAdmin/index.html`; the Control\nPanel's items are `ExternalSetupChecks.FirstHourItems`. The regression fixture is\n`test/RegressionTests/API/RestApiSetupChecklist.cs`: the thirteen items in order,\nthe records looked up in the suite's own zone for two domains, the skipped lookup\nsaying so, the submission port judged with a certificate that exists and with the\nself-signed one a new installation makes, the passwords item's fix sent as the\nfinding gives it and withheld while a listener has no TLS, port 25 judged On,\nHoneypot, Off and with the ban minutes at 0, the backup destination\njudged on disk, and the account the server runs as judged from its own token and,\nbeside the server, against what `sc.exe qc` reports. The Deck's button is\n`build/deck-script-test.js`'s.\n",
         nullptr
      };

